td-txt conformance corpus
=========================

The cases the td-txt conformance gate runs (see ../tests/conformance.rs). Three
provenances live here, and the harness (../src/lib.rs) reads all three into one
case type — argv, input files, stdin, expected stdout/stderr/status.

Why not one upstream corpus, the way td-sh vendors Oils' spec files: grep and
sed have no single self-contained suite with recorded goldens. What they have is
better in one way and worse in another — their upstream tests are DATA files
whose expected result is already written down (so no oracle binary is needed in
an offline sandbox), but that data covers the REGEX ENGINE and whole SCRIPTS,
not the programs' option surface. Hence the split below.


Vendored from GNU grep, UNMODIFIED
----------------------------------
  gnu-grep/bre.tests
  gnu-grep/ere.tests
  gnu-grep/spencer1.tests
  gnu-grep/khadafy.regexp
  gnu-grep/khadafy.lines

  Upstream:  https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/grep/grep-3.11.tar.xz
  SHA-256:   1db2aedde89d0dea42b16d9528f894c8d15dae4e190b59aecc78f5a951276eab
  Path:      tests/<file>  (copied byte-for-byte)
  License:   GPL-3.0-or-later (GNU grep). Copyright the Free Software Foundation.

  Selection rule (reproducible from the pin): the DATA files of grep's own
  regression suite — the three Spencer-format `.tests` files plus the Khadafy
  pair. Everything else under tests/ is a shell script driven by gnulib's
  init.sh, which needs a shell, a built grep tree and `$srcdir`; those are the
  scripts this corpus deliberately does not import.

  Row grammar (`<status>@<pattern>@<input>`) and the invocation come from
  upstream's own awk drivers at that pin. bre.awk emits

      status=$(echo '<input>' | { grep -e '<pattern>' >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?; })

  and ere.awk/spencer1.awk the same with `-E`. So this corpus runs
  `grep [-E] -e <pattern>` with `<input>` (plus a newline, as `echo` writes it)
  on stdin and asserts ONLY the exit status — upstream discards the output. A
  row with a FOURTH field is upstream's own "expected non-conformance" marker
  (`TO CORRECT`, `NO ALTERNATION`): its awk driver does not assert those rows,
  and neither does the reader here.

  The Khadafy case is upstream's `khadafy` test: one ERE read with `-f` over 32
  spellings, all of which must come back unchanged.


Vendored from GNU sed, UNMODIFIED
---------------------------------
  gnu-sed/<name>.sed  +  <name>.inp  +  <name>.good   (35 triples)

  Upstream:  https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/sed/sed-4.2.2.tar.gz
  SHA-256:   fea0a94d4b605894f3e2d5572e3f96e4413bcad3a085aae7367c2cf07908b2ff
  Path:      testsuite/<file>  (copied byte-for-byte)
  License:   GPL-3.0-or-later (GNU sed). Copyright the Free Software Foundation.

  Selection rule (reproducible from the pin): exactly the test names listed in
  the FIRST, uniform recipe of upstream's testsuite/Makefile.tests —

      enable sep inclib 8bit 8to7 newjis xabcx dollar noeol bkslashes numsub
      head madding mac-mf empty xbxcx xbxcx3 recall recall2 xemacs appquit
      fasts uniq manis linecnt khadafy allsub flipcase space modulo y-bracket
      y-newline insert brackets amp-escape

  — the ones upstream runs as plain

      LC_ALL=C sed -f <name>.sed < <name>.inp   |cmp -   <name>.good

  with no other flag or environment. That is why this pin is 4.2.2 rather than
  the current 4.9: 4.9 converted most of these into shell scripts, and the
  triples are the part that needs no shell. Names upstream runs differently
  (`0range` needs `-s`, `badenc`/`utf8-*` need a UTF-8 locale, `stdin` pipes two
  seds) are deliberately NOT here: importing them would mean encoding an
  invocation this corpus cannot read off the data.

  Why 4.2.2's goldens are still the right target: these cases exercise POSIX sed
  behavior that did not change between 4.2.2 and 4.9. Where td-txt is scored
  against NEWER sed behavior, that lives in sed-cli.test.txt below, whose
  goldens come from 4.9.


td-txt-authored case files
--------------------------
  grep-cli.test.txt   the grep option surface, prefixes, exit statuses, refusals
  sed-cli.test.txt    the sed command language, options, in-place editing, refusals
  divergence.test.txt where td-txt deliberately differs from GNU

  grep-cli.test.txt and sed-cli.test.txt each end with a block derived from td's
  OWN usage: every grep/sed invocation shape the image's generated /etc scripts
  and the recipes' build steps actually make, one case per shape, each paired
  with the negative that shows the shape DISCRIMINATES. That block is what makes
  the corpus a statement about the image rather than about the option list: the
  /bin/grep those scripts call is this binary. Re-derive the shapes by scanning
  the generated scripts (`recipes`' `script_applets_are_covered` derives applet
  usage from the same text) and the recipe step strings.

  grep-cli.test.txt ends with one further block, the `rootcheck td-volume` cases.
  Those score a shape that is not merely USED by the image but was MOVED to it:
  /etc/rootcheck decided whether the persistent volume was mounted read-only with
  a busybox `awk` field test, and that predicate is now an ERE. `ro` has to match
  as a whole comma-delimited option, so the block is mostly negatives —
  `errors=remount-ro`, `rootcontext=`, `rofoo` and a `subvol=/@ro` value all carry
  the letters without being the option. `recipes` pins the same pattern text in
  `rootcheck_pins_the_td_volume_ere`, which is the other half of the binding: that
  end proves the script still spells what this end scores.

  `-w` asks TWO questions, and GNU answers them in two different places. Whether a
  line MATCHES is its dfa's answer over the pattern WRAPPED in word boundaries,
  `\(^\|[^[:alnum:]_]\)\(PAT\)\([^[:alnum:]_]\|$\)` (grep/src/dfasearch.c:301-325);
  on a hit with no backreference that is the whole answer (dfasearch.c:479), so the
  question is "is there ANY word-bounded span". Which span to PRINT is a different
  reading: the retry loop at dfasearch.c:528-567, which shrinks a match that is not
  word-bounded at the same start and otherwise advances the start by one. The two
  DISAGREE, and a case pins each side of it: `grep -o -w '..\?'` on `a b c` prints
  `a` and `c` and passes the word-bounded `b` that selection finds. Two details
  carry most of that — the shrink window is cut at `s + len - from`, an offset
  measured from the SEARCH START but applied from the LINE start, so under `-o` it
  closes over the match as the line is consumed; and an EMPTY shrink does not count
  where an empty match the search itself reports does. The second is why
  `grep -w '\.*'` SELECTS `.a` while `grep -o -w '\.*'` prints nothing for it.

  Three further blocks come from DIFFERENTIAL FUZZING against grep 3.11 rather than
  from reading the option list — `grep -w` over a pattern that can match a shorter
  span, BRE anchor-vs-literal (`^^`), and POSIX leftmost-longest across a bounded
  repeat. All three were wrong ANSWERS, not refusals, and none of the shapes is
  exotic (`-w` with a `*`/`?` pattern; a doubled `^`; a `\{n,m\}` after a `*`). The
  hand-picked cases had missed all three — which is the point. How to run another
  campaign:

    - generate random patterns from a BRE/ERE grammar and random short inputs,
      run `grep [-E] <flags> -e PAT file` under both binaries with LC_ALL=C, and
      compare status + stdout + stderr;
    - BUCKET the divergences before reading them. Raw counts are dominated by
      noise: a deliberate refusal (`-b`) and GNU's own `warning:` notices --
      though the STRAY-BACKSLASH one is modelled now and is no longer on that
      list, so bucketing every `warning:` away would hide a real divergence in
      it. What remains unmodelled is the at-start-of-expression family
      (`* ? + {...}`), which shares the same gate and would be a small
      increment on top of it. The
      quantifier-on-a-zero-width-assertion corner (`\<*`, `\b?`) used to belong on
      that list -- excluding it took one run from ~450 divergences to 3 -- and no
      longer does: it is modelled now, in all four dialects, and what remains is
      recorded below. Bucketing it away is how one campaign hands the next a
      corner nobody has read; the model that replaced it was got WRONG twice
      first, both times by probing it through a single syntactic frame;
    - the bucket that matters is "both accepted the pattern and the ANSWERS
      differ". Everything else is a refusal-text question.

  The fuzzer is deliberately NOT committed: it needs a GNU oracle binary, which
  is not a declared input of any recipe, so it could not run in the offline gate.
  Its findings are committed instead — as cases with goldens captured from the
  oracle, which is what the gate can actually check.

  DRIFT campaigns are the other uncommitted tool, and the same reasoning applies:
  a drift run mutates one decision in a copy of the crate and re-runs the suite,
  asserting the mutation applied and expecting RED. A green axis is a coverage
  hole rather than a pass. Because it deliberately runs BROKEN builds, it needs
  three guards that an ordinary test run does not, and every one of them was
  learned by losing a filesystem to it:

    - CAP THE FILE SIZE. `StdioBuf::flush` and `direct` are `while` loops that
      end only by advancing through the bytes, and the unit tests write to real
      files, so a mutation that stops the advance writes until the disk is gone.
      That is not hypothetical: it filled a 1.9 TB root twice, ~700 GB in two
      abandoned files. Launch each run through `bash -c 'ulimit -f N; exec cargo
      test …'`. The cap is the KERNEL's, needs no `unsafe`, and turns a runaway
      into an immediate SIGXFSZ (signal 25) — verified by mutating the drain out
      of `flush` and watching the write die at exactly the cap.
    - REDIRECT `TMPDIR` into the run's own sandbox. The tests reach for
      `std::env::temp_dir()`, so by default their scratch lands in a shared
      `/tmp` where it is nobody's and outlives the run; pointed at the sandbox it
      is removed with it.
    - KILL THE PROCESS GROUP on timeout, via `start_new_session=True` and
      `killpg`. Killing the `cargo` child alone leaves the test BINARY running,
      orphaned and still writing — which is why the abandoned files belonged to
      PIDs that no longer existed.

  A disk-space floor checked between axes is worth having on top: an axis that
  cannot finish is better than one that fills the last of the disk for everyone
  else on the machine.

  What the fuzzer did NOT reach is worth knowing too. Its grammar never emits an
  EMPTY pattern, and an empty pattern short-circuited the regex engine entirely,
  so the whole empty-pattern short cut was outside the search space — and the
  MATCHING one of the two (since removed, see `match_at`) had the same `-w`
  bug in its own form. A reviewer found it by reading. A generator
  only covers the shapes its grammar can produce, so the block below it
  (`-w` over an empty pattern) exists because someone looked at the code instead.

  Its inputs were also all SHORT, which hid a whole class: what a change costs on
  a long line. Making the matcher explore every end for leftmost-longest turned
  `grep '.*=.*=.*'` on a 641-byte line into `too complex` (exit 2), and 798 green
  cases said nothing, because not one of them had a line that long. Fuzz LENGTH,
  not just shape, and diff against the PREVIOUS td-txt as well as against GNU: the
  question "did something that used to answer stop answering" is invisible when
  the oracle is the only comparison. The long-line block below is that regression
  test.

  The same campaign against SED, whose corpus had had none, found six — and its
  noise bucket was one flag rather than a regex corner. `--posix` dominated every
  early run, because GNU's `--posix` disables GNU's own extensions and refuses
  thirteen constructs td-txt accepts (`first~step`, a leading `+N`/`~N`,
  `addr,+N`, `addr,~N`, `0,/re/`, `s///I`, `s///M`, `Q`, `T`, `z`, `F`,
  one-liner `a text`, `q5`) while turning
  `\U\L\u\l\E` and BRE `\+ \? \| \w` into literals. When this campaign ran,
  td-txt implemented `--posix` for exactly ONE of those (`N` at end of input) and
  silently ignored the rest, so every script that mixed the flag with an extension
  read as a divergence. All three sides of that are now implemented -- regex,
  replacement and the command refusals -- so the `--posix` entry below records
  what is LEFT rather than what is missing.
  Excluding the flag took 750 cases from 20 divergences to 1. Make it the first
  thing you bucket, and note that the gap it hides is itself a finding: an option
  accepted and mostly ignored is the "silent no-op" ../src/sed.rs's header says
  this program never has. Fixing that is its own landing.

  Sed rewards fuzzing the SEPARATOR, not just the script. Four of the six bugs
  needed `-z` or an input with no final newline to show at all: `l` wrapped with a
  newline where GNU wraps with the record separator, `M` anchored at a newline
  under `-z` where GNU anchors at the NUL, and `q` paid a separator debt that
  reaching end of input must leave unpaid. Generate unterminated inputs and
  NUL-separated ones as a matter of course; a corpus of newline-terminated lines
  cannot see any of it.

  Two refinements of that, each of which cost a round of review to learn. First,
  fuzz the commands that put a separator INSIDE the pattern space. `M` is POSIX
  REG_NEWLINE relative to the record separator, and the first pass concluded that
  its anchor half must go inert under `-z` because no record contains its own
  separator -- true of a record, false of a pattern space, because `N` joins two
  records WITH one. Every `-z`+`M` case in the first pass omitted `N`, so 232 green
  cases agreed with a wrong rule. Second, fuzz more than one output stream and more
  than one input file, because sed's state is a mix of per-run and per-file and a
  single-file, single-stream generator cannot tell which is which.

  What `M` actually is, since two rounds of this branch got it wrong: GNU
  implements it as TWO mechanisms, and a pattern can tell them apart.

  - libc's REG_NEWLINE, bound to `\n` whatever the separator is. `^`/`$` gain the
    embedded newlines and `.` / a non-matching bracket list lose them -- but
    NOTHING else does, so `\W` and an explicit `[\n]` still match one.
  - Confinement to the segments between separators, which GNU needs only when the
    separator is not a newline, libc having no way to express it. There the anchors
    move to that byte and nothing consumes it: not `.`, not `\W`, not `\s`, not a
    named `\x00`, and no backreference spans one.

  Both of those are properties of the CALL, not of the pattern -- which is the third
  thing this branch got wrong about `M`, and the missing fuzz axis was the COMMAND
  FORM. GNU confines a SUBSTITUTION to the segment it is working in; an ADDRESS
  matches the whole pattern space. So `sed -z -n -e N -e '/a.b/Mp'` matches across
  the NUL while `s/a.b/X/M` on the same space does not, and the same split holds for
  `\W`, a named `\x00` and `[^x]`. The BUFFER ANCHORS split the same way, and not as
  you would guess from any other regex flavour: to an address ``\` ``/`\'` are
  indistinguishable from `^`/`$` and move to the record separator with them, while a
  substitution moves them only for a SEGMENT. So with a newline separator `/\`b/M`
  matches the pattern space `a\nb` and `s/\`b/X/M` finds nothing in it, and
  `s/\`a/X/Mg` over `a\na` rewrites ONE `a` where `s/^a/X/Mg` rewrites both. Under
  `-z` the segment moves them for both callers. The REG_NEWLINE rules above (`.` and
  a non-matching list losing `\n`) apply to both callers either way.

  One more thing splits the two paths, and no rule about `M` predicts it: a
  BACKREFERENCE anywhere in the pattern confines an ADDRESS as well. `sed -z -n -e
  'N;N' -e '/\(.\)\1/Mp'` over `a\0\0b\0` finds nothing, while `/../Mp` and
  `/\(a\)\x00/Mp` over the same pattern space both match -- and `/\(a\)\x00\1*/Mp`
  does not, though `\1*` need consume nothing, so it is the PRESENCE of a backref and
  not its own match that does it. That is glibc's backref matcher, not something sed
  documents; td-txt reproduces it with one compile-time flag rather than recording a
  divergence, because the corpus's oracle is what GNU 4.9 does here. A match wholly
  inside one segment is unaffected, and with a newline separator (no segment) a
  doubled NUL is an ordinary pair to a backref.

  So under `-z` a newline inside a record stays ordinary to `\W` while the NUL is
  invisible to everything a substitution can consume. Probing only `.` and `[^...]`
  cannot distinguish the two mechanisms, because those are the one place both apply
  -- probe `\W` against BOTH separators, which is the single cheapest
  discriminator. And confinement has to hold for the greedy-repeat fast path too,
  which consumes bytes without taking the ordinary single-byte step: `s/a.b/X/M`
  refused a NUL that `s/.*/X/M` swallowed.

  One ordering rule that only `r`/`R` against the quit commands can expose: `N` at
  END of input prints the pattern space BEFORE flushing the append queue, the
  reverse of every other path, `n` at end of input included. Two commands that both
  write, in the same cycle, is the shape to generate -- a queue and a print each
  look right alone.

  The inventory of per-run versus per-file state, which the `-s` bugs came out of:

  - Per RUN, surviving every operand: the output streams and their owed
    separators. The debt is per output TARGET (GNU keeps one per filename), so `w
    /dev/stdout` is a different stream from auto-print even though both reach the
    same fd, a `w` file has its own, and `/dev/stderr` is an ordinary target with
    raw bytes and no forced newline. `q` settles the auto-print stream alone.
  - Per FILE under `-s`/`-i`: line numbers, range state, the hold space and every
    REWINDABLE `R` read position. GNU clears the hold's TEXT but keeps its
    terminator flag, which shows only after an unterminated file. And it restarts an
    `R` source with `rewind(3)`, which fails silently on a stream that cannot seek:
    a pipe or fifo keeps its place across the boundary while a regular file starts
    over. GNU also aliases the literal `/dev/stdin` to its own stdin stream, which
    is not among the streams it rewinds however seekable that stream is -- so with
    stdin redirected FROM A REGULAR FILE, `R /dev/stdin` does not rewind while `R
    /proc/self/fd/0` and `R /dev/fd/0`, naming the same file, do. That last pair
    cannot go in the corpus: which of the two answers is right depends on whether
    the harness hands the case a pipe or a file.

    The same question decides `#n`, and GNU's form of it for a FILE script is
    `prog.file && !prog.base && 2 == ftell(prog.file)` (the `prog.cur == 2 +
    prog.base` spelling is the STRING-script branch): the script stream must SEEK
    -- `ftell` is -1 on a pipe, so a `-f`
    script arriving through one never carries the rule however it starts -- and it
    must have STARTED at its own beginning, so a descriptor handed over already
    part-read does not carry it either (`sed -f -` on stdin pre-positioned past
    three bytes auto-prints in GNU). Only the FIRST script part is asked, a later
    `-f` off a pipe cannot revoke it, and an `-e` or literal part always carries.
    Half of this is caseable and half is not: the harness gives every case a piped
    stdin, so `-f -` reaches the non-seekable side and a `## file` reaches the
    other, but it can hand a case neither a fifo nor a pre-positioned descriptor.
    The pre-positioned half is an integration test in tests/conformance.rs, which
    CAN seek a `File` and pass it as stdin. The fifo half has NO behavioural test:
    std cannot make a fifo and the gate runs no third-party program to make one.
    What stands in its place is a source assertion in the same file, and it guards
    the narrower property of never REOPENING fd 0 rather than the answer a fifo
    gets -- the two are not the same claim, and only the first is tested. The
    answer itself was measured by hand: writer gone, GNU and td-txt both
    auto-print. td-txt asks `stat` rather than reopening: reopening fd 0
    HANGS when it is a fifo whose writer has gone, a worse failure than the wrong
    answer it was meant to fix. What `stat` buys is that it does not join that
    OPEN handshake -- not that it can never block, since an attribute lookup on a
    stalled network or FUSE mount can. `stat` is a PROXY for `ftell`, not an
    equal. It holds wherever `st_size` equals what is readable from offset 0 and
    the thing is a regular file, and parts company BOTH ways. It DECLINES what GNU
    takes for a BLOCK device (seekable, so `ftell` answers), a virtual file sized
    0 (procfs) or a fixed 4096 (sysfs) against a shorter read, and a file RESIZED
    between the read and the stat -- grown, when the read missed the new bytes (a
    read that caught them agrees again), or shrunk to any size but the one just
    read. Those auto-print here, as an absent or unreadable /proc does. Every one
    of them is the `-f -` arm ONLY: a NAMED `-f` asks the stream itself to seek,
    which is the real question, so `-f /proc/PID/comm` on a `#n` comm AGREES with
    GNU (both suppress) where `-f -` redirected from that same file diverges (GNU
    suppresses, td-txt auto-prints). Measured both ways -- the proxy governs the
    one arm that cannot ask. A
    CHARACTER device is NOT among them, though an earlier draft of this entry said
    it was: measured through GNU's own sequence -- `fopen`, `fread` of two bytes,
    `ftell` -- not one readable here reaches 2. `/dev/zero`, `/dev/urandom` and
    `/dev/full` answer -1, glibc having gone negative subtracting the bytes it
    read ahead and did not hand over; and `/dev/null` answers 0, having read
    nothing. `/dev/ptmx` is the one that CANNOT take that sequence -- its `fread`
    never returns -- so what is measured there is a raw `lseek`, which is ESPIPE;
    an earlier draft listed it with the other four as though it had. So GNU
    declines them too --
    agreement, not divergence. (A draft of this said they "report offset 0", which
    is what raw `lseek` reports and not what `ftell` does. The conclusion held and
    the mechanism was wrong, which is twice on this branch that measuring the
    near-neighbour call has answered a question about a different one.) The
    block-device half is reasoning and not measurement: nothing here can open one.
    It can also wrongly ACCEPT, which the decline cases might suggest it cannot,
    but in two narrow shapes and NEITHER of them a static configuration. `S_IFREG`
    is not the same question as seekable, so a filesystem serving a regular file
    that refuses `lseek` (FUSE can) is taken here and declined there. And a file
    truncated to exactly the bytes just read, in the window BETWEEN the read and
    the stat, looks like a whole file read from 0 -- a pre-positioned descriptor
    cannot do it alone, because what is readable from offset k is `size - k`,
    which equals `size` only at k = 0. So the accepting direction needs a
    concurrent writer where the declining one does not. Exactness would need
    `lseek` on fd 0, which safe `std` cannot reach.
  - Every number a COMMAND or ADDRESS takes is read by one rule and it WRAPS,
    because GNU reads them all with `in_integer` accumulating into a counter it
    lets overflow. (Not every number in a script: a regex interval bound like
    `a\{N\}` belongs to the regex engine, does not wrap, and refuses identically
    in both.) So a
    number past 2^64 is its remainder, not a clamp: `2^64+1` is line 1, and
    `2^64` is line 0 and takes whatever complaint a bare 0 takes there. It is one
    rule over eight positions -- the line address, both halves of `first~step`,
    the `+N` and `~N` of a second address, `q`'s exit code, `l`'s width and
    `s///N`'s occurrence -- and all eight agree with the oracle, though two of
    them needed a second fix apiece BELOW to get there, since how a number is
    read and what it then MEANS are different questions. td-txt saturated
    until this landing, which was a silent WRONG ANSWER: the script ran, matched
    nothing and said nothing. The `l` width is the one that needs a fixture wider
    than the wrap to see, since a saturated width and a wrapped one both print a
    short line unfolded.
    What a number then MEANS is a separate question from how it is read, and
    `q`/`Q`'s exit code is where the two were confused: only the low 8 bits
    survive `exit`, so the code must be MASKED, not narrowed. td-txt narrowed
    with `i32::try_from(..).unwrap_or(0)`, so every code past `i32::MAX` became
    0 -- a different STATUS rather than a clamped one, and the SUCCESS status
    for a script asking to fail. `q2147483692` is 44 in GNU and was 0 here.
    The boundary is `i32::MAX` and not the wrap, which is why the number work
    above does not cover it, and why a probe has to use a remainder that is not
    0: `q4294967296` agrees under both answers.
    Masking alone is still wrong at ONE point, though, and it took a reviewer to
    find it: GNU holds the code in an `int` whose -1 means "no code was given",
    so a code whose 32-BIT truncation is -1 cannot be told from a bare `q` and
    falls back to the normal exit status. `q4294967295`, `q8589934591` and
    `q(2^64-1)` are all 0 in GNU, and a plain mask makes them 255 -- which also
    swallows a real failure, since `sed -n q4294967295 nosuch f` is 2 there. The
    first fix here masked and so broke those three, which had AGREED before it;
    the rule is: truncate to 32 bits, and if that is -1 behave as a bare `q`,
    else exit on the low 8. `q4294967294` is 254 and `q4294967039` is 255, so
    the sentinel is exactly one value in each 2^32 and nothing near it.
    `l`'s WIDTH lives in that same `int` and so truncates the same way, with two
    more rules on top -- and the SENTINEL is one of them, which a first fix here
    missed by reading every negative alike. A truncation of exactly -1 means "no
    width argument", so `l4294967295` folds where a bare `l` folds (at `-l N`, or
    70); any OTHER non-positive value means never wrap. So `l4294967306` folds at
    10, `l4294967295` folds at 70, and `l2147483658` folds nowhere. td-txt
    narrowed with `usize::try_from`, which never fails on 64-bit, so the whole
    `u64` arrived and nothing folded. Two of the eight
    positions therefore needed their CONSUMER fixed as well as the reader, and
    the shipped `l` case for the wrap (`18446744073709551636`, which is 20)
    could not see it: its value is under 2^32, so it agrees either way.
    That sentinel selects between TWO things, and the FALLBACK WIDTH is the other
    one -- refused here until `-l` landed, so "no width argument" could only ever
    mean 70 and the case pinning it could not tell the rule from the constant.
    `-l 10 l4294967295` folds at 10 and `-l 0 l4294967295` folds nowhere, which
    is what makes it a fallback rather than a default. The option is read when
    `l` RUNS, not when the script compiles, so `sed -e l -l 12` folds at 12
    though the option came after the part; and `--posix` withdraws the script's
    width argument without touching it.
    That fallback has TWO inputs, not one, which a reviewer found by reading
    GNU's `main` rather than its manual: `COLS` seeds it before any option is
    scanned, so `-l` overrides `COLS` and `COLS=12 sed -n l4294967295` folds at
    11. It is not `-l` under another name. GNU takes `COLS - 1` -- one column
    kept back so a tty does not wrap the line itself -- and only when the value
    exceeds 1, so `COLS=2` folds at 1 while `COLS=1`, `COLS=0` and `COLS=abc`
    leave 70 standing. The comparison happens AFTER the `int` lands in the
    unsigned counter both widths live in, which is the part no reading of the
    manual gives: `COLS=-1` folds NOWHERE rather than leaving the default, and
    `COLS=-4294967286` folds at 9, its truncation being a positive 10. The name
    is `COLS` and not `COLUMNS`, which has its own case.
    Both inputs are read by a THIRD reader, GNU's `atoi`, and it disagrees with
    the script's numbers in both directions a number can be read wrong. It cannot
    fail -- leading whitespace, an optional sign, decimal digits, and the rest
    of the word ignored -- so `-l abc` is a silent width of 0, which is "never
    fold"; and it SATURATES where `in_integer` wraps, so `-l 18446744073709551626`
    never folds while `l18446744073709551626` folds at 10. Same digits, same
    program, opposite overflow rules, and the pair ships as two cases side by
    side. The truncation is shared: `-l 4294967306` folds at 10 like the command
    does, and `-l -4294967286` folds at 10 as well -- a NEGATIVE argument whose
    low 32 bits are positive, which is the case that would survive reading the
    sign as "never fold". There is no -1 sentinel on this side, `-l` being what
    the other one defers to: `-l 4294967295` never folds where `l4294967295`
    falls back.
    The ARITHMETIC on a number wraps too, not just the parse. `addr,+N` resolves
    its end as `start + N` in that same counter, so `1,+(2^64-1)` ends at line 0.
    Two rules follow, and td-txt had neither. The addition wraps rather than
    saturating -- it saturated, so the end was `u64::MAX` and the range ran to
    the end of input where GNU selects two lines. And on the START line the range
    ends only when N is 0, NOT whenever `line >= start + N`: those agree for
    every N that does not overflow and part company for one that does, where an
    end behind the start must still give the range its second line
    (`3,+(2^64-2)` is lines 3 and 4). The full rule, measured over every start
    1-4 against every end 0-7: `end == start` selects the start line alone,
    `end > start` runs `start..end`, and `end < start` selects exactly two.
    `addr,~N` is deliberately NOT part of this: its end can only overflow when N
    is past the start, and then `start % N` is `start`, so the end is `N` itself
    -- unreachable by any input under saturating and wrapping alike. It is left
    saturating because changing it would be a rule no case could red, which a
    mutation run confirmed by reddening nothing.
  - `R` cursors are keyed by NAME, not per command: two `R f` commands share one
    cursor and advance it twice in a cycle, while an `r f` beside them is unrelated.
  - And `w /dev/stdout` still means the real standard output under `-i`, where the
    auto-print stream is the replacement buffer.

  WHEN those targets are opened is a separate question from how many there are, and
  the answer is that COMPILING the script opens them: GNU opens a `w` target while
  it parses the command that names it, not on first write. So the file exists and
  has been truncated even though no write ever reaches it -- `sed -n '2w out'` over
  one line leaves an empty `out` -- and a target that will not open is exit 4 from a
  command no cycle runs, reported bare because the script was well formed and the
  filesystem refused. The `s///w` FLAG goes the same way and is the sharper case,
  because the open happens AT THE FLAG -- before the pattern is compiled and before
  the replacement's backreferences are checked, both of which GNU does at the end of
  the `s`. So an unopenable target beats a bad regex in the same command (`s/\(/b/w
  /nodir/o` is exit 4, not `Unmatched (`), and an openable one is created before that
  regex is refused. It is also what a parser-shape generator keeps rediscovering: a
  `w` filename runs to end of line, so any generator that puts a `/` after one hands
  sed a path whose directory does not exist. `r` is the one that is opened when it
  RUNS, and a source that never opens is not an error at all.

  `R` is neither, and this paragraph had it filed with `r` until a sweep disproved
  that. GNU opens an `R` source at COMPILE time like a `w` target (compile.c:1217)
  and unlike one passes `fail = false`, so a source that does not exist yet is not
  an error and is instead DEAD FOR THE RUN: its `fp` stays null, and
  `rewind_read_files` skips a null `fp`, so even `-s` cannot revive it. td-txt
  opens it there too, which puts BOTH file lists in the compile and makes SCRIPT
  ORDER the whole of the answer -- `-e 'R f' -e 'w f'` reads nothing all run,
  where the same pair the other way round reads everything the `w` writes.

  What is opened is a HANDLE and not a copy, which is the half that a "the
  compile reads the file" reading would get wrong: a source that DOES exist at
  compile survives the later `w` truncating it, and goes on serving what that
  `w` flushes into the file it is already holding open. The three special names
  are the one kind of source the compile does not open at all -- `get_openfile`
  serves them from a static entry `ck_fopen` never sees -- so `R /dev/stdout` is
  still refused by the READ, and an `R` that never runs still never refuses.

  Two consequences are worth naming because neither is visible in the corpus. A
  source that will not open is spent for the RUN -- `fp == NULL` in GNU, a spent
  source here -- and `-s` cannot revive it, `rewind_read_files` skipping a null
  `fp`. That was already so, the failed open having been cached before this too;
  what MOVED is when the one attempt is made. And an `R` whose source BLOCKS --
  a fifo with no writer --
  now blocks the COMPILE, so `sed -e 'R fifo' -e ZZZ` hangs instead of reporting
  the bad command. That is GNU's behaviour, measured, and it is the price of the
  rule rather than an oversight.

  Position in the script is therefore what orders that failure against everything
  else, and it is the whole of the rule. A script error LATER loses to it and an
  EARLIER one wins (`-e 'w /nodir/f' -e '{'` is exit 4, `-e ZZ -e 'w /nodir/f'` is
  exit 1); the first of two unopenable targets is the one reported; an operand that
  cannot be read is never even opened. Labels are the case that looks like an
  exception and is not: they resolve only after the WHOLE parse, so a `w` failure
  beats an unresolvable label in EITHER order, and an openable one is created before
  the label is looked up. `--sandbox` is the other way round -- it refuses the
  command at the point the parse reaches it, BEFORE the open, so nothing is created,
  and in script order too, which is how `--sandbox -e 'w out' -e ZZ` reports the
  sandbox refusal while the same script without the flag creates `out` and reports
  the `ZZ`. The two device names are not files and nothing is created for either.

  The consequence for td-txt is that its PARSER can fail with a status other than 1.
  Compiling a script now touches the filesystem, so `parse_script` returns the same
  two-bucket error the runtime does: a malformed script is exit 1 behind an `-e
  expression #N' prefix, a refusal by the filesystem is a bare exit 4. Opening after
  the parse finished would be simpler and is wrong -- it cannot lose to an earlier
  script error, because by then there are no earlier errors left.

  What ends a command's ARGUMENT is the same question one step in, and the answer is
  not "the end of the command" for any of the three arguments that look like text. A
  LABEL (`:`, `b`, `t`, `T`) ends at a BLANK as much as at `;`, `}`, `#` or a newline,
  so `sed -n ':a p'` is the label `a` and then `p`, and `b a p` jumps to `a`. `v`'s
  optional version ends there too -- `v;p` runs the `p` -- and GNU compares it with
  glibc's `strverscmp` against its OWN version, so `v 4.10` is refused while `v 4.9`
  is not, `v 4a` is refused because `a` sorts above `.` while `v 4-9` is accepted
  because `-` sorts below it, and an empty argument compares as 4.0. td-txt compares
  against the GNU LEVEL it implements rather than its own version number, since
  comparing against 0.1.0 would refuse the `v 4.2` real scripts carry.

  `v` also DISCARDS any ADDRESS it was given, which is that same `continue` seen
  from the other side: GNU counts a command's slot only after the switch, so the
  slot `v` leaves through is abandoned with the address already copied into it and
  the next command overwrites it. `//v;p` therefore runs the `p`, where a committed
  `v` would fail for want of a previous regex to reuse. What can TELL is an address
  whose application leaves something behind, and two kinds do. A regex is what a
  later `//` reuses, so `/a/v;//p` prints `a` if the `v` applied it and is `no
  previous regular expression` if it did not -- either half of a range, empty or
  not, whatever the delimiter. And `$` forces the LOOKAHEAD that decides whether
  more input follows, which over several operands opens the next one: `sed -n
  '$v;F' f1 f2` names `f1 f1 f2` where a committed `$` names `f1 f2 f2`, and `$!`
  asks the same question so negating it does not spare it. A line number and a
  step are genuinely invisible.

  `s`'s FLAGS end only at a command terminator: a blank separates flags rather
  than ending them (`s/a/b/ g p` is both), an unknown letter is `unknown option to
  `s'` rather than the next command, and `g`, `p` and the occurrence number may not
  repeat while `i`/`I`/`m`/`M` may.

  One acceptance rule the command-form axis turned up on its own: GNU requires a
  command SEPARATOR after every command that does not consume the rest of the line,
  so `sed -n 'gp'` is an error and not `g` then `p`, and so is `g p`. `;`, a newline,
  `}`, `#` and end-of-script separate. A filename, `a`/`i`/`c` text and a comment
  need nothing because they swallow the line; a label and `v`'s version need nothing
  because GNU asks for nothing after them (`:a p` is a label then a command); and
  `s` needs nothing because its own flag loop enforces the same terminator set with
  its own diagnostic, while `y` has no flags and does need a separator. td-txt accepted all twenty of those juxtapositions, which is how a
  generator emitting `/re/Mgp` -- `g` is no address flag -- produced 34 divergences
  in one seed and looked like a regex bug.

  `-` is not stdin everywhere it can appear. It is stdin as an OPERAND and for `-f`
  (where GNU prefers stdin even when a file called `-` exists), and an ordinary
  filename to `r`, `R` and `w` (where it always was) and to an operand under `-i`
  (there being no rewriting a pipe). So `R -` reads a file named `-` and reads
  nothing at all when there is none, silently, because a missing `R` source is not
  an error.

  BEFORE any of that, the DELIMITER is found, and it is found by one rule with no
  regard for what character it is: GNU's `match_slash` tests the delimiter FIRST
  and the backslash second, so `\<delim>` is the delimiter as a literal for EVERY
  delimiter class -- `sed 'sxa\xbxXx'` replaces `axb` and `sed -n '\xa\xbxp'`
  selects it, an alphanumeric being no different from a `/`. Two consequences fall
  out of the ordering rather than being separate rules. A BACKSLASH is a usable
  delimiter, ending its half instead of escaping past it, which is how the manual's
  `\%regexp%` address reads when `%` is itself a backslash: `s\a\b\`, `y\ab\ba\`
  and `-n '\\^\p'` all parse. And `\n` is a newline only when `n` is NOT the
  delimiter, so `sna\nbnXn` matches the text `anb` where `sxa\nbxXx` matches a real
  newline.

  One byte is tested BEFORE the delimiter, and it is a bare NEWLINE: it ends the
  half as `unterminated`, ahead of bracket state too. So a newline is the one
  character that cannot be a delimiter (`s<nl>a<nl>b<nl>`), a bracket expression
  cannot contain one (`s/[<nl>]/X/` is refused, where a `/` there is fine), and a
  replacement needs the backslash that makes it a continuation (`s/a/x<nl>y/` is
  refused; `s/a/x\<nl>y/` inserts the newline). The diagnostic names the command
  that read the half, so `y/a<nl>/xy/` is `unterminated `y' command` and an address
  is `unterminated address regex`.

  A REPLACEMENT differs in exactly one place: the backslash is kept when the
  delimiter is `&`, because there it is the escape that means the character rather
  than the match, so `s&x&\&&` emits a literal `&`. The pattern half of that same
  command collapses as everything else does (`s&a\&b&X&` matches the text `a&b`).
  A delimiter inside a bracket expression is not a delimiter at all (`s/[/]/X/`).

  The escape VOCABULARY is decoded before anything else parses the text, and what
  the decoded byte means then depends on who reads it. GNU knows `\n`, `\t`, `\r`,
  `\f`, `\v`, `\a`, `\cX`, `\dNNN`, `\oNNN` and `\xHH` -- and NOT `\e`, worth
  pinning because it is the one every other tool has. In a PATTERN the byte is
  inserted RAW, arriving as regex SYNTAX rather than as a literal: `\x2e` is the
  metacharacter dot, `\x2a` quantifies, `\x5e` anchors, `\x5b` is an unmatched
  bracket, `\x5c` is a trailing backslash, `\x5cw` is the word class, and
  `[a\x2dz]` is a RANGE. That is also why an escape works inside a bracket
  expression, where the regex parser itself takes a backslash literally: the
  decoder ran first, so the bracket never sees one. A REPLACEMENT decodes to a
  LITERAL instead -- `\x26` is an ampersand that does not stand for the match and
  `\x5c1` is a backslash and a `1`, not a backreference -- while `a`/`i`/`c` text
  and both halves of `y///` decode to plain bytes, `y`'s operand lengths being
  measured AFTER the decoding (`y/X\x59/12/` is two for two). An escape GNU does
  not know keeps its backslash for the next parser in a pattern (`\w`, `\1`) and
  sheds it everywhere else (`a A\qB` appends `AqB`). Three more rules earn their
  place: the one-liner `a text` form eats a LEADING backslash before decoding, so
  `a \t.` appends `t.` while `a \\x41` appends `A`; a decoded byte is never read
  again, so `\x5cx41` is a backslash and `x41`; and `\c` takes exactly one
  character, refusing a nested escape (`\c\q` is `recursive escaping after \c not
  allowed`) except the doubled `\c\\`, which is 0x1c. A `\c` with NOTHING to
  control leaves a BARE BACKSLASH, and one rule covers all three readers because
  each resolves that differently: the regex compiler rejects it (`Trailing
  backslash`), a replacement keeps it, a text buffer drops it -- which is why
  `y/X/\c/` is a length error and `s/X/A\c/` emits `A\`. FILENAMES decode
  nothing: neither implementation reads an escape in an `r`/`w` target, so `r
  \x72f` reads a file literally named `\x72f` in both. An earlier draft of that
  last note claimed otherwise and a reviewer caught it; the probe is one line, so
  run it rather than reasoning by analogy from the bracket case.

  A POSIX SUB-EXPRESSION -- `[:class:]`, `[.collating.]`, `[=equivalence=]` --
  runs to the character that OPENED it followed by a `]`, and both of sed's
  readers have to know that. To the delimiter reader it means the `]` of
  `[:alpha:]` does not end the set, so a delimiter after one is still a member
  (`s/[[:alpha:]/]/X/`, `s][[:alpha:]]]X]`), a closer that does not match its
  opener never closes (`[[:alpha.]]` runs to the end of the script and is
  `unterminated`, as is `[[:]]`), and a `]` inside a collating element is a
  member with the set still open (`[[.].]]`). To the regex compiler it decides
  what may BOUND A RANGE: only a collating element names a single character, so
  `[[.a.]-z]` is the range `a-z` -- it selects `m` and not `-` -- while
  `[[:alpha:]-z]` and `[[=a=]-z]` are `Invalid range end`, on either side of the
  `-`. A completed RANGE names no single character either, which is the same rule
  and the reason `[a-b-c]` is refused; a trailing `-` before the `]` is the
  literal it is anywhere else, so `[a-b-]` is not.

  The two readers part company on ONE thing, and it is why `[[...]]` is a set
  holding a dot to grep and `unterminated` to sed. sed's is a FOUR-STATE MACHINE
  over the bracket expression (`snarf_char_class`, compile.c:456) rather than a
  parser: the kind character only ARMS the closing `]`, and any other byte
  disarms it again, where the regex compiler looks for the pair. So the PARITY
  of a run of kind characters decides -- `[[..]]` and `[[....]]` close, while
  `[[...]]` and `[[.....]]` run to the end of the script. Same for `[[:::]]` and
  `[[===]]`.

  A `[` is the one byte that disarms nothing, and that is the half no amount of
  reasoning about a consumed closer predicts -- an earlier draft here had the
  parity right and this wrong. It arms the machine from outside a sub-expression
  and is inert everywhere else, so `[[..[]]` is still closed at the `]` after it
  (`[[::[]]` and `[[==[]]` likewise), while `[[...][.[.]]` is the parity rule
  again and is unterminated. The reader REJECTS none of these: all it decides is
  where the half ends, so the first three reach the compiler and come back
  `Unmatched [` where the fourth never gets there.

  GNU also refuses the `[:alpha:]` that was meant to be `[[:alpha:]]`, and by the
  SHAPE of the list rather than the name in it: colons at both ends, a NON-COLON
  somewhere between them, and neither a sub-expression nor a range in the list.
  So `[:*:]` and `[^:a:]` are refused while `[:]`, `[::]`, `[::::]`, `[:alpha]`,
  `[:a[.b.]:]` and `[:a-z:]` are all ordinary lists. A member error outranks it
  (`[:a-:]` is `Invalid range end`), and so does EVERY other error, anywhere in
  the pattern: it is a lint on something that otherwise compiles, so
  `[:alpha:]\` is `Trailing backslash` and `[:alpha:][z-a]` is `Invalid range
  end`. Its message is GNU's own -- `character class syntax is [[:space:]], not
  [:space:]` -- and in sed it is the one PATTERN error reported BARE and exiting
  4, where every other one is exit 1 behind an `-e expression #N' prefix; grep
  reports it at its usual exit 2.

  Two smaller orderings came out of the same sweep. A sub-expression at a range's
  HIGH end is judged by its KIND before its name is read, so `[a-[:bogus:]]` is
  `Invalid range end` rather than a bad class name, while at the LOW end it is a
  member first and `[[:bogus:]-a]` reports the name; a collating element is named
  at either end, being the one kind that could have bounded the range. And a
  bracket whose body is still EMPTY when the pattern runs out is `Invalid regular
  expression`, not `Unmatched [` -- `[` and `[^` against `[a` and `[]`.

  A BRE `$` is an ANCHOR at the end of the pattern and before the `\)` or `\|`
  that ends a branch, and the LITERAL it looks like anywhere else -- except that
  GNU tests the byte after it against `|` and `)`, the ERE spellings of those two
  operators, while keeping the length test their two-byte BRE spellings need. So
  a BARE `|` or `)` anchors it too, and only where it is not the pattern's LAST
  byte: `x$|` and `x$)` select the text they look like, `x$||`, `x$|a` and `x$))`
  select nothing, and an ordinary byte never reaches the rule (`x$az`). What is
  counted is the bytes left in the PATTERN, not in the branch, so neither
  `x$|\|zz` nor `\(x$|\)` hands the literal back. GNU's two engines then read
  that mid-branch anchor differently and `Options::glibc_engine` is that split:
  grep's dfa satisfies it at a true end of line, so `grep 'x$|\?'` selects a line
  `x`, while glibc satisfies one never, so the branch carrying it is dead and
  sed's `s@x$|\?@<&>@` replaces nothing. "Last byte" is of the whole LEX, which
  is wider than the pattern in two ways, both grep's and both carried by
  `Options::lex_continues`: it joins its `-e`/`-f` patterns with `\n` before
  lexing one, and `-x`/`-w` wrap what it lexes in context. So `grep -e 'x$|' -e
  zz`, `grep -x 'x$|'` and `grep -w 'x$|'` all anchor the `$` that `grep 'x$|'`
  alone leaves a literal, and an empty `-e ''` after it counts as a byte too.

  One thing SHORTENS that lex: GNU drops a DUPLICATE pattern before lexing it,
  keeping the FIRST. Matching cannot see that -- a repeat selects nothing its
  first copy did not -- but the rule above can, because dropping a repeat can
  change which pattern ENDS the lex. So `grep -c -e 'x$|' -e q -e 'x$|'` answers
  as `-e 'x$|' -e q` does and `-e q -e 'x$|' -e q` as `-e q -e 'x$|'` does. The
  test is on BYTES rather than meaning, so `-i` does not make `X$|` a repeat of
  `x$|`, and there is ONE list: `-e`, `-f` and the newlines inside a single
  argument all contribute to it.

  One more property of the list as a whole decides whether grep reads the input
  at all. With `-v`, an EMPTY pattern selects every line and so leaves nothing to
  select, and GNU takes a short cut that suppresses even `-c`'s count line -- but
  only where EVERY pattern is empty. That is narrower than "every line matches":
  `-e '' -e a` matches every line too, and `.*` and `^` match every line without
  being empty, yet all three print `0`. So `grep -cv -e ''` prints nothing while
  `grep -cv -e a -e ''` prints `0`. The syntax does not matter (`-F` and `-E`
  read alike) and `-x`/`-w` take the short cut away from both, having narrowed
  the empty pattern to something that no longer selects every line.

  What an output line carries in FRONT of it is decided per LINE: a name, then a
  number under `-n`, each followed by `:` on a selected line and `-` on a context
  line, with `-Z` replacing the byte after the NAME only. `-H` and `-h` decide
  whether there is a name at all, and absent either it follows from the OPERANDS
  rather than from how many files were searched: more than one operand, or one
  that was a DIRECTORY descended into under `-r` (or `-R`, or `-d recurse`, which
  is the same setting spelled a third way and names the same way).
  So `grep -rn a sd` names the
  single file it finds under `sd` while `grep -rn a sd/only` does not, and a bare
  `grep -r a` names too, the `.` it walks being a descended directory.

  What counts as a file PROCESSED is decided at the OPEN -- with one exception
  below -- rather than at the read. An
  operand grep opened and then could not read is one it processed and found
  nothing in, so `grep -c alpha d` prints `0` for a directory and `grep -L alpha
  d` names it, both beside the `d: Is a directory` diagnostic and exit 2, and
  `grep -c alpha d f` prints `d:0` before `f:1`. An operand it could not open at
  all earns none of that: a missing file and one refusing permission print no
  count line. `-l` and a plain search have nothing to say either way, having
  found no match. `-l` and `-L` are ONE question -- does this file match, or does
  it not -- so GNU answers whichever was asked LAST and a third flag does not
  reopen the first: `grep -lL a f g` answers as `-L`, `-Ll` as `-l`, `-lLl` as
  `-l` again, and the long spellings mix with the short ones. Whichever won still
  outranks `-c`, and `-q` outranks all of them, printing nothing at all. `-s`
  silences the diagnostic and leaves the count line, the two being different
  questions. The exception is GNU's `fstat` after the open: a descriptor it
  opened but cannot stat earns no count line either, which is reachable only as
  a CLOSED stdin (`grep -c alpha - 0<&-`) and not reachable at all from here,
  since Rust's runtime re-points a closed descriptor 0 at `/dev/null` before
  `main`. It is in the gap list rather than this rule.

  The MATCHER selectors have the same shape and the opposite answer: `-E`, `-F`
  and `-G` choose one thing, and GNU REFUSES two different ones -- `conflicting
  matchers specified`, exit 2 -- rather than keeping the last. Repeating one is
  fine (`-EE`), so the rule is not "at most one selector"; and the test is per
  TOKEN against what was already CHOSEN, so `-EEF` refuses too, while the
  `Basic` that is merely the default is not a choice and a bare `-E` is fine
  where `-G -E` is not. The long spellings choose the same thing and conflict
  with the short ones. It is raised where the second selector is READ, so an
  error before it wins (`grep -m x -EF` is `invalid max count`) and everything
  after it loses -- a bad pattern and a missing file among them.

  CONTEXT is a range, and three rules follow from that rather than from what
  gets printed. The group separator marks where the RANGES break, so `-o`
  suppresses no `--` at all: `grep -o -B1 a f` over `a/b/A/aa` prints `a`,
  `--`, `a`, `a`, the `--` standing between ranges whose context line printed
  nothing. A ZERO count is still a range, so `grep -B0 a h` separates where a
  bare `grep` does not -- the question is whether a context flag was GIVEN, not
  whether its count is non-zero. And `-C` does not override an explicit
  `-A`/`-B`: it supplies the default for whichever was not given, so `-A2 -C0`
  keeps two lines after and takes zero before, `-C0 -A2` is the same thing, and
  `-A2 -B1 -C3` ignores the `-C` outright. Among themselves each is
  last-one-wins: a second `-A` replaces the first, a second `-C` the first.
  `-NUM` is a fourth spelling of `-C` and shares its slot, so `-C3 -1` is one
  and `-1 -C3` is three; it is an ordinary cluster element, a RUN of digits
  making one count (`-12` is twelve, not one then two) with parsing going on
  after it, so `-2n`, `-n2` and `-2m1` all mean what they look like. It is one
  count in every POSITION too, which GNU is not -- see the permuted-`-NUM`
  divergence below, where GNU drops a run's first digit and reads the rest as
  one number all the same. That run
  is capped at 21 SIGNIFICANT digits -- leading zeros not counting -- and the
  22nd is `<the 21 it took>...: invalid context length argument` at exit 2. The
  cap belongs to the spelling and not to the number: the same value handed to
  `-A`/`-B`/`-C` is read without complaint.

  WHERE an option may SIT is decided by the environment. GNU permutes: it
  collects options wherever they appear, so `grep a h -n` numbers its output
  even though the flag came after the file. POSIX says the first operand ends
  the options, and `POSIXLY_CORRECT` is what asks GNU for that -- so with it set
  the same command reports `-n: No such file or directory` at exit 2, having
  read it as a third operand. Both applets do this, sed with its SCRIPT in the
  first operand's place. The variable is read for PRESENCE and never for a
  value: empty, `0` and `no` all stop the permutation, so nothing but unsetting
  it turns the permutation back on. What "operand" means here is positional and
  nothing else -- an option-ARGUMENT is not one, so the `a` in `-e a -n h` and
  the `2` in `-m 2 a h -n` do not end anything, and each flag that PRECEDES the
  first operand is still taken; a bare `-` is one, being stdin's name; and which
  operand SLOT is being filled does not matter, so in `-n h a` the pattern `h`
  ends the options and `a` is a file. Past that point `--` has no marker
  meaning left to have and names a file like anything else, and so does a
  `-NUM`. BEFORE the first operand everything parses as it always did.

  Option ORDER is all td-txt reads the variable for. In sed GNU gives it a
  second job -- a POSIX mode -- which td-txt does NOT drive from it; that is
  the gap recorded below, and the reason is that the variable and `--posix` are
  not the same switch. grep gets only the option-order half in GNU too: its ERE
  rules do not change with the variable.

  WHICH lines `-o` prints is a third thing, and it is not "the ones that
  match". GNU prints the spans of the MATCHING lines it prints -- the selected
  line without `-v`, the CONTEXT line with it. So `grep -o -B1 -n -v a m` over
  `a/aa/b/aaa` prints `2-a` twice, the two spans of `aa`, prefixed `-` like any
  context line, while the selected lines print nothing at all, having no match
  to print by definition. Without `-v` the two questions have the same answer,
  which is why the rule reads as "`-o` drops context lines" on almost every
  input; `-m` is where they come apart, since a context line PAST the limit can
  match and still print nothing. `-m` never shortens the drain itself.

  A NUMERIC option argument goes through ONE reader, shared by `-A`, `-B`,
  `-C` and `-m`: leading whitespace (the C set, tab through carriage return and
  space), an optional SIGN, decimal digits with leading zeros allowed and no
  octal reading of them, and nothing at all after them -- so ` +1` and `010`
  are counts where `1 ` and `0x10` are `invalid context length argument`. A
  value past what fits SATURATES rather than failing, since any count past the
  input's length already means what the largest one means. What a NEGATIVE
  means is the option's question rather than the reader's: `-m -1` is no limit
  at all, a context count refuses one -- and `-0` is a zero to both, being the
  one negative whose value is not.

  `--help` and `--version` are answered AFTER that scan rather than where they
  are read, so anything the scan itself raises outranks them from either side:
  `grep --help -EF` is the conflict above and `grep --help -m x` is `invalid max
  count`, both at exit 2, and so is `grep --help -f /nonexistent` -- a `-f` file
  is READ during the scan, which is what puts it on this side of the line. What
  comes after the scan loses instead, so `grep --help -e '['` and `grep --help
  nosuchpat nosuchfile` both print the usage and exit 0. Asking for both, in
  either order, answers `--version`. The TEXT of either answer is td-txt's own
  and is pinned in divergence.test.txt.

  `-V` is `--version`'s short spelling and every sentence above holds for it,
  because it sets the same flag rather than answering where it is read. It was
  a REFUSAL until this was written down, which made td-txt reject an option
  whose own synonym it served -- a gap rather than a decision, and the only
  one of GNU's short options in that shape. It needs its own cases even so:
  the cluster loop reaches it, not the long-option table, so `-iV` and `-Vi`
  are paths `--version` never takes.

  That rule is what makes td-txt's REFUSALS visible from both sides. td-txt's
  scan raises on options GNU accepts -- `-P`, `--color` and the rest of the
  deliberate omissions below -- so `grep --help -P` is a refusal at exit 2
  here where GNU prints its usage at exit 0. The refusal is the pinned decision
  and the deferral only decides WHEN it is reached; before it, the same command
  written the other way round (`grep -P --help`) already refused, so this makes
  the two orders agree with each other rather than one of them agree with GNU.
  That case read `-b` until `-b` was served; the point is the refusal rather
  than which option carries it.

  `-r`'s WALK admits the ROOT and the tree below it by different rules. The root
  is FOLLOWED, so a symlinked directory named as an operand is descended and a
  device named as one is read; below it nothing is followed, so a symlink the
  walk finds is skipped and never reaches the search at all -- that is `fts`'s
  FTS_PHYSICAL and no option turns it back on. Everything else the walk finds
  DOES reach the search, devices included, and whether it is read is `-D`'s
  answer below rather than the walk's. It used to be the walk's: only regular
  files were collected, which reproduced the default by hard-coding it and left
  `-D read` with nothing to act on. `-` is stdin under `-r` as it is without it,
  being neither a directory to descend nor a name to walk, and it does not name
  on its own -- but only as an OPERAND: a file the walk finds that happens to be
  called `-` is searched like any other.

  The walk's ORDER is td-txt's and not GNU's, and deliberately. Both descend
  into a directory at the point they meet it -- that is fts(3)'s traversal --
  and what differs is the order the entries of one directory come in. GNU
  passes NO comparison function to `fts_open` (grep.c:1868), so it takes the
  kernel's; td-txt sorts them (`entries.sort()` in `walk_from`). td-txt is
  therefore fts with a name comparator rather than a different traversal.

  Per-DIRECTORY, which is not the same as sorting the paths and is worth being
  exact about, since the two agree on almost every tree. `-` and `.` sort below
  `/`, so for `mid/aaa`, `mid-z` and `mid.a` a path sort gives `mid-z mid.a
  mid/aaa` where this walk gives `mid/aaa mid-z mid.a`: `mid` sorts before both
  siblings among the entries of the root, and is descended when it is reached.

  So GNU's `grep -r` output order is a property of the FILESYSTEM rather than
  of grep. Two checkouts of one tree can print the same matches in a different
  order, and so can one tree after a file is REPLACED -- write-a-temp-and-
  rename, which is what `sed -i` does; measured on btrfs, that moves the entry
  to the end (`a b c d` -> `b c d a`) where an in-place truncate-and-write
  leaves it alone.

  "Whatever readdir returns" is itself only true of SMALL directories, which is
  worth knowing before checking this against grep. gnulib's fts -- which grep
  bundles -- sorts a directory's entries by INODE NUMBER when it has no
  comparator and the directory holds MORE than 10000 of them, on every
  filesystem but CIFS/NFS/TMPFS. Line numbers are grep 3.11's vendored copy,
  which is what the scored binary runs, and later gnulib has both moved them
  and added an exemption: lib/fts.c:151 the threshold's value, 1568 the test,
  1624 the sort, 766-771 the three exempt types.

  More than, not at least, and the off-by-one is the code's rather than a
  quibble: the test reads `nitems == THRESHOLD` and runs BEFORE the `++nitems`
  under it, so it is true while reading the 10001st entry and a directory of
  exactly 10000 never reaches it. Measured on names and inodes deliberately
  ordered against each other, the boundary is exactly there -- 9999 and 10000
  come back in readdir order, 10001 and 10002 in inode order. That strengthens
  the point rather than weakening it: above the threshold the order depends on
  the filesystem's ALLOCATION HISTORY, which is less reproducible still.

  Measured on the tree the conformance test builds, GNU prints `root/mid/beta
  root/mid/aaa root/adir/x root/zdir/y root/zeta root/alpha root/mmm
  root/mid-z root/mid.a` -- readdir order, which is that tree's creation order
  on btrfs, and neither sorted nor td-txt's.

  Sorting is the choice a distribution builder should make: a recipe that greps
  a tree has to produce the same bytes on every build, and "the order the kernel
  felt like" is the one input to that which nothing declares. It is also why
  this is recorded rather than closed -- matching GNU here would mean
  reproducing an artifact of the directory, which is not reproducible even for
  GNU. There is no golden against GNU to have; there IS one against td-txt, and
  both the conformance test and a divergence.test.txt case use it, on a fixture
  shaped so that a path sort and a per-directory sort disagree.

  `-R` is the LOGICAL walk, and it is not `-r` with a longer name: EVERY symlink
  is followed rather than only the operand, and -- the one consequence of `-R`
  that is not about symlinks at all -- it FLIPS the device default (grep.c:3007),
  so the devices `-r` passes by are read. `grep -R` over a tree holding a FIFO
  therefore blocks where `grep -r` finishes, which is GNU's behaviour rather than
  a defect in either. An explicit `-D` still wins, whichever order the two came
  in, because the flip tests for the default alone.

  - `-D`/`--devices` is `-d`'s neighbour in every way but the ones that matter,
    and all three divergences run the same direction: the option that looks like
    a matched pair with `-d` shares none of its machinery. GNU parses `-d` with
    `XARGMATCH` and `-D` with plain `STREQ` (grep.c:2529), so `-d rec` resolves
    to `recurse` while `-D rea` is an error; `-d`'s complaint quotes the value
    and names the option (`invalid argument 'nope' for '--directories'`) where
    `-D`'s does neither (`unknown devices method`); and `-d` exits 1 where `-D`
    exits 2. None of the three is inferable from the others, and an empty
    argument shows the first most sharply -- `-d ''` is AMBIGUOUS, the empty
    string being a prefix of all three names, while `-D ''` is the same flat
    refusal every other non-name gets.

    Its DEFAULT is a third state neither spelling can ask for. GNU calls it
    `READ_COMMAND_LINE_DEVICES`: a device NAMED as an operand is read, and the
    same device found by the WALK is skipped without a word. That is why
    `grep PAT fifo` waits for a writer while `grep -r PAT dir` passes the same
    fifo by, and why the option's two values are both overrides rather than one
    of them being the status quo. A device is four file types and not the two
    the word suggests -- character, block, socket and FIFO (`is_device_mode`,
    grep.c:612) -- so a regular file and a directory are the only things that
    are not one. A DIRECTORY is therefore `-d`'s to answer and never `-D`'s;
    the two skips compose. Stdin is exempt by DESCRIPTOR, as it is for `-d`, so
    `grep -D skip PAT - <fifo` reads the fifo and a device merely NAMED `-`
    cannot make `-D skip` pass over a pipe.

    WHERE the question is asked differs between the two paths, which is not a
    detail: a name found BELOW the walk's root is decided from a stat BEFORE
    the open, and an OPERAND is opened first and decided from the descriptor.
    GNU splits it the same way and says why -- `grepdirent` stats "since
    opening might have side effects on a device" -- and a SOCKET makes the
    difference observable rather than theoretical, because opening one fails
    `ENXIO`. The same socket is silent when the walk finds it and loud when it
    is named, and `-D skip` does NOT quiet the named one: the open fails before
    any policy is consulted. Deciding the walk case after the open instead
    produces a diagnostic GNU never prints, which is how the split was found.
    The operand path still needs `O_NONBLOCK` (grep.c:1758) for the case the
    stat does not cover, and only where skipping is possible: a FIFO opened to
    be skipped must not wait for a writer, while under `-D read` that wait IS
    the request. Its value is Linux's and is pinned against the KERNEL rather
    than against the header it came from -- `/proc/self/fdinfo` reports the
    flags an open really took -- because a wrong constant is a different flag
    the kernel accepts, and `-D skip` would come to block on a fifo with every
    other test still green.

    The walk's ROOT is on the OPERAND side of that split, and it is the one
    place "the walk produced this name" and "this name is not an operand" come
    apart. GNU counts `fts_level == FTS_ROOTLEVEL` as a command-line name
    however far the walk below it goes, so `grep -r PAT fifo` READS the fifo
    where the same fifo one directory down is skipped -- and a socket named as
    a walk root is OPENED, reporting its ENXIO, even under `-D skip`. Treating
    every walk result alike makes `grep -rzam1 '^' /dev/zero` exit 1 where GNU
    exits 0. `/dev/null` cannot show any of this: it reads as empty, so read
    and skip are both exit 1 with nothing printed, which is exactly why the
    drift axis for it came back green until `/dev/zero` replaced it.

    The type each name turns out to be is carried FROM the walk rather than
    asked again: the walk stats every entry moments before the search opens it,
    and asking a second time cost about 28% of `grep -r` over a wide tree of
    small files (a further 10% went on asking the descriptor as well, for names
    the walk had already answered for).

    NOT done here, and pre-existing rather than this option's: GNU opens every
    search with `O_NOCTTY` (grep.c:1753) and td-txt opens with none, so
    `grep PAT /dev/tty` from a session that has no controlling terminal could
    ACQUIRE one. `--devices` widens the reach slightly, a walk-found character
    device now being openable under `-R` or `-D read` where before the walk
    dropped it, but the hole is as old as the search. Closing it means deciding
    whether sed's reader shares the flag, which GNU sed does not set -- a
    question about a different program, so it is its own landing.

    What is NOT reproduced is the ambiguity POSSIBILITY ORDER, which `--devices`
    makes reachable in more cells without causing it. GNU lists the candidates in
    its own `long_options` order (`--devices` `--directories`
    `--dereference-recursive`) and td-txt's table is alphabetical, so `--d=skip`
    names the same three in a different sequence. It already diverged for `--d`
    before this option existed, and `--de` is the one ambiguity `--devices` newly
    creates, having resolved uniquely to `--dereference-recursive` before.

    Matching it means reordering the whole table, and the size of that is worth
    recording so the landing is not guessed at: exactly FOUR prefixes print a
    different list, `--d` and `--de` in one cluster and `--f`/`--fi` in the
    other. Every other ambiguous prefix names candidates the two orderings agree
    on, and an EXACT name is never an ambiguity at all, so the rest of the table
    is untouched either way. What it costs is that td-txt's table stops being
    alphabetical -- GNU groups its own by purpose, which is why the orders differ
    -- and that is the trade to weigh, not the message count.

  Following what the walk finds is the usual way a directory becomes reachable
  from inside itself, but NOT the only one -- a bind mount does it with no
  symlink at all -- so BOTH walks carry the ancestor chain and refuse to
  re-enter one, as GNU's do. Identity is device and inode rather than the path:
  two names for one directory are equal without being the same string, and that
  bind mount is the case where no comparison of strings could tell. The refusal
  is a WARNING, `X: warning: recursive directory loop`, after which the walk
  carries on and the exit status is whatever the search concluded; `-s` silences
  it like any other message. Only an ANCESTOR is a cycle: a link to a SIBLING is
  followed, and the file under it is searched twice under its two names. The
  warnings and the walk's ERRORS are one stream in the order the walk met them,
  which is how GNU emits them -- not all of one kind and then all of the other.

  A name that EXISTS without RESOLVING -- a dangling symlink, a loop -- is passed
  to the search rather than reported by the walk, wherever it is found. That is
  what keeps a search which never OPENS it silent: where a match is reached
  first, `grep -Rq a t` says nothing while `grep -Rl a t` reports `No such file
  or directory`, and GNU draws the line in the same place. Reached first is the
  whole of it -- with the dangling link alone in the tree, both tools report
  under `-q` too.

  A COUNT line is the exception to the number -- it takes the name, `-Z` and all,
  but never a number, having no line to number. So `grep -cn a one two` prints
  `one:2` where `grep -n a one two` prints `one:1:a`, and `-cnZ` ends the name
  with the NUL that `-n` would otherwise have preceded.

  A REPETITION OPERATOR needs something to repeat, and the two tools disagree
  about what happens when it has nothing -- which is the first place their regex
  grammars are not one grammar with two spellings. `Options::strict_repeats` is
  that split (sed true, grep false); it is not a knob but which syntax bits glibc
  was handed. A zero-width assertion is never something to repeat, so an operator
  after one is in the same position as an operator after nothing at all:

    * in a BRE the operator is then a LITERAL character -- `\B\?a` reads its `\?`
      exactly as `\?a` does and selects the text `?a`, and `\B*a` selects `*a`.
      grep says `warning: stray \ before ?` where sed says nothing, and neither
      refuses. A leading `^` behaves the same way (`^*` selects lines starting
      with `*`), which is the older rule this one generalizes;
    * sed reads it that way ANYWHERE, not only at the start of a branch: `x\b*`
      selects the `x*` in `x*y`, `[x]\b*` too, and a second operator quantifies
      the LITERAL, so `q\b**` selects `q*` where `qa**` is refused. grep parts
      company past the branch start and repeats the assertion instead (`x\B\+b`
      selects `xb` and not `x b`);
    * an INTERVAL is the exception: sed refuses `\{` with nothing to repeat
      (`\{2\}a`, `\<\{2\}a`, `x\b\{2\}y`) where grep warns `stray \ before {` and
      reads a literal brace, so `grep '\{2\}'` selects lines containing `{2}`;
    * an ERE keeps its operators operators, so the two dialects part again: sed
      refuses every one of them on an assertion -- `^*`, `^?`, `^{2}`, `$*`,
      `\`*`, `\b+`, `\B*`, `\<*` -- mid-pattern included (`x\b*b`), which a group
      around the assertion lifts (`(^)*a` compiles); grep REPEATS the assertion,
      and asserting a position n times at one position asserts it once, so only
      the lower bound survives: `\b*a` leaves nothing behind, `\b+a` still
      anchors. An operator with nothing at all before it is DROPPED there rather
      than made literal (`grep -E '*a'` is the pattern `a`), except that the drop
      costs the pattern one `)`: `(*)` and `(a|*)` are an unmatched `(` where
      `(*|a)` and `()` are fine. A brace grep cannot read as
      an interval is a literal, content and all, so `-E '{}a'` selects `{}a`
      while `-E 'a{}'` -- a brace WITH something to repeat -- is an error.

  That last refusal is not about branch starts, which is what makes it a rule
  rather than a special case: an ASSERTION gives its operators nothing to repeat
  either, so a grep ERE drops them and is back exactly where a branch start is.
  Hence `(a\b*)` refuses like `(*)` though its branch is not empty, `(\b*a)` is
  fine because something follows, and it is the INNER paren that decides --
  `((a\b*))` refuses, `(\b*)a` refuses, `(z|\b*)` refuses and `(\b*|z)` does
  not. A brace left with nothing to repeat refuses the same way without any
  operator at all: `({)` and `(\b*{)`, against `({a)` and `(a{)`. Only `*`/`+`/`?`
  drop; `{n}` does not, and a BRE never does this at all (`\(a\b*\)` compiles),
  which is why this is `strict_repeats`-and-ERE territory.

  What the drop does to that `)` is EAT it. gnulib drops the operator and
  recurses on the next token, and an unmatched `)` reached that way is an
  ordinary character, so the paren is consumed: it neither closes the group nor
  is available to anything else. A `)` no drop has eaten then closes the group,
  which is why `({))`, `(\b*))` and `(a\b*))` all compile while `({)`, `(\b*)`
  and `(a\b*)` do not. One an ENCLOSING group needs is not available either
  (`((a\b*))` refuses), and neither is one inside a bracket expression, where it
  is content (`([)]\b*)` refuses).

  Eating is what DEPTH has nothing to do with, and that is the part two drafts of
  this paragraph got wrong. A drop at depth 0 eats a paren no group needed --
  nothing is owed for it -- but it is eaten all the same, so it cannot settle the
  group still waiting behind it: `(*)\b*)` refuses, where `(*))` compiles and
  `(*)\b*))`, with a third paren, compiles again. The first draft read the rule
  as "what it dropped may not sit against a `)`", which a reviewer's sweep put at
  ~860 wrongly refused patterns, every one carrying an extra `)`; the second read
  it as a debt any spare `)` could pay, which wrongly ACCEPTED 50 that a paren
  eaten at depth 0 should have left unclosed, and which main had right. Adjacency
  and balance are both the wrong frame; consumption is the right one.

  Eating is also idempotent, per paren rather than per drop, because two drops
  reach the same one in more shapes than is obvious: a branch start runs twice
  over `(*{)`, dropping the operator and then meeting the brace, and an assertion
  hands `(\b*{)` to that same branch start. Each of those eats one `)`, so
  `(*{))` and `(\b*{))` compile. Groups still count separately: `({)({))` leaves
  two waiting against one paren and refuses, `({)({)))` has two of each and
  compiles, `(*{)(*{))` refuses. And it is spent left to right, so a `)` reached
  BEFORE the drop settles nothing -- `)({)` refuses, that paren having already
  been read as a literal.

  The complaint is raised only once the WHOLE pattern has parsed, so an error
  found further along outranks it -- `(a\b*)\` is `Trailing backslash`,
  `(a\b*)[z-a]` is `Invalid range end` -- while it outranks the class-syntax
  lint, which is judged later still: `(a\b*)[:alpha:]` is the unmatched paren and
  `(a\b*))[:alpha:]`, its group settled, is the lint. Same deferred shape as
  `class_syntax` itself, and the same reason: a rule about the whole pattern
  cannot report before the whole pattern has been read.

  An INTERVAL earlier in the branch then RESCUES what would otherwise refuse --
  but not uniformly, and the asymmetry is the part no reading predicts. It
  rescues a bare dropped operator and a brace (`({2}*)`, `(*{2})`, `({2}{)` and
  `(\b*{2}{)` all compile, where every one of them without the interval
  refuses), and it does NOT rescue an assertion's own operator: `({2}\b*)`
  refuses where `({2}*)` compiles, and so do `(\b{2}\b*)` and `({2}a\b*)`. So
  the interval is not simply "a token that counts"; which drop it covers depends
  on what did the dropping. Probing only the bare-operator half would have
  confirmed "an interval turns the rule off", which is what an earlier draft of
  this paragraph said -- differential fuzzing against the oracle is what produced
  the counterexample.

  All of that turns on the NOTHING-TO-REPEAT state, and where it holds is a rule
  of its own with a second consequence. An unusable brace -- one that CLOSES on
  content no interval can have, `{}` and `{2,1}` -- is the text it is while that
  state holds, and the complaint about its content once it does not. So `{}` and
  `a{}` differ, and the state is what says which a pattern gets.

  It is decided by the token just before the brace, and only that one. An
  ASSERTION or ANCHOR ESTABLISHES it wherever it stands, which is the part that
  reads as preservation and is not: `a\b{}` is text though `a{}` is a complaint,
  and so are `a${}`, `(a)\b{}` and `a{2}\b{}` -- after an atom, after a group,
  after an interval alike. `*`/`+`/`?` leave it alone, applied to an assertion or
  dropped at a branch start (`\b*{}`, `*{}`). A brace already made text PRESERVES
  it without establishing it, so `{{}` compiles and a run does too (`{{{{}`)
  while `a{{}` does not. Everything else ends it: an ordinary atom (`a{}`,
  `\ba{}`), a group (`(){}`), a `}` -- an ordinary atom like any other, which is
  why `}{}` refuses and therefore `{}{}` does, a complete pair ending on one.

  A VALID interval ends it too although it may be dropped, and that is the
  asymmetry no reading predicts: `{2}{}a` is `Invalid content of \{\}` where
  `{}a` compiles, `^{2}{}` and `\b{2}{}` are errors where `^{}` and `\b{}` are
  not, and every spelling does it (`{2,}{}`). But it ends it only until the next
  assertion, not for the branch: `\b{2}\b{}` and `a{2}\b{}` compile. An earlier
  draft of this entry said "for the rest of the branch" and tracked a latch to
  match; a reviewer's `a\b{}` is what showed the state has no memory at all.
  A brace that never CLOSES needs none of this and rewinds to text on its own,
  which is why `{2}{` compiles where `{2}{}` does not.

  This is grep's ERE alone. Its BRE reads a brace as text wherever it sits, and
  sed's ERE refuses the lot, a brace there always opening an interval -- so
  `sed -E 's/^{}/X/p'` is `Invalid preceding regular expression` while the BRE
  spelling substitutes. Both are cases rather than left to the reader.

  Two more places sed's ERE is the strict one where grep's reads TEXT, both
  turning on the same `strict_repeats` flag. A `{` in sed's ERE always opens an
  interval, so an unusable one is an error and never a literal: with nothing to
  repeat it is `Invalid preceding regular expression` whatever follows the brace
  (`{`, `{2`, `{x}`), and with something to repeat GNU asks whether it CLOSES
  before judging content -- `a{` and `a{2` are `Unmatched \{` where `a{x}` is
  `Invalid content of \{\}`. That question skips escaped pairs, so `a{x\}` is
  unmatched, `a{x\}}` is bad content, and `a{x\\}` is bad content again because
  the escaped BACKSLASH is what got skipped. And an unmatched `)` is
  `Unmatched ) or \)` there (`)`, `a)`, `(a))`), where grep makes it the character
  it is at depth 0; an EMPTY group `()` is not that and still matches empty in
  both. That second rule is a GNU EXTENSION, so `--posix` drops it and the
  character is ordinary again -- while leaving the interval rules alone, which is
  why the two halves of this paragraph need different treatment under the flag.
  td-txt drops it for BOTH spellings, as GNU does: `sed --posix 's/a\)/X/'` and
  the ERE `s/a)/X/` each read the paren as the character.

  Stacking is otherwise unchanged and is the same two rules as before, both of
  them SED's: its BRE refuses a second `*` or interval after ANY operator (`a**`,
  `a\+*`, `a\{2\}*`, `\(a\)**`) while TAKING `\+` and `\?` there (`a*\+`, `a\?\?`),
  and its ERE stacks all four freely (`a**`, `a+*`, `a{1,2}{1,2}`). grep refuses
  none of them in either dialect.

  A BACKREFERENCE names a group that must be in scope AND must have participated.
  Out of scope is a compile error in both tools: a group whose `\)` is unreached
  (`\(\1\)`, ERE `(\1)`), and a group in an EARLIER branch of an alternation the
  reference is not in (`(a)|b\1`, `(a)|(b)\1`) -- the second becomes legal again
  once the alternation closes, so `((a)|b)\2` compiles. Participation is a
  matching rule instead: a group that matched zero times has no text and the
  reference FAILS rather than matching the empty string it has no claim to, so
  `(x)*\1` selects nothing in a line without an `x`, while `(x*)\1` -- a group
  that matched empty on purpose -- matches.

  The gaps this campaign found and did NOT fix, recorded here so the next round
  starts from the probe results rather than re-deriving them. All but the first
  have corpus cases carrying GNU's goldens, listed as xfail -- with the
  exceptions that say so in their own entry, where capturing the behaviour is
  what the case cannot do; `--posix`'s cases
  PASS, and the probe table below is its specification instead. What is missing
  under that flag is MOSTLY a restriction rather than an answer -- but not
  entirely, and the exceptions have now caught this paragraph out TWICE. An
  early draft said the flag never changes an answer here; a later one said
  every answer it does change is modelled. Both were the same mistake, which is
  reaching for a tidy summary of a flag that has no tidy summary. What is true
  as measured: SIX answer-changing rules are modelled. The entry below names
  four; `N` at end of input is the fifth (`sed --posix N` on a one-line file
  prints nothing where the default prints the line); and the sixth is the one
  this paragraph twice named as the exception. `--posix` stops GNU aliasing
  `/dev/stdout` to its own stdout and opens it as an ordinary, separately
  buffered file, so `sed --posix -n 'p;w /dev/stdout'` over three lines prints
  them grouped `1 2 3 1 2 3` rather than interleaved -- which landed with the
  special-file table, and whose case PASSES. So the paragraph has now been
  caught out a THIRD time, by its own example rather than by a new one, and
  the count above is deliberately a count of what the CORPUS asserts rather
  than a claim about the flag. What remains under it is `-u`, which is a gap
  of its own (below) and not a `--posix` rule at all.

  - `--posix`: its REGEX half is implemented, and it splits in two along a
    line that is GNU's rather than this reimplementation's. The flag's OWN
    rules are the GNU operator extensions the compiler drops (`\|`, `\+`,
    `\?`, and the class and anchor escapes) -- `RE_NO_GNU_OPS`, which
    regexp.c:77 sets for POSIXLY_BASIC alone, and `RE_LIMITED_OPS`, which
    additionally wants a BRE (regexp.c:78), so `--posix -E 's/a|b/X/g'` still
    alternates in both tools. Three more
    rules look like the flag's and belong to the LEVEL, so the environment
    variable reaches them too: the unmatched close-paren in both spellings,
    the escape decoding `normalize_regex` stops inside a bracket expression,
    and the out-of-range `\N` on an `s` RHS. Those three are `Options`'
    `unmatched_rparen_ordinary` and `!extended()`, not `posix`; see the
    POSIXLY_CORRECT entry below. Its COMMAND half is
    implemented: `N` at EOF followed the flag from the start -- it is a
    fourth level rule now, and the entry below is where that moved -- and the
    withdrawals now do too -- the eight commands GNU stops knowing (`v`, `Q`,
    `T`, `z`, `F`, `W`, `R`, `e`), the step and `+N`/`~N` addresses, line
    address 0, `l`'s width, `q`'s exit code, the one-line `a text`, a text
    whose backslash ends its `-e` part -- one after the TEXT as much as the
    command's own -- the `I`/`M` modifiers on a regex ADDRESS, which are a
    different parser from the `s` flags of the same names, the `s` flags
    `I`/`i`/`M`/`m`/`e`, and
    a one-address limit on `=`, `a`, `i`, `l` and `r`, for which GNU otherwise
    takes a range (`q`/`Q` are limited in BOTH modes; `c` is not limited at
    all, a range being the point of it). "POSIX's limit" is right for four of
    the five and NOT for `l`, which POSIX.1 defines as `[2addr]l` -- the limit
    there is GNU's posix-mode behaviour rather than the standard's, measured
    from the oracle and not inferred from the spec.
    Almost none of that needed a new message: withdrawing the EXTENSION leaves
    the parser's ordinary complaint, and GNU's wording is the ordinary
    complaint too, which is why `--posix '1~2d'` is `unknown command: `~''
    rather than anything about steps. Its REPLACEMENT
    half is implemented, and existed only because a review asked what the
    two-half split left out -- "regex half" and "command half" sound
    exhaustive and are not, which is a reminder that a tidy taxonomy is a
    claim like any other. It is two rules: `\U`, `\L`, `\u`, `\l` and `\E`
    become the LITERAL letter (`s/a/\Ux/` substitutes `Ux`), and a group
    reference the pattern does not have stops being the error `invalid
    reference \1 on `s' command's RHS` and expands to nothing instead. That
    second one is the only `--posix` rule anywhere that turns a REFUSAL into
    an answer rather than an operator into a character, and nothing but a
    sweep of the replacement would have found it.
    `POSIXLY_CORRECT` selects a POSIX mode in GNU too, but a WEAKER one, and
    td-txt does not drive this flag from it at all; that is its own entry
    below. Both halves of `v` are served: it is refused outright under the
    flag (`unknown command: `v'`), and where it is allowed its documented side
    effect -- clearing posix mode -- reaches both the RUN and the regex
    COMPILER through `Script.v_promoted`, which is why `-e v -e '$!d;N'`
    prints the last pattern space under `POSIXLY_CORRECT` and not under a
    `--posix` given after it.

  - WHEN a script part is compiled relative to the OPTIONS around it. GNU compiles
    each `-e` and `-f` as it scans the command line, so a flag that follows one is
    not in effect for it and a failure inside one outranks anything a later option
    hits. td-txt collects the parts and compiles once, after every option is known.
    Invisible until `w` targets started opening at compile time, and now not:
    `sed -n -e 'w out' --sandbox` writes the file and exits 0 in GNU because sandbox
    mode was not set when that `-e` compiled, where td-txt refuses it; `-e 'w
    /nodir/o' --badopt` is GNU's exit 4 and td-txt's exit 1; and `-e 'w out' -f
    /nosuch` creates `out` in GNU before failing on the script file. The same rule
    scopes `-E`, which is why `sed -e 's/a+/X/p' -E` does not apply the flag to that
    expression. Two sharper witnesses than any of those: `-e 'w /nodir/o' -f
    /nosuch` is exit 4 in BOTH, naming a different file in each, which is the
    status-identical message-divergent pair this corpus normally pins; and where a
    `w` target names the `-f` SCRIPT, `sed -n -e 'w s.sed' -f s.sed` truncates the
    script before reading it, so GNU runs an empty program and prints nothing while
    td-txt reads the `p` and prints -- the axis reaches STDOUT, not just files,
    which the other witnesses make it easy to assume otherwise. Two xfail cases,
    that one and the `--sandbox` one. Pre-existing -- the parent commit's binary
    answers identically -- and closing it means compiling per part rather than per
    run, which changes what every mode flag applies to and so is its own increment,
    not a corner of this one.

  - `R /dev/stdout` and `R /dev/stderr` HANG when the named stream is a pipe --
    RECORDED BUT NOT REPRODUCING, so read the rest of this entry as history
    rather than as current behaviour. As written: GNU is `read error on stdout:
    Bad file descriptor` and exit 4 where td-txt hangs, and with that stream on a
    regular file td-txt exits 0 and GNU still exits 4. A hang rather than a wrong
    answer, so it was recorded here even though no case can carry it -- the
    corpus would only see its 10s timeout. The sweep below reproduced none of it
    on either build, so until it is reproduced the claim above -- the
    NUL-truncation one included -- stands unverified. Pre-existing and a
    different axis than the `w` work above; a reviewer's cross-product found it
    here. The NUL-truncation landing WIDENS what reaches it without changing it:
    `R /dev/stdout\0X` used to be a name nothing could open, so it passed
    through, and it now truncates to the name above and hangs with it. Every
    spelling that truncates to one of these two is this entry.

    Two things measured while `R`'s open moved to compile time, neither of them a
    fix, and between them the reason the claim above is marked unverified. The
    entry below predicted that the move would change WHEN this hangs rather than
    whether; it changes neither, because a special name is exactly
    the kind of source the compile does NOT open, and under `POSIXLY_CORRECT`,
    where the name IS opened for real, the open succeeds at once and it is the
    read that blocks. And the sweep that established that -- both names, stdout a
    pipe, a regular file and `/dev/null`, with and without `POSIXLY_CORRECT`, 12
    arrangements against GNU, the parent binary and this one -- found all three
    AGREEING in every one of them, with no hang anywhere. So the divergence this
    entry opens with did not reproduce, on either build. That is not a claim it
    was never there; it is a note that it needs re-establishing before it can be
    closed, which is its own increment and not this one's.

  - An `r` of the file a `w` in the SAME run is writing, CLOSED -- by doing the
    thing this entry said was worse than the divergence. GNU's `w` output is
    stdio-buffered, so `sed -n -e 'w out' -e 'r out'` appended nothing while
    td-txt, writing through, appended the line it had just written. The reason
    given for leaving it was that the answer is a function of how many bytes have
    piled up rather than a rule: raise the volume past the buffer and GNU's `r`
    starts seeing them. That much is true, and it is the SPECIFICATION now rather
    than the objection -- the pile is 4096 bytes, and the rule is that the buffer
    fills to CAPACITY before writing anything, which is the half a `BufWriter`
    gets wrong. Both numbers are MEASURED rather than read off a header: across
    eight volumes the amount GNU leaves visible is 0, 0, 4096, 4096, 4096, 8192,
    8192, 16384 for 1000 through 20000 bytes written, where a `BufWriter` of the
    same capacity answers 4000/4000/4000/8000/8000/16000 from the third row on.
    Six of the eight rows are therefore discriminators, and
    `a_w_target_flushes_on_the_buffer_boundary` holds all eight (outside the
    corpus, whose files are written inline and whose smallest interesting one
    would be 4100 bytes). The two xfails this entry carried are promoted.

    "Fills to capacity" is still not quite the rule, and the difference is an
    EXACT fill. stdio OVERFLOWS rather than tops up: a full buffer is not a
    reason to write, having more to put and nowhere to put it is. So `w` of one
    4096-byte record shows an `r` NOTHING, where flushing at capacity shows it
    all 4096 -- and every one of the eight rows above crosses the boundary
    mid-line, so not one of them can tell those apart. Four more rows do:
    one record of 4096 and of 4095 are both 0, one of 4097 is 4096, and two of
    4096 are 4096 rather than 8192. Found by a reviewer's randomised differential
    over record lengths, which is the shape a table of round volumes misses.

    `R` had the same face and it got SHARPER when `R` began streaming, so it was
    named here rather than left as an `r` story: a source read whole at its first
    use saw the `w` target as it was THEN -- usually empty -- and never looked
    again, where one held open sees what the run has written to it since, and
    `-s` restarts it besides. `sed -s -n -e 'R wf' -e 'w wf' f1 f2` was nothing
    in GNU and `a` here; it is nothing in both now. It was never `R` reading
    wrongly, so buffering `w` closed it without `R` being touched. What that
    shape diverged on past the buffer was `R`'s OPEN TIME -- a different cause
    with the same face, which is the entry below and is closed too.

  - An `R` SOURCE IS OPENED AT COMPILE TIME, CLOSED -- td-txt opens it there too
    now, so both of sed's file lists are the compile's and SCRIPT ORDER is the
    whole of the answer. GNU compiles `R` the way it compiles `w` --
    `get_openfile (&file_read, read_mode, false)`, compile.c:1217 -- and the
    `false` is the entry: unlike a `w` target, a source that will not open is not
    an error, so `R` of a file that does not exist YET leaves a null `fp` and is
    dead for the rest of the run. `rewind_read_files` (compile.c:1641) skips a
    null `fp`, so not even `-s` revives it. `sed -s -u -e 'R wf' -e 'w wf' h h2`
    over `1\n2\n` and `3\n4\n` was `1 2 3 4` in GNU and `1 2 3 1 4 2` here; it is
    `1 2 3 4` in both. Compile the `w` FIRST and it is `1 1 2 2 3 1 4 2` in both,
    which it already was and is the case that stops the fix being "R is always
    dead".

    The open is a HANDLE and not a copy of the bytes, and that half needs its own
    case or a compile that SLURPED would pass: `-e 'R pre' -e 'w pre'` over an
    EXISTING `pre` reads `1 2 3 1 4 2`, the `w` having truncated the very inode
    the source is holding open and the source going on to serve what the `w`
    flushes into it. Four more cases pin that the rule is about the compile and
    not about `-e` counting -- the same pair inside one `-f` script, a `W`, an
    `s///w` flag, and `-e 'R wf' -e 'w wf' -e 'R wf'`, where the SECOND `R` is
    deduped against the first at compile and so stays dead even though the `w`
    between them has created the file. That last one is the case the dedup needs:
    without it the second `R` opens for real and the pair goes live, which is a
    mutant the other three all survive.

    Two consequences, neither of them corpus-shaped. A source that will not open
    is spent for the run and `-s` cannot revive it -- unchanged, since the failed
    open was cached before this as well; what moved is WHEN the one attempt is
    made. And an `R` whose source BLOCKS now blocks the COMPILE: over a fifo
    with no writer, `sed -e 'R fifo' -e ZZZ` reported the bad command before and
    hangs now, which is what GNU does -- measured, 124 from a 3s timeout for GNU
    and for this build, exit 1 for the parent. That is the price of the rule, and
    it is the one place this landing makes td-txt worse at reporting a script
    error. Script order decides that WITHIN the compile and no further: a later
    `-f` whose READ fails still beats the open here and does not in GNU, which is
    the compile-per-part entry above and is bounded to exactly that case --
    `-e 'R fifo' -f /nonexistent` hangs in GNU and is `couldn't open file
    /nonexistent` here, while the same pair with a READABLE `-f` hangs in both
    and an earlier script error stops both. None of this moves the
    `R /dev/stdout` hang above, which the entry there now records.

    Opening at compile also means a script can exhaust the DESCRIPTOR TABLE
    before the run starts, and GNU is exhausted by it in the same way: with 100
    distinct `R` sources under `ulimit -n 64`, `sed -n -e '<100 R lines>' -e p
    in` is `can't read in: Too many open files` and exit 2 in GNU and here
    alike, where the parent -- opening on first use, so the operand was already
    open -- printed 60 lines and exited 0. Two notes on that. The stdout
    descriptor is now taken BEFORE the compile, because otherwise the sources
    take the last one and the failure is a `write error` on a stdout that never
    opened, which is worse than GNU rather than equal to it; the RESULT is
    carried to where it was checked before, so a script error still beats it.
    And the `-f` form still differs by exactly ONE descriptor -- GNU prints 61
    lines there and this refuses -- because GNU holds the script's own
    descriptor open across the compile and frees it afterwards, where td-txt
    reads a `-f` script whole before compiling anything. That is the
    compile-per-part difference already recorded above, seen from the resource
    side, and not a separate gap.

  - An `r` SOURCE read whole, CLOSED, and it was a MEMORY divergence rather than
    a behavioural one. `r` dumps the entire file by definition, so neither
    program returns from `r /dev/zero` and the bytes always agreed wherever the
    source ended -- but GNU streams WHILE it dumps, through a fixed buffer, where
    td-txt held the whole source in memory first. `sed -n -e 'r BIG' -e p A` over
    a 600 MB source under a 700 MB address-space limit was `read error on BIG:
    out of memory`, exit 4, against GNU's exit 0; and over `/dev/zero` GNU wrote
    bytes for ever while td-txt wrote none and grew. `copy_source` now copies to
    the sink a block at a time, and to the STDOUT sink the two agree: exit 0 at
    600 MB, and bytes out of `/dev/zero` in either.

    Under `-i` they still do not, because the sink is a BUFFER holding the whole
    edited file rather than GNU's temporary file, which the source's size is now
    the largest contributor to: the same 600 MB under the same limit is GNU exit
    0 and `write error: out of memory`, exit 4, here. That is the `-i` design
    rather than this one, and it is a diagnosed error rather than the ABORT the
    first copy gave, which is what `try_reserve` in the buffer sink is for --
    reported by two reviewers of that landing, since holding what you read is
    exactly the thing it set out to stop doing and `-i` was where it survived.

    Three things follow that are worth having written down. The bytes read
    BEFORE a failed read are now WRITTEN rather than discarded, which is what
    copying as you read means and what GNU does -- unreachable in practice,
    since the failure a source can actually be made to give (a directory) comes
    on the first read. A CLOSED reader ends the dump, which is the only end an
    endless one has: `Out` swallows the EPIPE and latches instead, so `r
    /dev/zero | head` needs the copy to ASK, where GNU is simply killed by
    SIGPIPE. And it is a different fix from the `R` entry above even though the
    entries read alike: `r` wants the source COPIED to the sink, `R` wants one
    line handed over per cycle and the handle KEPT between them.

  - A sed CYCLE that never noticed the reader had gone away, CLOSED as far as
    the HANG goes, which was the whole of the cost. `Out` turns EPIPE into a
    latched flag rather than a failure, and nothing on the ordinary output path
    read that flag, so sed went on reading its input and writing bytes nothing
    could receive: `sed -z -n p /dev/zero | head -c 8` never returned.

    The question is asked in THREE places, and the first alone was not enough.
    BETWEEN cycles, before the read -- so `give_back` still owes the next
    reader an accurate stdin position, which is why before the read rather
    than after the write. INSIDE the cycle, at the top of the command
    dispatch, because a script can loop without ever ending its cycle and a
    check between cycles is then one nothing reaches: `:a;p;ba`,
    `:a;s/x/xy/;p;ta` and `p;s/^/z\n/;D` each still ran forever with only the
    outer one, `b`, `t` and `D` being the three doors back into a cycle. And
    in `copy_source`, which asked already, a dump being unbounded within one
    cycle too. Nine shapes were measured hanging and all nine now exit at
    once: `-n p`, `=`, `-n l` and an extended `w /dev/stdout` over an endless
    source, plus five loops -- those three and `:a;p;n;ba` and `:a;p;N;ba`,
    which go round through `b` as well but consume input as they spin rather
    than only writing. A `--posix w /dev/stdout` never hung, that target being
    an ordinary file whose EPIPE is reported rather than latched.

    Stopping is recorded as a QUIT rather than as this file's end, because to
    the caller those are the same answer and must not be. Under `-s` an end
    means OPEN THE NEXT OPERAND, so a broken sink went on to report a missing
    one -- `sed -s -n p big /nonexistent` exiting 2 with a diagnostic, where
    the run is otherwise the silent 0 below -- and blocked forever in
    `open(2)` on a FIFO nobody writes. That is the argument `copy_source`
    already made about opening a FIFO after the reader has gone, one function
    away and not applied to the operand loop. `q`'s machinery already means
    end the whole run, and 0 is its status.

    A cycle that stops mid-dispatch abandons the commands after the write that
    failed, so the record `p` broke the pipe on never reaches a later `w`.
    That is GNU's behaviour rather than a cost of this one -- it dies at that
    same write -- and it makes the input SWALLOWED exactly one record more
    than the output records, which is what the test pins.

    What is NOT closed is the STATUS, and the reference for it is not the GNU
    one might expect. GNU dies of SIGPIPE; td-txt cannot, the Rust runtime
    having ignored that signal before `main`, and restoring the disposition
    needs `rt_sigaction` -- an `unsafe` surface this crate does not have and
    that UNSAFE.md would have to gain. The honest comparison is therefore GNU
    UNDER THE SAME DISPOSITION, and it was measured: `sed` exits 4 with
    `couldn't write 1 item to stdout: Broken pipe`, and `grep` exits 2 with
    `write error: Broken pipe`. td-txt exits 0 silently in both, which is what
    grep already did and what sed now matches. Neither answer is free -- 0 is
    a lie a `pipefail` shell can catch, while 4-with-a-diagnostic is noise on
    every `sed | head` where GNU prints nothing -- and choosing between them
    is a reviewed decision rather than a measurement, so the two are recorded
    here and the quieter one is what ships.

    The second half is smaller and remains: a run that does not DIE goes on
    doing everything else it was told to in the cycle that broke the pipe.
    `sed -n -e 'r /dev/zero' -e 'w OUT' f1` leaves `OUT` empty under GNU --
    which never reached the second command, and lost its buffers to the signal
    besides -- against all three lines before this and ONE line now. That last
    line is the cycle in which the write failed, and removing it means dying
    where GNU dies rather than asking one question later.

  - A `\c` with nothing to control eats an `a` text's own TERMINATOR: GNU
    accumulates the text WITH its trailing newline and decodes that whole
    buffer, so `a\<newline>\c` appends `J` -- the newline upper-cased and XORed
    with 0x40 -- and no newline after it. CLOSED, by giving the text that
    terminator here too: `parse_text` ends the buffer with a newline before
    `normalize_buffer` sees it, where GNU's `read_text` puts it
    (compile.c:877).

    What made this look larger than it is was the `-z` asymmetry it seemed to
    put back in play (`i foo` ends in a NUL where `a foo` ends in a newline),
    and the answer is that the asymmetry belongs to the WRITERS rather than to
    the parser. All three commands compile identically -- `sed --debug` prints
    `a\J` and `i\J` alike -- and `a` dumps the buffer verbatim from the append
    queue where `i`/`c` write `text_length - 1` bytes and supply the record
    separator themselves (execute.c:1444). So `a` keeps a literal newline
    under `-z` because the newline is IN the text, `i`/`c` end with the NUL
    because they put the separator where the text's last byte was, and that
    same rule is why only `a` ever shows the `J`.

    The neighbouring shapes were never gaps and are untouched. A text ending
    in an unpaired backslash is emitted RAW because GNU returns from
    `read_text` on that path without normalizing at all -- it adds the newline
    and returns (compile.c:870), which is what td-txt's `undecoded` arm
    models, so the terminator joins that buffer too and nothing else about it
    changes. A continuation still collapses only across an `-e` boundary.

  - A backreference to a group under a repetition whose BODY can match empty,
    which POSIX leaves undefined and where GNU's answers do not follow a rule.
    Participation itself is modelled -- the body runs once so `^(x*)*\1$` and
    `^(x*)?\1$` select an empty line while `^(x)*\1$` does not -- but GNU splits
    the family in a way nothing here predicts: `*`, `?`, `{0,1}` and `{1}`
    participate, and `+`, `{0,2}`, `{1,2}` and `{2}` do not, so GNU selects an
    empty line for `^(x*)*\1$` and refuses it for `^(x*)+\1$`. Over the 81
    body/quantifier/subject shapes probed, main answered 22 wrongly and this
    landing answers 15, regressing none of them; the remaining 15 are that split.
    Encoding it would be a lookup table keyed on the bounds with no principle
    behind it, so what is implemented is the engine rule instead: an empty
    iteration runs once, to record participation, and never twice.

  - A file holding a NUL under `-c` and `-v`, where GNU zaps the NUL to a
    record end and so counts more lines than td-txt did: `printf 'a\0b\0aa\0'`
    was `2` in GNU and `1` here. CLOSED, and it turned out to be the same
    entry as the `-x` one below rather than a different question about the
    same byte -- see the record-terminator entry there for the mechanism,
    which this one had already named, and for its scope.

  - GNU's extra EMPTY span under `-o` with `-x` and `-w` together: `grep -o -w
    -x a` over `a/ab` is `a` and an empty line in GNU and just `a` here.
    CLOSED, and it was never an extra SPAN: GNU's `start_ptr` shortcut is
    guarded by `!match_words` (dfasearch.c:512), so with both flags set it
    falls through to the `match_lines` arm, whose length is `end - ptr` --
    measured PAST the record separator. `-o` prints that byte and then adds
    its own, which is the blank line. `-z` is what shows it is the record
    separator rather than a newline: the extra byte is a NUL there.

    The byte follows GNU's MATCHER and not its flags, which is the half that
    makes it more than a curiosity. `Fexecute` measures the same span without
    the separator (kwsearch.c:152-156), and grep.c:2953-2972 switches between
    the two under the run's feet: a single-pattern `-F -w` run moves TO the
    regex matcher, and a multi-pattern `-E`/`-G` run whose patterns are all
    literal moves AWAY from it. So `grep -w -x -o -e ab` prints the byte while
    `-e ab -e cd` prints it for neither line, under every syntax, and one
    metacharacter anywhere in the set brings it back. td-txt does not switch
    matchers -- it has no reason to prefer either -- but it has to model the
    CHOICE, since this byte is observable through it. The count is taken after
    GNU drops duplicate patterns (grep.c:182-201), so `-e ab -e ab` is one.

    "No reason to prefer either" was too strong, and a review of the
    stray-backslash lint found the second observable: a set GNU converts to
    fixed strings is never REGEX-PARSED, so a pattern that is not a valid
    regex still works. `printf '\\\n' | grep -e a -e '\'` prints the
    backslash and exits 0 under GNU -- `try_fgrep_pattern`'s escape switch is
    guarded by `if (1 < len)`, so a lone trailing backslash is copied as a
    literal -- where td-txt compiles it and refuses with `Trailing backslash`
    at exit 2. STILL open. It is one pattern short of the `-o -w` entry below
    in what it wants: the matcher CHOICE is modelled, and what is missing is
    letting that choice pick a different COMPILE. The lint is gated on the
    same choice and is correct, since GNU's silence there follows from the
    same never-parsing.

  - `-o` with `-w` over a pattern LIST, which is STILL open and now xfailed.
    GNU joins the list with newlines and compiles it as ONE regex when nothing
    holds a backreference (dfasearch.c:236-295), so its retry loop shrinks the
    union's span; td-txt runs the loop per pattern and takes the
    leftmost-longest answer. A start the union cannot shrink into a word can
    still be word-bounded for one pattern alone, so td-txt prints spans GNU
    passes. Selection agrees, the wrapped dfa over the union asking what the
    per-pattern scan asks. Closing it wants the pattern list compiled as one
    regex, which reaches well past `-w`.

  - A huge `-B`/`-C`/`-NUM` count costs GNU TIME, not memory, and past a point it
    stops finishing at all. Its output path walks back the before-count in a
    loop that runs to the full count however little input there is, so the
    cost is linear in the NUMBER rather than in the file: `-B 100000000` takes
    119ms, `-B 200000000` 204ms, `-B 999999999` 1024ms, and
    `-B 99999999999999999999` was still going at 20 seconds. It is not an
    allocation -- under a 200 MiB address-space cap `-B 999999999` still
    completes in 1027ms. `-A`, which only counts down, is 2ms at any count.
    (A careless measurement sees none of this: GNU treats a `/dev/null`
    stdout as `-q`, so the same command is 6ms there.) td-txt indexes input
    it has already read, so every count costs the same 4ms, and what it prints
    is what GNU prints for every count GNU finishes. Deliberate: a wait with
    no end is not a behaviour to reproduce, and this is the answer GNU is
    computing.

  - A missing OPTION-ARGUMENT prints no usage line after the diagnostic for
    `-m`/`-A`/`-B`/`-C`, where `-e`, `-f`, an unknown short option and every
    long-option error all print one and GNU prints one for all of them. So
    `grep -m` is one line here and three in GNU. Pre-existing, and td-txt is
    inconsistent with itself rather than only with GNU. Invisible to the
    sweeps, whose classifier strips each side's usage pointer before
    comparing.

  - `POSIXLY_CORRECT` selects a POSIX MODE in GNU sed, on top of the option
    order it decides, and that mode is now SERVED but for one pre-existing
    family named at the end of this entry -- without td-txt gaining a second
    flag, and without the variable being wired to `--posix`. The shape
    of the answer is GNU's rather than this reimplementation's. `posixicity`
    is THREE-valued (sed.h:86-90): `POSIXLY_EXTENDED` by default,
    `POSIXLY_CORRECT` under the variable, `POSIXLY_BASIC` under `--posix`.
    Every behaviour gated on THAT is written `== POSIXLY_EXTENDED` or its
    negation, so each is one both LOWER levels SHARE, and a level the variable
    already sets reaches all of them. Six are:

      `N` at end of input (execute.c:1478) -- the RUN reads the level, which
      `Sed.extended` carries and `Mode::extended_with_v` computes.
      `/dev/stdout` special-file resolution (compile.c:402) -- per PART, on
      the same `Mode.extended`; this one was right before the rest.
      The unmatched close-paren, both spellings (regexp.c:70) -- `Options`'
      `unmatched_rparen_ordinary`, split OUT of `posix` for exactly this.
      The escape decoding inside a bracket expression (compile.c:1473) --
      `normalize_regex`, which takes the level rather than the flag.
      The out-of-range `\N` on an `s` RHS (regexp.c:122).
      `v`, which ASSIGNS the level mid-compile (compile.c:1079) rather than
      reading it -- `Script.v_promoted`.

    A SEVENTH rule answers to the variable and is not on that list, because it
    does not read `posixicity` at all: `dfawarn` asks
    `getenv("POSIXLY_CORRECT")` directly (regexp.c:52), so the confusing-bracket
    lint -- `[:alpha:]` written without its outer bracket, GNU's one bare exit-4
    pattern error -- is DISCARDED whenever the variable is present, at any
    level, `--posix` included, and a `v` cannot bring it back. Served, and by
    the switch GNU uses rather than by the nearest one to hand: the variable's
    PRESENCE, read once per run and carried to the compiler by `Invocation` --
    the struct for the compile inputs that are properties of the run rather
    than of an `-e` part, and so cannot go in `Mode`. WHERE sed then raises the
    lint is a separate question and the entry below answers it; `Options`'
    `confusing_bracket_ok` is what tells the ENGINE not to, and is a per-applet
    constant rather than a reading of the variable. Only the DIAGNOSTIC is
    conditional; `class_syntax` is a flag the
    bracket parser raises and the error is built from it afterwards, so the
    pattern parses identically either way and accepting one costs nothing but
    the refusal. FIVE cases, four of them saying which switch it is: `--posix`
    alone still lints, the variable suppresses even under `--posix`, an EMPTY
    value suppresses (`getenv` answering non-NULL, as the option-permutation
    half already has it), and the correctly-spelled class lints nowhere. Two
    more in grep-cli.test.txt say it is SED's: grep sets
    `DFA_CONFUSING_BRACKETS_ERROR`, so dfa.c:1142 takes the `dfaerror` arm
    there and the variable cannot reach it. Those two exist because the wrong
    fix is the obvious one -- reusing sed's switch in grep -- and it left the
    whole corpus green until they landed.
    Recording the METHOD beside it, because it is the reason this was missed
    rather than found: the roster above came from grepping `posixicity`, and
    the complete net is `grep POSIXLY_CORRECT sed/*.c` -- exactly two `getenv`
    sites, sed.c:245 for the level and regexp.c:52 for this. A roster built
    from the wrong grep looks finished. `dfawarn` has SIX callers in
    `lib/dfa.c` and only this one can fire in sed, which is a GUARANTEE and
    not an observation: `regexp.c:130` builds `dfaopts` as `0` or `DFA_EOL_NUL`
    and nothing else, so `DFA_STAR_WARN`, `DFA_PLUS_WARN` and
    `DFA_STRAY_BACKSLASH_WARN` are never set, and the five other sites are each
    guarded by one of them. An earlier draft here said instead that sed's own
    compiler refuses those patterns first, at every level. That is false as
    well as weaker: `sed 's/*a/X/'` is exit 0 in both tools, a BRE taking the
    leading `*` as a literal, and only `-E` refuses. A fix scoped from the call
    count would have been five rules too wide.

    What is NOT shared stays with the flag: `RE_NO_GNU_OPS` (regexp.c:77) and
    `RE_LIMITED_OPS` are POSIXLY_BASIC's alone, so `\w`, `\<`, the BRE `\|`,
    `\+` and `\?`, the replacement's case operators, the `I`/`M` modifiers
    and every command withdrawal are `--posix` and untouched by the variable.
    That is the whole reason the two are separate fields rather than one:
    the split runs THROUGH the regex compiler, not between it and the rest.

    Measured after, in two rounds, and the second round is the interesting
    one. Over 990 script/input/level runs `--posix` diverges from GNU 0 times
    and the variable 5 -- and those 5 are the SAME five the DEFAULT level
    shows, the unimplemented `e` command, so none of them is about
    posixicity. That looked like a closed gap and was not, TWICE over, and
    both times because the sweep had no script of the shape. One reviewer
    found the bracket carry: a second sweep of 330 bracket-family runs shows
    both lower levels diverging on three scripts -- the `[::]`, `[..]` and
    `[==]` closers followed by a bracket. That sweep was read at the time as
    BOUNDING the family and did not: an exhaustive enumeration later found
    `[:a]` behaving identically, so it was a third sample being mistaken for a
    proof. See the `--posix` bracket entry below. Another found `dfawarn`
    above, which no sweep here had a `[:alpha:]` shape for at all. Both are
    served now. The variable INHERITED the bracket gap by being routed through
    the same decoder, so it was one defect with two spellings rather than a new
    one, and it was carried as an xfail under each until the decoder's own
    bracket walk became GNU's. The lesson is the sweep's rather than the code's,
    and is worth more than either finding: a divergence a sweep has no script
    for reads exactly like a closure. Neither gap is a REGRESSION -- the
    parent binary answers both identically -- so what the reviews caught was a
    CLAIM, which is the failure mode this file exists to prevent.
    Twenty-one corpus cases pin what IS served, five of them guards that
    assert the variable leaves the flag's own rules alone; eight of the
    sixteen in the first block are red on the parent commit and eight pass on
    both, which is the split a guard is supposed to have.

    Two things this entry got WRONG are worth keeping, because both were
    tidy-sounding and neither survived measurement. It prescribed a SECOND
    FLAG for the behaviours both spellings share; there is no second flag in
    GNU, and asking the LEVEL the question GNU asks is what served them with
    no new switch. And it recorded, correctly, that driving `--posix` from the
    variable is the wrong shortcut -- across 200 script/input probes the two
    spellings DISAGREE on 76, the commonest shape being `POSIXLY_CORRECT`
    behaving like the default while `--posix` refuses a GNU extension outright
    (`1~2d`, `0~2p`, `l 5`, `v`) -- but it then read that as a reason the
    variable could not be served at all. It was a reason one FLAG could not
    serve both, which is a different claim. `--posix` is the strictly stronger
    switch; wiring one flag to both would make td-txt refuse, under a variable
    build systems routinely set, scripts GNU accepts. Its own counter-example
    still stands and is now a passing case: GNU's `v` CLEARS posix mode, so
    `POSIXLY_CORRECT=1 sed 'v;$!d;N'` prints `a` in GNU, and a td-txt with the
    two wired together printed nothing.
    The wiring measurement itself, as of the landing that took it: over 972
    argv it FIXED 99 and BROKE 48, all 48 breaks containing `v`. Both numbers
    are stale now and deliberately not re-measured -- the thing they were
    measuring is a shortcut nobody should take, and what replaced it fixes the
    99 without the 48.

  - WHICH compile diagnostic wins when two apply, which is a rule about GNU's
    ORDER rather than about either error. `compile_regex_1` does three things
    in a fixed sequence (sed/regexp.c:96-133): `re_compile_pattern`, then the
    `s` command's out-of-range `\N` check, then `dfacomp` -- and `dfacomp` is
    what raises the confusing-bracket lint. So a pattern carrying BOTH faults
    is `invalid reference \1` at exit 1, not the lint at exit 4:
    `sed 's/[:alpha:]/\1/'` is the minimal shape. td-txt raised the lint
    inside its single compile and answered exit 4, which is a different STATUS
    and not merely a different message. `add_regex_pending` returns a
    `#[must_use]` `PendingLint` the `s` path holds across its reference check;
    every other caller has nothing to run in between and raises at once, which
    is where GNU raises it too. `s` is the only command that can have anything
    in between, being the only one with a replacement to read a `\N` from.

    Found by a 1500-script randomized fuzz across the three posixicity levels,
    run right after the lint's own commit landed and reporting 3 cases, all at
    the DEFAULT level. Only the default has two diagnostics to order:
    `POSIXLY_CORRECT` suppresses BOTH -- the lint by `dfawarn`'s getenv and the
    reference check by the level -- and `--posix` suppresses the reference
    check alone, leaving the lint with nothing to race. Worth recording
    as the fuzz's first find that no hand-written sweep had reached: every
    sweep in this campaign varied ONE rule at a time, and this needs two faults
    in one pattern. The same fuzz now reports none. Twelve cases pin it, nine
    of them guards: either fault alone, an in-range group, a pattern that will
    not compile at all (GNU's first step, which outranks both), each POSIX
    level, and the OTHER raise site the split creates -- an address, in both
    spellings, which raises at once, plus the variable suppressing one. That
    last group is the coverage the split silently opened: with the lint
    reachable only through `s` in the corpus, a mutant that never raised it on
    the address path left all 2453 cases green.

  - The POSIX levels' bracket suppression carries past a sub-expression that
    never closed, and this WAS an xfail here on the ground that "what state GNU
    carries across the first bracket cannot be read off from the outside". It
    can be read off the SOURCE, which is where it now comes from. The decoder
    keeps its own `bracket_state` (compile.c:1390) -- outside, inside, or the
    byte that opened a `[:`/`[.`/`[=` -- and its `]` arm returns to "inside"
    only when the byte one back IS the opener's kind and the byte two back is
    NOT (compile.c:1493). That is a LOOKBEHIND rather than a state, and it is a
    second machine over the same syntax as the delimiter reader's
    `snarf_char_class`, agreeing with it nowhere in particular. `[::]` fails
    the two-back test, so it never closes and `sed --posix
    's/[::][]\x41]/Z/g'` suppresses the second bracket entirely -- class {], \,
    x, 4, 1} -- where the same `[]\x41]` alone stops at its leading `]`.

    The family is wider than the entry it replaces said, and the correction is
    the useful part. That entry named `[::]`, `[..]` and `[==]` as the whole of
    it, from a 330-run sweep it described as bounding the family rather than
    sampling it. Closing needs the kind one byte back and NOT two, which no
    FOUR-byte `[:X]` can satisfy, whatever X is -- the byte two back from that
    `]` is the opener itself -- so `[:a][]\x41]` suppresses exactly as `[::]`
    does. THREE bytes close (`[:]`, where two back is the `[`) and five close
    (`[:a:]`); only four cannot, and a draft of this entry said "needs five
    bytes" having forgotten the three. An exhaustive enumeration finds all of
    it at once; the sweep that "bounded" it had no script of the shape, which
    is the same lesson the paragraph above draws.

    The lookbehind reads the buffer GNU is COMPACTING IN PLACE rather than the
    original pattern: `normalize_text` reads through `p` and writes through `q`
    into one array (compile.c:1379-1380), so a byte below `q` has already been
    rewritten. That is invisible until a decoded escape makes the cursors
    differ, and at a gap of exactly ONE it puts `p[-1]`'s value at `p[-2]`, so
    the closing test reads `x != k && x == k` and NO sub-expression closes.
    `sed --posix 's/\t[:][]\x41]/Z/g'` is the witness -- `\t` is two bytes
    decoding to one. ONE is the only gap that does it: at zero (`\q`, which
    keeps its backslash) nothing has shifted, and at two (`\x4`) or more
    (`\x41`, which is three) both bytes are still original. td-txt read the
    original text here until a review caught it; the enumeration could not,
    having no escape ahead of the bracket in any of its 39062 scripts.

    The three neighbouring shapes a reviewer once flagged with this
    (`[[:]\x41]`, `[[.]\x41]`, `[[=]\x41]`) are NOT divergences: both tools
    refuse all three identically, down to the `char N:` offset.

  - `v` DISCARDS its own address in GNU, which only the empty regex can show.
    GNU's `case 'v'` ends in `continue` rather than `break` (compile.c:1081),
    so the command slot is never committed and the address goes with it --
    `sed -n '//v'` exits 0 in silence, the `//` never reaching a line. td-txt
    keeps the command, so the empty regex is evaluated and there is no earlier
    one: `no previous regular expression`, exit 1. Every non-empty spelling
    agrees (`/a/v`, `1v`), the address being compiled and then merely unused,
    which is why the shape hides. `v`'s other half of that `continue` --
    setting posixicity back to EXTENDED, so a `v` would turn `--posix` off for
    the rest of a script -- is unreachable in both programs, `--posix`
    withdrawing `v` itself. Found while sweeping for the address-modifier
    landing and outside it.

  - An `R` SOURCE read whole -- the NAMED half now closed, the standard-input
    half not. A named source is read a BLOCK at a time (`open_source`, the same
    4 KiB `Records` the operands take), so it is held open across cycles and
    gives one line per invocation as GNU's `ck_getdelim` does. What that closes
    is measured: `sed -n -e 'R /dev/urandom' -e p A` returns, where reading to
    EOF never did; a 600 MB source runs under a 700 MB address-space limit,
    where the whole read died `read error on BIG: out of memory` at exit 4; and
    a fifo whose writer is still open no longer waits for it. `-s` rewinds by
    seeking the handle to 0 and RESTARTING the reader, since a reader that kept
    its buffer would hand back records from before the seek.

    WHEN a source is opened is a second axis, unchanged by this and recorded
    here because this entry is now the place for it: GNU opens every `R` source
    at COMPILE time, td-txt at the command's first execution. So `sed -n -e
    '999R F' -e p f1` over a fifo nobody writes hangs before GNU reads a line of
    input and returns at once here, and a source that exists at startup but is
    removed before the first `R` still yields its lines in GNU and none here.
    Pre-existing in both builds either side of this landing.

    `R /dev/stdin` was what was left, and it is CLOSED: it is the one source
    that could not simply take a reader of its own. It is the INPUT's own
    descriptor, and two readers over one descriptor cannot each be right about
    a RELATIVE seek -- the operand reader hands back its over-read that way, and
    a second reader taking bytes between those reads sends the seek somewhere
    else entirely. GNU has no such problem because it has no second reader:
    `R /dev/stdin` and the input share ONE stream. So does this now. The run
    holds one reader over descriptor 0, made at most once, and both roles take
    records from it -- an operand naming standard input, and `R`.

    Three faces closed together, which is why they were one landing. The BYTES:
    sharing a cursor means alternate lines, so `printf 'A\nB\nC\n' | sed -n -e
    'R /dev/stdin' -e s/^/P/ -e p` is `PA B PC` in both now, where td-txt read
    each input whole and its `R` found nothing left. That was an xfail with
    GNU's golden and is promoted, with four cases beside it for the shapes the
    one position spans: a file operand then `-`, `-s` (which restarts every
    REWINDABLE `R` source per operand and must not restart this one, since
    standard input cannot be rewound), two `R`s in one cycle, and an `R` before
    a `-` operand.

    The `$` LOOKAHEAD is the one thing that can hold a record of that reader
    back, and it hands it over. `Stream` keeps one record ahead because `$`
    cannot be answered without having looked; GNU's `$` test peeks a CHARACTER
    and ungets it, so nothing is ever kept from `R`. Reading past a held record
    gives `R` the line AFTER the one it is owed and the next cycle the one
    before it -- `sed -n -e '$!p' -e 'R /dev/stdin' -e p` over four lines is
    `o1 o1 o2 o3 o3 o4` in GNU and was `o1 o1 o3 o2 o2 o4` here. Three cases
    cover it, and a record carries WHERE IT CAME FROM so the question can be
    asked at all.

    The TIMING: the source is read a block at a time like any other, so
    `R /dev/stdin` answers from a stream that has not ended. Measured with a
    pipe fed a line every 150ms, GNU and td-txt now both emit all six lines
    within the first second, where td-txt emitted nothing until the writer
    closed.

    And the CONSUMPTION, which was a cost the special-file table paid rather
    than one it found: `R /dev/stdin` used to swallow the operator's standard
    input to end of file. `( sed -n -e 'R /dev/stdin' -e p A; cat ) < EIGHT`
    left `cat` NOTHING, against GNU's lines 3-8; it now leaves exactly 3-8 as
    well. That follows from the accounting rather than from a rule of its own:
    what `R` takes is DELIVERED, as a cycle's record is -- it was printed --
    so the give-back subtracts it and hands back only what the block read
    over-read. It is a descriptor another program owns, which is the shape of
    harm `w /dev/stdin` truncating it was.

    What that leaves is `r /dev/stdin`, the LOWERCASE one, and it is not the
    same question: GNU opens the NAME there rather than sharing the stream, so
    it dumps whatever is left in the descriptor and re-reads a regular file
    from its start every cycle. td-txt does the same and agrees on every shape
    measured. The one cell that used to differ was under `-u`, where GNU leaves
    the descriptor a record on and so has something left to dump: `sed -n -u -e
    'r /dev/stdin' - -` over a PIPE carrying `x\ny` was `y` there and empty
    here. That followed from the read granularity rather than from anything
    about `r`, and the `-u` entry below closing that half closed this with it --
    both are `y` now, and both are empty without the flag.

  - A COMPILE-TIME flag is POSITIONAL, and used not to be here. GNU compiles each
    `-e`/`-f` inside its own getopt loop, so such a flag governs the parts scanned
    after it and no earlier one; td-txt settled every option first and applied
    them all retroactively. THREE flags reach a compile: `--posix` withdraws
    extensions (`sed -n -e '1~2p' --posix h` prints `a c`, where the same flag
    FIRST refuses), `-E`/`-r` picks the regex grammar (`-e 's/a+/X/p' -E` matches
    nothing, the part being a BRE, and `-r` is sharper still -- `-e
    's/(a)/[\1]/p' -r` is `invalid reference \1`), and `--sandbox` refuses
    `e`/`r`/`w` (`-e 'w out' --sandbox` writes the file). They are INDEPENDENT:
    `--posix` does not withdraw an earlier `-E`, nor buy back an extension. A
    bare script OPERAND is not in the class, since GNU compiles it once the scan
    is over, so it takes the final values as run time does; `-n`/`-s`/`-i` are
    run-time and order-independent in both, and `-l` is read when `l` RUNS
    (above), which is this rule seen from the other side. Each part now carries
    the flags it was SCANNED with, keyed on where a COMMAND starts rather than on
    where the parser stands -- by the time an `s///` is read the parser is at the
    command's end, which for a part's last command is the boundary, so `pos`
    would consult the NEXT part's flags. The ONE construct whose parse crosses a
    boundary is `a`/`i`/`c` text, and it asks a different question: GNU refuses a
    dangling one at the end of EVERY part under THAT part's posixicity
    (compile.c:1369), so `-e 'a\' --posix -e 'x\' -e 'y'` is `incomplete
    command` even though the `a` began where the flag was not set.

  - The `v` COMMAND is an input to that same state, and not a flag at all: GNU's
    `case 'v'` sets `posixicity = POSIXLY_EXTENDED` (compile.c:1079) for the rest
    of the compile, across later parts. td-txt does that now, so
    `POSIXLY_CORRECT=1 sed -n -e v -e p -e 'w /dev/stdout' h` aliases in both
    (`1 1 2 2 3 3`) where it did not here before (`1 2 3 1 2 3`). It is not an OR
    onto the part's flags: `v` ASSIGNS a level, and so does `--posix`, which
    assigns GNU's third one -- so a `--posix` scanned after a `v` wins. In the
    other order the question does not arise, `v` being refused outright under the
    flag.

    What a `v` promotes is now every rule GNU gates on the LEVEL, which it was
    not (the variable's seventh rule, `dfawarn`, is a `getenv` and no `v`
    reaches it in GNU either): the level the special-file table reads while it compiles, the one
    the RUN reads at end of input, an unmatched `)` (regexp.c:70/73), `invalid
    reference \N` on an `s` RHS (regexp.c:122), and a regex's escape handling
    inside a bracket (compile.c:1473). Every one is written
    `== POSIXLY_EXTENDED` or its negation, so BASIC and CORRECT behave ALIKE in
    all of them and `--posix` shows each exactly as the variable does --
    measured, since an earlier draft of this entry claimed the opposite.

    Three of those five were unreachable to promote until the compiler was
    handed the LEVEL, and the reason was td-txt's shape rather than GNU's: they
    were gated on the `posix` FLAG, which no `v` moves. They read
    `Mode::extended` now, through `ScriptParser::extended()`, so a `v` reaches
    all three -- `POSIXLY_CORRECT=1 sed -E -e v -e 's/a)/X/g'` is the refusal
    GNU gives where without the `v` it is a literal paren, and a `v` BETWEEN
    two patterns compiles them at different levels, which the corpus pins.
    (`Mode::extended` IS driven from the variable -- `!conf.posix && !posixly`;
    what td-txt does not drive from it is the `--posix` flag, and the entry
    above is why that distinction is the whole design rather than a quibble.)

  - `-z` LOOKS like a fourth member of that class and is a HYBRID, which is what
    an attempt to add it turned up -- and BOTH halves are served now. GNU keeps
    one `buffer_delimiter` and reads it at two different times: compiling a part
    sets glibc's `newline_anchor` from it (`regexp.c:101`) and builds the
    filtering dfa's end-of-line from it (`regexp.c:130`), while running the
    regex chooses from it the segment split that stands in for that anchor once
    the delimiter is not a newline (`regexp.c:303`). So the flag reaches a part
    it FOLLOWS, but only through those two, and everything else about it is the
    run's. `Mode` carries the part's reading beside `--posix` and `-E`;
    `Invocation` keeps the run's, which is still what a record is.

    THREE rules, and the second and third are why "a late `-z` anchors on both"
    is the wrong summary alone.

    1. `^`/`$` hold at BOTH bytes -- `sed -n -e 's/^x*/</Mgp' -z` marks the
       start of every NUL record AND of every line inside one, where the same
       script with the flag first marks only the records. `newline_anchor` moves
       those two and nothing else, so a substitution's `` \` ``/`\'` stay on the
       segment; an address's are `^`/`$` by another spelling and do move.
    2. A buffer the pattern cannot match under the COMPILE-time reading ALONE
       is no match at all, because the dfa GNU filters with was built from that
       delimiter and knows the newline, not the NUL. So the late flag can match
       LESS than the early one: `N;s/^c/</Mg` over `a\nb\0c` rewrites nothing
       here and rewrites the `c` with the flag first. The filter reads as an
       ADDRESS whatever asked it (a dfa models neither segments nor buffer
       identity), and its verdict covers the whole buffer -- it runs only at
       offset 0 (`regexp.c:280`), so a `g` loop's later searches are not
       filtered. Nothing without a NUL in the PATTERN SPACE reaches it, the two
       readings naming the same positions then, which is why every case joins
       with `N`.
    3. A BACKREFERENCE changes both of those, and not in the same way. GNU
       hands such a pattern to glibc's backref matcher, which sees one segment
       and its true ends, so an address's `` \` ``/`\'` stop moving with `M` --
       the same condition `consumable` already used, not a second rule, and it
       closes a divergence that had nothing to do with `-z`: `N;/\`b\(c\)\1/Mp`
       over `a\nbcc` matched here and not in GNU. Rule 2 still applies, with
       the reference APPROXIMATED: a dfa cannot enforce one, so it reads it as
       a single NULLABLE position under NO constraint (lib/dfa.c:2285,2796),
       and `dfaexec`'s `backref` flag says only that a HIT needs verifying --
       a MISS still returns 0. Both simpler answers are wrong in a direction a
       case pins. Filtering with the reference ENFORCED rejects where GNU
       accepts (`N;s/^\(.\)\1*$/</Mg` over `\na\0b\n` rewrites both bytes
       there); not filtering a backref pattern at all accepts where GNU
       rejects (`N;/^c\(x\)\1/Mp` over `a\nb\0cxx` prints nothing there).
       The filter is also not allowed to FAIL: a step budget exhausted inside
       it is an artefact of filtering with a backtracker where GNU has a
       linear dfa, so it is read as "cannot decide" and lets the buffer
       through rather than becoming an error the pattern itself would not
       raise.

    Two more rules came out of review rather than out of the fuzz, and both
    are about WHICH pattern or WHICH compile the two readings apply to.

    4. The lone-`^`/`$` exception is on the DECODED text. `normalize_text`
       runs before `compile_regex_1` (regexp.c:168-170), so `\x5e`, `\o136`
       and `\d094` are each a one-byte `^` when `sz == 1` is tested, and
       `\x24`/`\o044`/`\d036` a lone `$`. Matching the RAW pattern leaves
       them four or five bytes long and compiles the anchor, which anchors at
       the newline as well -- an ordinary record shows it, no `N` needed.
    5. There is a THIRD read of the delimiter, and it is not about placement.
       An ADDRESS compiles with `needed_sub == 0` (compile.c:927) and so with
       `RE_NO_SUB` (regexp.c:87); the first use that wants REGISTERS -- which
       is only an `s//…/` reusing it -- frees the pattern and the dfa and
       compiles it AGAIN (regexp.c:198-211), at run time. So for that regex
       both halves of `-z` agree however the flag was placed. The rebuild
       MUTATES the object, so it persists: every later use of that address
       reads what the rebuild read. `Sed::recompiled` is the set, since the
       state belongs to the regex rather than to the call. Modelling only the
       substitution half would leave the persistence face wrong, and it is
       measurable -- `sed -n -e 'N;/^b/Mp;s//&/' -z` over two `a\nb\0c\0`
       records prints ONE record in GNU, where reading the part's delimiter
       for the address prints two and this crate printed none before either
       rule existed.

    Method, because the finding is about the METHOD as much as the flag. The
    ordinary `-z` fuzz -- 2000 random scripts over inputs carrying both
    separators, compared on status, stdout AND stderr -- reports NOTHING here,
    on the parent binary as well as this one, so it would not have found any of
    this. It puts `-z` first every time, and even placed late a random 1-5 atom
    pattern almost never both carries an anchor and lands on one of these
    boundaries. A fuzz BIASED at the family -- short bodies likely to match,
    an anchor in every draw, `-z` in all three positions -- reports 353 shapes
    on the parent and 0 here. Every refinement above except the first came from
    it rather than from reading GNU: the filter, its address reading, and both
    backreference halves were each a shape it reported after the previous fix.
    The corpus's own `-z`+`M` cases had the same blind spot in the other
    direction (see the `N` note at the top of this file), which is twice now
    that this family has hidden behind a sweep that had no script of the right
    shape. THREE times, counting rules 4 and 5: both live outside that
    generator's alphabet -- it draws no `\x5e`-style spelling of an anchor and
    no `s//` at all -- and a cross-model review found them where 12,000 fuzzed
    shapes did not. The alphabet has since been widened to carry both, so the
    guard is real rather than retrospective.

  - STDIN is left where sed stopped READING, which used to be drained here and
    now holds for a REGULAR FILE and for a PIPE alike -- by two different
    mechanisms, a seek back and a read that never took the bytes in the first
    place, both described below. `{ sed -n 1q; cat; } <
    four` hands `l2 l3 l4` to the `cat` in both now; `2q` leaves `l3 l4`, so it
    is exactly "after the last RECORD consumed", and `Q` behaves as `q` does. It
    is what makes reading a header with sed and handing the rest to another
    program work at all. Only an EARLY EXIT shows it -- a script that runs to
    EOF ends at the same offset either way -- and it is not a `-u` gap: it holds
    with and without the flag. An operand NAMING A FILE never diverged, for a
    reason that is not about reading at all: sed never touches stdin, so nothing
    moves. `-` is not one of those, being stdin spelled out, and behaves exactly
    as the redirect does; `/dev/stdin` as an operand also agrees, since the
    table opens the stream and the reader never gets it.

    What closed it is the trick td-sh uses for `sh < script`, with its caveats
    (`stdin_raw` in td-sh/src/process.rs is the dup, line.rs the seek-back, and
    UNSAFE.md's `poll(2)` section the write-up): a safe `dup` of fd 0 --
    `stdin().as_fd().try_clone_to_owned()`, which SHARES the file description,
    so seeking the clone moves fd 0 -- then a seek back by what was read and not
    consumed, counted in BYTES, which is what td-txt holds anyway. Regular
    rather than merely seekable, since a descriptor can answer a position query
    and still refuse the negative seek. That leaves a BLOCK DEVICE out, where
    GNU would restore, and the reason there is only conservatism rather than
    the one above -- a block device takes the seek quite happily. Nothing on
    td's paths puts one on sed's stdin, and widening the test to `!is_dir() &&
    seekable` is a change with its own cases rather than a wording fix.

    There is exactly ONE such dup, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy.
    The reader holds one for the whole run (it is how the block size reaches
    the kernel, below), so a give-back that made a SECOND one had a failure the
    single-dup version does not: with `RLIMIT_NOFILE` at 4, the second dup fails,
    the seek is skipped, and `sed -n 1q` leaves a regular stdin at 4096 instead
    of 8 -- measured, against GNU's 8. So the seek goes through the descriptor
    the reader already holds. And when THAT dup is the one that fails, the
    fallback reads stdin through `std::io::Stdin` instead, whose 8 KiB buffer
    takes more off the descriptor than the reader counts: there is no honest
    number to seek by, so no give-back is attempted at all, which is what
    happened before any of this existed.

    A PIPE now agrees, and it took the other fix rather than a seek: sed reads
    each operand a BLOCK at a time (`Stream::BLOCK`, 4 KiB), so what a `q`
    does not consume simply stays in the pipe, where a seek has nowhere to put
    it. Measured against GNU 4.9 with `sed 1q`: 4104 bytes in leaves exactly 8,
    8192 leaves 4096, and 20000 leaves 15904 -- td-txt matching on every one,
    where the whole-file reader left 0 in all three. `-s` and `-i` open each
    operand themselves rather than through the operand list, and they read
    through the same reader, so they leave the same 15904 and `sed -s 1q
    /dev/urandom` returns as the plain form does.

    The BLOCK SIZE is what makes those numbers agree, and it is deliberately not
    a promise. 4 KiB is GNU's, so the leftovers line up; a different size would
    leave a different amount and be equally correct. Same answer grep's landing
    reached for its 96 KiB, and for the same reason -- the RULE is reproduced,
    the arithmetic is not owed.

    One trap is worth recording because it cost a measurement. Reading fd 0
    through `std::io::Stdin` does NOT give the block size to the kernel: that
    type is a `BufReader` with an 8 KiB buffer, so a 4 KiB request takes 8 KiB
    off the descriptor and `sed 1q` over 20000 bytes left 11808 instead of
    15904 -- exactly one block too few. Stdin is therefore read through a
    DUPLICATED raw descriptor (`util::stdin_unbuffered`), which shares the file
    description so the position still moves, and what a pipe keeps is td-txt's
    own decision rather than std's.

    That GNU does BOTH -- over-read a block AND leave precisely the bytes after
    record 1 on a seekable descriptor -- is what a 10,000-byte first record
    shows, so "after the last RECORD consumed" is literal rather than an
    artifact of block size. td-txt now does both too: the seek half was already
    landed, and this is the read half.

    `R /dev/stdin` was NOT closed by this and was a separate reader: an earlier
    draft of this entry said one fix would close both, and that was wrong --
    `( sed -n -e 'R /dev/stdin' -e p A; cat ) < EIGHT` left `e3`..`e8` in GNU
    and nothing here, because `R` read its source through the `rfiles` table
    rather than through the input stream this landing accounts for. It wanted
    the same reader, which is a later landing and closed it: `R /dev/stdin` is
    served from the run's one descriptor-0 reader now, so what it takes counts
    as delivered and the seek above is right about it.

    No CASE can show any of this, for a reason that is not about pipes: the
    harness runs ONE child and captures its output, so nothing else ever reads
    that descriptor and there is no second reader to be short-changed. The test
    is therefore a Rust one that hands the child a real `File` on fd 0 and reads
    what is left through a `try_clone` of it -- a second `File::open` would be
    an independent offset that could not observe the child at all.

  - `-u` was accepted and IGNORED, and it is CLOSED on both halves -- which took
    two mechanisms rather than one flag, since the halves meet nowhere in the
    code: a record-sized read at the front, a flush per output operation at the
    back.

    The WRITE half is GNU's `flush_output` (execute.c:413), which ORDERS two
    streams sharing a descriptor. `sed --posix -u -n -e p -e s/^/W/ -e 'w
    /dev/stdout' h` interleaves here as it does in GNU, where it used to come
    out in two groups; that case was the xfail this landing promotes. Without
    the flag the two still GROUP, which td-txt already had right, though this
    entry named the wrong reason for it: the `w` target was an unbuffered
    `File` here and is stdio-buffered in GNU, so what orders GNU's two groups
    is the CLOSE rather than either buffer (see the `w`-target entry below,
    which measured it). So the cell the special-file table's posixicity gate
    TRADED is bought back rather than swapped again: both sides of it agree now.

    WHERE that flush goes is the whole of the write half, and GNU's answer is
    not "every write" or "every line" but a list of five: `flush_output` is
    called from `output_line` (execute.c:433), from `dump_append` after the
    WHOLE queue (505), from `do_list` for `l` (959), and from `=` (1627) and `F`
    (1635). Read off the pinned 4.9 tarball rather than remembered, after a
    reviewer asked what the citation this entry used to carry was pointing at.

    All three wrong neighbours were built and measured before that list was
    read, and they fail in two different ways -- one reorders bytes, two only
    rechunk them.
    Flushing every write splits a line's text from its separator into two
    `write(2)`s: identical bytes, and a chunking a concurrent reader can still
    tell apart, which is the one thing this flag is for. Flushing only at the
    end of a LINE leaves `=`, `l` and an append sitting in the buffer, which the
    corpus did not notice and a 3,200-case sweep did in 17 cells -- so those are
    cases now, six of them, each with its unflagged grouping beside it. The
    queue is the third: flushing per ITEM rather than once makes `a\X` followed
    by `r f` two `write(2)`s where GNU issues one, which no case can see because
    the bytes agree. Under strace the shapes measured issue byte-identical
    `write(2)` sequences to GNU's: three whole lines for `-u -n p`, six for
    `=;p`, an `r` dump arriving as one call, and a two-item queue as one. That
    parity is over the shapes measured and not a general property: the entry
    below records the one thing that still splits a flush in two, and it splits
    the same way without the flag.

    The IMMEDIATE `0r` is the exception on that list, and it is an absence
    rather than an entry: GNU's zero-address read calls `print_file` with no
    `flush_output` after it (execute.c:1520), so its bytes ride the next flush
    point. `sed -u -e '0r sub' h` is `S\n1\n` in ONE write there and here.
    Nothing dependency-free can hold that: `0r` is refused under `--posix`, and
    without `--posix` a `w /dev/stdout` is aliased to the same sink, so there is
    no second descriptor to order it against. It is written down because it
    looks like an oversight beside the other four -- a review of this landing
    proposed adding the flush -- and the source and strace agree it is not.

    The READ half is that GNU under `-u` consumes one RECORD, so the next
    program on a shared descriptor gets the rest. `Stream::block` is 1 under the
    flag, and a byte-sized READ is what produces a record-sized CONSUMPTION: a
    reader cannot find a separator without having read past it, so leaving the
    rest for somebody else means asking for a byte at a time. Measured with a
    second reader on the pipe, `sed -u -n 1q` against `sed -n 1q`, td-txt now
    matches GNU in all twelve cells: 4104 fed leaves 4096 rather than 8, 8192
    leaves 8184 rather than 4096, and 20000 leaves 19992 rather than 15904, for
    8- and 80-byte lines alike. No CASE can show that, for the reason the
    give-back entry above gives -- the harness runs ONE child -- so the test is
    a Rust one that clones the pipe's read end and reads what the stream did
    not. One shape does reach a case, and it is the only one that pins the
    DESCRIPTOR-0 reader's block rather than an operand's: `sed -n -u -e 'r
    /dev/stdin' - -` over a pipe carrying `x\ny` prints `y`, because the
    lowercase `r` reopens the name and dumps whatever the reader left. That cell
    was empty here before and is the one the `r /dev/stdin` entry above records.

    Those twelve cells are the MAIN input stream, and GNU's flag reaches no
    other. `R` opens its source as a stream of ITS OWN, which stays block-
    buffered under `-u` -- so `R /dev/stdin` still swallows the pipe there.
    Measured with a second reader: `sed -u -n -e 'R /dev/stdin' -e p IN` over 20
    fed lines leaves GNU's pipe EMPTY and leaves s4-s20 here. Where a `-`
    operand follows the `R`, GNU has already dropped what that operand was going
    to read and stops early -- `sed -u -n -e 'R /dev/stdin' -e p IN -` prints
    through s3 in GNU and everything here. Mixed with the `$` lookahead, whose
    peek comes off the same pipe a byte at a time, GNU can also SPLIT a record
    between the two streams: `sed -n -u -e '$=;R /dev/stdin' IN -` prints `s1
    s2 3`, where `3` is the tail of `s3` whose `s` the peek took. td-txt has one
    reader for that descriptor by construction, so the flag changes how MUCH is
    read and never which record reaches whom -- its `-u` answers here are the
    answers both programs give without the flag. Not matched, deliberately, and
    it is the same choice as the swallowed byte below: every cell where GNU
    differs is one where GNU has lost or split a record. Bounded by sweep rather
    than by argument -- 2,400 cases over `R`/`r` on `/dev/stdin` crossed with
    operand lists, where every remaining GNU divergence is under `-u` and every
    one is identical on the build before this landing.

    What the flag does NOT change is where a SEEKABLE stdin is left: the
    give-back is computed from the reader rather than from the block, so every
    row of `sed_leaves_a_seekable_stdin_after_the_last_record_it_read` comes out
    at the same offset either way. GNU agrees on all of them but one, and that
    one it LOSES A BYTE on. `sed -u -n -e '1{$!p}' -e 1q` over a seekable stdin
    leaves GNU at offset 4 and td-txt at 3, so GNU's next reader gets
    `2\nl3\nl4\n` where this one gets `l2\nl3\nl4\n`. The `$` lookahead is a
    pushback, and glibc does not return a pushed-back byte to the file offset
    when the stream is unbuffered -- so the byte is swallowed. Measured over
    five shapes: every one where the lookahead fired and the run quit before
    consuming that record loses exactly one byte, and a `1q` with no lookahead
    loses none. Deliberately NOT matched, for the reason the paragraph above
    gives: reproducing it means mis-positioning a shared descriptor by one byte
    on purpose, which is the harm the whole give-back exists to avoid. Those two
    are the whole of where this landing chooses against GNU, and both are cells
    where GNU has lost part of somebody else's input.

  - A FLUSH CAN SPLIT IN TWO on the way out, CLOSED, which bounded the
    `write(2)` parity claimed above and was not about `-u` at all. `Out` was a
    `BufWriter` over `std::io::Stdout`, and that is itself a `LineWriter`:
    flushing a buffer whose LAST byte is not a separator splits at the last one
    it does contain. So `printf '1\n2\n3' | sed -u p` ended `"3" "\n" "3"`
    against GNU's `"3" "\n3"`, and `sed -z -e G h` was two calls here against
    one there -- UNFLAGGED, which is what says the cause was the double
    buffering rather than the flag. Found by a review of the `-u` landing, which
    is why it is written beside it; the six shapes that landing measured all end
    their buffer on a separator, which is why they came out identical.

    Both are one write now, because the sink is the same `StdioBuf` a `w` target
    is, over a DUP of descriptor 1 rather than over `Stdout` -- safe,
    `try_clone_to_owned` needs no `unsafe`. It had to move for grep too, which
    shares `Out`, and `Out::new` became fallible in the doing: the dup can fail
    only on a closed or exhausted descriptor table, which is exactly when a
    silent fallback to a second buffering layer would be worst.

    The sink's buffer SIZE was the other half of the same divergence, found
    while measuring the `w` target's, and it closed with it: `BufWriter::new` is
    8192 where GNU's stdout takes the pipe's `st_blksize` and gets 4096. Nothing
    can see that until two streams share the descriptor and each flushes when
    ITS buffer fills. Under `--posix` -- where `special()` is not consulted at
    all, so `w /dev/stdout` is an ORDINARY open of that path and a second stdio
    buffer rather than the alias an extended run makes it -- 200 lines of 100
    bytes through `sed --posix -n -e p -e 's/^/W/' -e 'w /dev/stdout'` comes out
    of GNU in runs of 41 L, 40 W, 41 L, 81 W, 41 L, 41 W, 41 L, 37 W, 37 L: two
    4096 buffers filling at nearly the same rate, stdout crossing first because
    `p` runs before `w` within a cycle. td-txt reproduces that sequence exactly;
    the build before it gave 41 W, 81 L, 81 W, 81 L, 78 W, 38 L, the first run
    being W because the 4096 target filled at line 41 while the 8192 sink waited
    until 82. `a_posix_w_of_dev_stdout_is_a_second_buffer_over_one_pipe` holds
    the smallest version of that -- 41 records, where the sink crosses and the
    target does not, so the run must OPEN with the sink's 4096 -- outside the
    corpus, whose files are written inline.

    Two of stdio's rules past the buffer arrived with the sink and apply to
    every `StdioBuf`, both from `_IO_new_file_xsputn` and neither reachable by a
    `BufWriter`. A write that does not fit does NOT go through the buffer a
    block at a time: what is left after the fill is flushed is handed to the
    kernel DIRECTLY, rounded down to whole blocks (`do_write = to_do - to_do %
    block_size`, and no rounding at all below 128), with only the sub-block tail
    buffered. And the buffer does not EXIST until the first overflow, so the
    first write of all finds no room and takes that path whole. Together they
    are why one 8193-byte record is `8192` then `1` where a fill-and-flush loop
    gives `4096, 4096, 1`, and why the same record written second is `4096,
    4096`: `sed -n p` over two such lines is `8192, 4096, 4096, 2` in GNU and
    here. A line-buffered stream is bound by them too -- stdio scans for the
    newline only when the write FITS in the room -- so a terminal is no promise
    that every record is written when it ends.

    grep is where the same question is still OPEN, and it is `l`'s exactly:
    GNU's grep writes a matched line and its separator in ONE `fwrite`, so a
    12288-byte record reaches the descriptor as a single write, where td-txt
    writes the line and the separator separately and gets 8192 then 4096. Both
    the bytes and their order are identical; only the boundaries differ, and
    only for a record longer than the buffer. PRE-EXISTING -- the build before
    the sink answers differently again (12287 then 1) -- and not closed with
    `l` because the separator is not part of the slice grep holds and a final
    line may carry none at all, so joining them is a copy on the hot path
    rather than a re-slice. Two cells of the sink sweep, and the only two it
    still reports.

    Neither rule can be seen from the byte harness, and that is arithmetic
    rather than a missing case: every scheme that fills before it writes flushes
    exactly `floor(total / cap) * cap`, so the `r`-of-a-`w` trick above answers
    identically for all of them. Only a syscall trace tells them apart. They are
    pinned by a unit test on `StdioBuf`, which reads the file back after each
    `put` and so sees the direct write as it happens, and measured by a
    differential strace sweep of 143 sed and grep shapes against GNU -- write
    lengths in order, which is the only differential in this crate that compares
    something other than bytes.

    `l` is the one COMMAND those rules reached, and it moved to meet them: GNU's
    `do_list` writes its listing ONE BYTE at a time (`ck_fwrite (o, 1, 1, fp)`),
    so a listing never takes the direct path, where td-txt submitted the whole
    escaped buffer and did. It now submits in pieces one byte UNDER the buffer,
    which cannot overflow it and so gives the byte's boundaries at four
    thousandths of the calls -- per byte was written first and measured 4x
    slower than the parent over a 20 MB listing, and per wrapped LINE would
    agree at the default width and not at `l 0`, which wraps nowhere. Under the
    buffer rather than equal to it because a piece of exactly the capacity finds
    NO room on the first write of a run, the buffer not existing yet, and so
    goes to the descriptor ahead of anything sharing it: `sed --posix -n -e 'l
    0' -e 'w /dev/stdout'` over a 4094-byte record put the listing first where
    GNU puts the `w` record first. Both cross-model reviews of the landing found
    that independently, and it is why the accessor is `piece()` and not the
    capacity.

  - A `w` TARGET is buffered, which is CLOSED and was the third mechanism `-u`
    above left standing. It is the same divergence the `r`-of-a-`w` entry
    further up carries, seen from the flag's side: `-u` made it visible rather
    than caused it, because `r` re-opens its source every cycle and so needs no
    second reader to see how much of the target has reached disk. `sed -e 'w wf'
    -e 'r wf' h` over `1\n2\n3\n` is `1\n2\n3\n` in both now -- six bytes, well
    under the buffer, so the `r` finds nothing until the run ends -- and under
    `-u` both give `1\n1\n2\n1\n2\n3\n1\n2\n3\n`, GNU because it flushes every
    output after every line and td-txt because a `w` file is one of those
    outputs. The unflagged case was an xfail with GNU's golden beside a passing
    flagged one; both are promoted.

    Where a buffer goes, a FLUSH has to follow it out of every exit, and that is
    the part `-u` did not already answer. GNU reaches it two ways. The ordinary
    one is `finish_program` (sed.c:380), which walks `file_write` and closes each
    target, and only then does `ck_fclose (NULL)` close stdout -- last, and
    deliberately so. A `q` takes that same route, RETURNING rather than exiting;
    it is a fatal read that takes the other, `panic`ing into plain `exit` whose
    stdio teardown writes what it can and reports nothing. td-txt flushes every
    target BEFORE the sink at each exit that flushes the sink.

    The ORDER, though, is structural rather than a thing those calls buy: `out`
    is declared before `sed`, so a local's reverse-declaration drop order puts
    the targets' flush ahead of the sink's on every path, including a `?` from
    somewhere nobody enumerated. What the explicit calls add is the ERROR, which
    a drop cannot report -- GNU exits 4 on a target it could not flush, so a `q`
    that would have exited 0 exits 4. Getting that backwards was this landing's
    own bug and a reviewer's finding: a run-time error (an `r` of a directory)
    propagates through none of the named exits, and with `out` still declared
    last it came out `1 A` where GNU and the parent both give `A 1`.

    A FATAL READ is the exception to the reporting half, and it goes the other
    way: GNU's reader panics into `exit`, which says nothing about a flush it
    could not complete, so the read error is the ONLY diagnostic even when a
    target also failed. Measured -- a `/dev/full` target under a fatal read is one
    message there and was two here.

    Two of stdio's choices are the DESCRIPTOR's rather than constants
    (`_IO_file_doallocate`), so both are asked of it: the capacity starts at
    `BUFSIZ` and takes `st_blksize` only where that is positive AND SMALLER, so
    4096 for every target on this platform -- which is why the rows above are
    what they are -- and 8192 rather than its own number on a filesystem
    reporting 64 KiB, which is a divergence this platform cannot show and two
    reviews of the sink landing caught by reading; and a target that is a
    TERMINAL is
    LINE-buffered instead, flushing through the last `\n` -- stdio's newline and
    not sed's record separator, so `-z` does not move it. Missing the terminal
    half was a REGRESSION rather than a gap: with two `w` targets on one PTY,
    `sed --posix -n -e 'w /dev/stdout' -e 's/^/E/' -e 'w /dev/stderr'` interleaves
    `1 E1 2 E2 3 E3` in GNU and in the build before this, and a block-buffered
    target grouped it. A corpus case cannot carry it, the harness handing its
    children pipes, so the rule is pinned by a unit test on `StdioBuf` instead --
    the buffer the sink now shares, which is why the entry below closed.

    The SINK on a terminal was the same divergence one layer out and closed with
    the entry below it: GNU line-buffers its stdout onto a PTY where `Out`
    block-buffered, so `sed --posix -n -e p -e 's/^/W/' -e 'w /dev/stdout'` is
    `1 W1 2 W2 3 W3` there and grouped as `W1 W2 W3 1 2 3` here. Both orders
    agree now, measured over a real PTY, since the sink asks the descriptor the
    same two questions a `w` target does. The isatty DECISION is still untested:
    the harness hands its children pipes, so no case in this crate reaches it.

    WHICH target goes first is that same walk read carefully, and it is LAST
    OPENED FIRST: `get_openfile` PREPENDS to `file_write` (compile.c:425), so the
    list runs newest to oldest. td-txt keys its targets by name in a `BTreeMap`, which
    iterates by PATH, and the two orders differ as soon as two names both reach
    standard output -- `/dev/fd/1` is a second spelling of the pipe and a
    different target, since targets are keyed by name. `sed --posix -n -e p -e
    's/^/W/' -e 'w /dev/fd/1' -e 's/^/V/' -e 'w /dev/stdout'` was `W… V…` here
    against GNU's `V… W…`; each `w` now carries its open index and the flush
    sorts on it. Found by pushing on a drift axis that stayed green -- flushing
    only the first target broke nothing, because every case until then had one.

    The cost this entry predicted is real and is accepted: a line written and not
    yet flushed is a line a crash loses, where the write-through target had it on
    disk. That is GNU's behaviour, and a `w` target that disagreed with GNU about
    what an `r` in the same run can see was the larger of the two problems.

  - An AMBIGUOUS long-option abbreviation lists a different SET of
    possibilities for grep. `grep --c` is `'--context' '--color' '--colour'
    '--count'` in GNU against `'--context' '--count'` here, because
    `--color`/`--colour` are among the options td-txt does not implement, so
    they are not candidates to be ambiguous WITH. Same wording, same status, and
    the COUNT of possibilities is what differs; closing it needs those options
    built, not anything about the table. Predates the branch; recorded when the
    raw-name landing rewrote both joins (a `join(" ")` over `String`s cannot
    carry a name, so the list is now built as bytes) and a review asked what the
    rewrite was holding itself to.

    The `--b` family is now CLOSED, and how it closed is the general answer to
    the rest. Serving `--binary-files` put a fourth name under `--b` but left
    `--binary` -- `-U`'s spelling, refused -- out, which was worse than the gap
    it fixed: `--binary` is a proper PREFIX of `--binary-files`, so `grep
    --binary pat f` stopped being a refusal and became an ill-formed
    `--binary-files=pat`. So `--binary` sits in the table and its arm refuses,
    and `--b`, `--bi` and `--bin` now print GNU's diagnostic line to the byte.
    A name is therefore in that table to be RESOLVED, which is not the same as
    being served -- the dispatch decides that, exactly as it already decided
    `--binary-files=without-match` is refused while its siblings are not.
    What would close the REST of this entry was counted rather than guessed,
    because the obvious list is short. THIRTEEN names: `--color` and
    `--colour`, `--exclude`, `--exclude-from`, `--exclude-dir`, `--include`,
    `--initial-tab`, `--group-separator`, `--no-group-separator`,
    `--no-ignore-case`, `--label`, `--line-buffered` and `--perl-regexp`. The
    first draft of this sentence named eight and would have left five
    divergences standing, `--c` -- this entry's own example -- among them,
    since `--color` sits beside the `--colour` it named. And thirteen still
    leaves TWO, `--col` and `--colo`: `color` and `colour` share a `val` and an
    arity in GNU, so glibc collapses them the way it collapses
    `--fixed-regexp`/`--fixed-strings`, which is a `SYNONYMS` entry rather than
    a table row. (That prediction held and is now DONE -- the two names went
    in together with the `SYNONYMS` entry beside them, and `--col`, `--colo`
    and `--colou` all resolve here as they do in GNU. It is the only one of
    the landings below that needed anything but a table row.)

    ALL THIRTEEN are done, over four landings recorded in the paragraphs
    below: the four filters, then `--initial-tab`/`--perl-regexp`, then
    `--color`/`--colour`, then the last five together. They went a family at
    a time rather than as one sweep because a name in the table is a refusal
    that has to say WHY, and that is a per-option decision -- which is also
    why the last five could go together: their whys are four different ones
    and each got its own case.

    The FILTER family -- `--exclude`, `--exclude-from`, `--exclude-dir` and
    `--include` -- is the four, and it had to enter together: `exclude` is a
    proper prefix of the other two, so tabling one without its siblings repeats
    the `--binary` mistake above. It also shows this entry's `-D` lesson from
    the other side, and the sweep is what found it rather than a prediction.
    Re-run over the same 2,268 spellings, the parent commit answers 51
    both-ambiguous, 30 identical, 21 by SET, none by ORDER; this one answers
    60, 36, 24, none. Two families CLOSED -- `--e` and `--ex` are byte-for-byte
    GNU now, where td-txt had silently resolved them to `--extended-regexp`
    while GNU called them ambiguous, which is the worst kind of divergence
    because it differs in what got COMPILED and says nothing. And one was
    UNCOVERED: `--in` was unambiguous here (only `--invert-match` matched) and
    is ambiguous now that `--include` is beside it, so it joins `--i` in
    lacking `--initial-tab`. So the by-SET count went UP by three spellings
    while the tool moved closer to GNU, and both readings are honest -- what
    grew is the number of divergences that are VISIBLE as diagnostics, which is
    what a previously-silent one becomes when the option table stops guessing.
    That left seven names under this sweep, in eight families — as of the
    filter landing, superseded by the `--initial-tab` paragraph below and the
    six-family count after it: `--c`/`--co`
    want `--color` and `--colour`, `--i`/`--in` want `--initial-tab`, `--l`
    wants `--label` AND `--line-buffered` while `--li` wants only the second
    (`--label` is not a `--li` prefix match, so that pair is a union and not a
    per-family list -- a first draft of this sentence gave both names to both
    families), and `--n`/`--no` want `--no-group-separator` and
    `--no-ignore-case`. `--group-separator` and `--perl-regexp` are on the
    thirteen and NOT here, because `--g` and `--p` are unambiguous in GNU and
    so never reach this sweep's both-ambiguous bucket at all.

    How much of "GNU's declaration positions" a test can actually hold is
    narrower than the phrase suggests, and the boundary was measured rather
    than assumed. A position is observable only through a prefix that matches
    the moved name AND something declared near it, so: the `exclude` group is
    pinned to sit after `--extended-regexp` (moving it to the front reds `--e`)
    and its internal order is pinned, but moving the whole group to the END of
    the table reds NOTHING -- no spelling matches both an `exclude` and a
    non-`e` name. That was first written here as "unobservable by
    construction", which is WRONG and is corrected rather than quietly
    dropped: `--=` is an empty prefix, so it matches every name and its
    diagnostic is the whole table in order. Every position in the table is
    observable through that one case. What is true is narrower -- no
    OTHER spelling can see the `exclude` group's position, and pinning it
    through `--=` would mean re-recording that assertion on every landing that
    serves another option, which is why the case pins the leading group and
    stops. Unpinned deliberately, then, not unobservable.

    `include`'s slot IS observable, through `--i`
    and `--in` alone, and was pinned by nothing until those two cases were
    added: moving `include` even ONE slot, past `--ignore-case`, had left the
    corpus green. Both now red. This is the rule at the end of this entry
    applied to a new family, and it is why the claim above is that the table
    MATCHES GNU's order rather than that the tests prove it does.

    `--initial-tab` then closed the `--i` family -- `--perl-regexp` landed
    with it and had nothing to do with that, its own family being `--p` -- and
    the sweep re-run gives 60 both-ambiguous, 42 identical, 18 by SET, none by
    ORDER. Neither option is shadowed and neither changes what gets compiled;
    they are in the table for the reason the module doc gives LAST, which is
    that a name absent from it is absent from every ambiguity list and from
    every abbreviation that would reach it -- removing either row leaves the
    tool erroring exactly as before, only with a different diagnostic. So
    `--i` and `--in` now match GNU's diagnostic LINE, and its status and
    stdout, and LEFT divergence.test.txt for grep-cli.test.txt — the one
    way a case leaves that file, td-txt having stopped diverging — where they
    are also, still, the only assertion holding `--include` in its declared
    slot. `--p` is the mirror image and stayed: GNU resolves it to
    `--perl-regexp` and SERVES it (this host's grep has PCRE compiled in, so
    `grep --p a` matches and exits 0), where td-txt resolves the same name and
    refuses it. The refusal is the deliberate omission; what the table row buys
    is that the diagnostic names `--perl-regexp` rather than reporting `--p`
    unrecognized.

    That left SIX families and six names — as of the `--initial-tab` landing,
    superseded by the paragraph below — all of them options this crate
    would have to actually implement rather than merely resolve: `--c`/`--co`
    want `--color` and `--colour`, `--l` wants `--label` and
    `--line-buffered` (`--li` only the second), and `--n`/`--no` want
    `--no-group-separator` and `--no-ignore-case`. The cheap half of this
    entry is therefore done — every name whose absence distorted an
    ambiguity list without costing a feature is now in the table — and what
    remains needs the features.

    `--color`/`--colour` then closed `--c` and `--co`, taking the sweep to 60
    both-ambiguous, 48 identical, 12 by SET, none by ORDER. It needed the two
    things flagged above: a THIRD arity,
    `Arg::Optional`, because `--color` is grep's only `optional_argument`, and
    a `SYNONYMS` pair. Both are pinned from both
    sides — declared `Arg::None` the option would answer `doesn't allow an
    argument` to `--color=always`, declared `Arg::Required` it would answer
    `requires an argument` to a bare `--color`, and dropping the `SYNONYMS`
    pair makes `--col` ambiguous where GNU searches. The arity's third effect
    is real and NOT observable here: an `optional_argument` never consumes the
    next argv element, so GNU's `grep --color always f` searches for `always`,
    while td-txt refuses whichever way it is declared.

    Two things about that count, both of which a reviewer got wrong from this
    text and neither of which is a wrong NUMBER. The universe is BOUNDED AT
    TWO CHARACTERS -- 27 one-character prefixes over `a-z` and `-` plus 729
    two-character ones, each bare and with `=1` and `=`, which is where 2,268
    comes from. It is defined in full further down, in the reorder paragraph
    now marked as-of; marking that paragraph superseded made its NUMBERS
    historical and accidentally made its METHOD look historical too, so the
    definition is repeated here. Every figure in this entry is 3x a count of
    prefixes: as of THIS landing, 20 both-ambiguous, 16 identical, 4 by SET
    -- the next one takes it to 20/20/0.

    And because of that bound, four families did NOT exhaust the divergent
    spellings -- they exhausted the divergent NAMES. Past two characters
    `--lin`, `--line`, `--line-` and `--no-` diverged by SET as well, each for
    the same missing name as the two-character prefix it extends. (All four
    closed with those names, in the landing below; this sentence is what the
    bound was hiding AT THIS LANDING, not a live gap.) The bound
    buys a sweep that reruns in seconds and a count that moves only when a
    NAME lands; it costs the tail, which no landing has ever been decided by.

    THE LAST FIVE NAMES then closed it. `--label`, `--line-buffered`,
    `--no-group-separator` and `--no-ignore-case` were what those four
    families wanted; `--group-separator` went with them for completeness and
    for `--group-separator=XX` to say `unsupported` rather than
    `unrecognized`. A draft of this said `--g` wanted it, which is wrong and
    this entry says why a few paragraphs up: `--g` and `--p` prefix exactly
    one GNU name each, so neither is ever ambiguous and neither can be closed
    by a table row. Measured, `grep --g a f1` takes `a` as the separator in
    GNU and reports nothing at all.

    The two-character sweep is now 60 both-ambiguous,
    60 identical, none by SET, none by ORDER -- 20 prefixes, all agreeing,
    where the paragraph above counted 4 of 20 divergent.

    The tail that bound leaves out was then swept too, which is the point of
    naming it a bound rather than a universe. Every NON-EMPTY prefix of every
    one of GNU's 50 long names -- 427 of them -- gives 40 ambiguous in both
    tools and all 40 identical, closing `--lin`, `--line`, `--line-` and
    `--no-` along with everything else past two characters.

    NON-EMPTY is load-bearing and was got wrong here first. The empty prefix
    is a prefix too, `--=` reaches it, and it DISAGREES: GNU lists 50 names
    and td-txt 49. The missing one is `--unix-byte-offsets`, the single GNU
    long name deliberately not in the table (its divergence is a status, not
    a diagnostic -- GNU warns it is obsolete and then matches), so the empty
    prefix is exactly where that omission shows. A first draft of this
    paragraph claimed "no prefix of any GNU option name disagrees", which is
    false by one name, and false against a measurement that had already been
    taken and not connected to the claim. The true statement is narrower and
    still worth having: every non-empty prefix agrees, and the only prefix
    that does not differs by exactly the one name this crate chose to leave
    out.

    The entry is therefore finished as an ambiguity entry. It stays because
    the METHOD is the durable part -- re-run the sweep when a name lands, and
    expect a landing that closes one gap to uncover another, which happened
    twice here (`-D` uncovering an order gap, `--include` uncovering `--in`).

    Every "byte for byte" in this entry means the DIAGNOSTIC LINE and carries
    TWO preconditions, not the one this paragraph first claimed. The second is
    the block BELOW that line: GNU follows a getopt error with `Usage: grep
    [OPTION]... PATTERNS [FILE]...' and `Try 'grep --help' for more
    information.', where td-txt prints its own single usage line. That applies
    to every refusal in the corpus and is why a case can assert GNU's exact
    diagnostic while its `stderr-json` holds a block GNU never prints. The
    first precondition is argv[0], and it is a divergence of its own that
    nothing here had recorded. GNU prefixes
    a diagnostic with `argv[0]` VERBATIM, not its basename, so
    `/usr/bin/grep --e a` opens `/usr/bin/grep: option '--e' is ambiguous`;
    td-txt hardcodes `grep: `. So the two agree exactly when invoked as plain
    `grep` and differ by that prefix otherwise. No case can show it -- the
    harness runs everything with `argv[0]` = `grep` -- which is also why it
    went unnoticed: the scripts that measured the identity claims above
    normalised the prefix away before comparing, and a normalisation is easy to
    mistake for a result. It applies to every diagnostic the crate emits, not
    to the ambiguity ones alone, and it predates every landing on this branch.
    Fixing it is a decision rather than an oversight: it means the multicall
    reporting under whatever name it was invoked by, which for a name that is
    not an applet is already a different message (`td-txt: unknown applet`),
    so the change is about the multicall's identity and not about grep.

    The ORDER half of this was sed's and is closed: `sed --s` answered
    `'--sandbox' '--separate' '--silent'`, td-txt's alphabetical table order,
    where GNU walks `longopts[]` (sed.c:197) and answers `'--silent'
    '--sandbox' '--separate'`. sed's table is that declaration now -- its ORDER,
    which is what a diagnostic shows; the flag beside each name is only whether
    it takes a value at all, GNU's required/optional distinction being left to
    the dispatch rather than encoded twice. grep's is that declaration too, as
    of the reorder below.

    Every number from here to the end of this entry is AS OF THE REORDER
    LANDING and is left standing as the record of it; the current ones are in
    the filter-family paragraph above, which is where a re-run goes. Two
    reviewers have now read the past tense below as a live claim -- one of them
    filing "the table is alphabetical" as a finding against a commit that only
    inserted into GNU's order -- so the tense is being marked rather than
    trusted.

    grep's table stayed alphabetical for a long time because reordering bought
    nothing measurable, and that was a measurement rather than a guess. What
    changed is
    worth more than the number. Sweeping 2,268 spellings -- every one- and
    two-character prefix over `a-z` and `-`, bare and with `=1` and `=` -- there
    were 48 where GNU and td-txt BOTH reported an ambiguity. Alphabetical answered
    12 of them identically, 30 differed by the SET above, and SIX differed by
    ORDER ALONE: `--d` and `--de`, in three spellings each, where td-txt put
    `--dereference-recursive` first and GNU puts it last.

    That last count was NOT ONE until the `-D` landing, and how it moved is the
    part to keep. Before `--devices` existed the two tools disagreed about
    `--d`'s SET -- GNU had a candidate td-txt lacked -- and `--de` was not
    ambiguous here at all. Adding the option closed the set gap and UNCOVERED an
    order gap that had been hiding behind it: a landing that strictly reduced
    one divergence introduced another without touching the table's order. That
    is the general shape to expect as options are served, and it is why the
    sweep is re-run rather than cited.

    The table became GNU's `long_options[]` (grep.c:504) with the unserved
    options left out and the order intact -- which it still is, every landing
    since having inserted into that order rather than re-sorted -- and that
    took those six to ZERO: 18 identical, 30 by SET, none by order, again as of
    the reorder. GNU's table is not alphabetical in
    several places and TWO of them show, differently. `--dereference-recursive`
    is declared beside `--recursive`, with seven names after it, rather than
    among the `d`s where it would sort: that moves `--d` and `--de`, and
    both become GNU byte for byte. The regexp-syntax group is hoisted to the
    front, so `--fixed-strings` precedes `--file`: that moves `--f` and `--fi`
    into GNU's arrangement without making them identical, the name in that first
    slot being `--fixed-regexp` for GNU. (Both ARE identical now -- serving
    `--fixed-regexp` later put that name in that slot and closed the pair. The
    sentence stands as what the reorder ALONE bought.) A fifth listing changed
    and shows it
    most plainly: `--=` splits into an EMPTY name and a value, every option
    prefixes the empty string, so that one diagnostic is the whole table read
    out in order. FIVE in all, and that is an enumeration rather than an
    argument -- every proper prefix of every name served, 298 of them plus the
    empty one, diffed against the previous build. The bucket counts above
    answer a narrower question and cannot see `--f` improve at all, since it
    was SET-divergent before and remains so.

    Three inversions against alphabetical order is the whole of it, and the
    third is inert: `--invert-match` is declared after `--regexp`, but `--i`
    reaches `--ignore-case` first under either order, so no listing moves.
    Nothing ELSE is out of sorted position -- `--silent` and `--version` sit
    where their spelling puts them and are not departures at all, which is a
    better reason for them not mattering than the one first written here.

    `--d` and `--de` are pinned as corpus goldens, possibilities list and all,
    along with `--r`, which agreed under either order and is there so that
    reordering cannot quietly break what already matched. All three assert
    EXACT stderr rather than a substring, because a substring passes equally
    with another candidate appended after the ones named -- which is the whole
    property being claimed. What is GNU's byte for byte is the DIAGNOSTIC LINE;
    the exact assertion also covers the usage line under it, and that one is
    td-txt's own (`usage: grep [-ab...]` against GNU's `Usage: grep [OPTION]...`
    plus a `Try 'grep --help'` line), which is a divergence recorded in its own
    right. `--=` is pinned too but only at the HEAD of its
    list: 34 names would have to be re-recorded by every landing that serves
    another option, where the head moves only if the leading group does.
    `--n` stays name-only, since what still differs there is the SET and no
    order can fix that. `--f` no longer does -- see below.

    `--fixed-regexp` is GNU's obsolete second spelling of `-F`, and serving it
    is what takes `--f` from SET-divergent to identical. It arrives with the
    rule that makes it safe, because alone it would have made things WORSE:
    `getopt_long` compares each later prefix match against the FIRST one and
    drops it when both name the same option with the same argument policy, so
    GNU's `--f` lists `--fixed-regexp` and NOT `--fixed-strings`. Without that
    rule td-txt would have listed five where GNU lists four, and `--fixed`
    -- a prefix of both spellings -- would have become ambiguous where GNU
    resolves it. The two are one landing for that reason.

    It is compared against the FIRST match and not against all of them, which
    is the part no summary of `getopt_long` would tell you and which the corpus
    now pins from both sides: `--f` omits `--fixed-strings`, while `--=` lists
    it, the first match there being `--basic-regexp`. GNU behaves that way on
    its own table too -- `--co` lists BOTH `--color` and `--colour`, equal to
    each other in every field but not to `--context`, which it matched first.
    A global dedup would drop one of those and be wrong.

    td-txt has THREE such pairs: `fixed-regexp`/`fixed-strings`,
    `color`/`colour`, and `quiet`/`silent`. Two of them collapse and the third
    never can, which is a fact about their LETTERS rather than about the rule.
    Collapsing requires one of the pair to be the FIRST match, so it needs a
    prefix that selects one of them, and the only string that is a prefix of
    both `quiet` and `silent` is the empty one -- whose first match is
    `--basic-regexp`, not either of them. `--=` duly lists both `--quiet` and
    `--silent`. The roster names that pair anyway, since what makes two names
    collapse is being one OPTION rather than anything about their letters.

    `color`/`colour` was written up here as a case from OUTSIDE td-txt's table
    -- GNU resolves `--colo` where td-txt, lacking both names, could not be
    asked. It is inside now, and it is the second pair that collapses:
    `--col`, `--colo` and `--colou` resolve here exactly as `--fixed` does,
    and spec/divergence.test.txt's `--col` case is what holds it. That case
    exists BECAUSE dropping the roster entry makes `--col` ambiguous, which is
    the failure this whole paragraph is about, reproduced on the one pair
    added since it was written.

    glibc's condition is `val` AND `has_arg`, and td-txt splits it: the roster
    above is the first half, the caller compares the arity for the second.
    Nothing reaches that second half. Enumerating GNU's table there are exactly
    four same-option groups -- `fixed-regexp`/`fixed-strings`,
    `color`/`colour`, `quiet`/`silent`, and
    `group-separator`/`no-group-separator` -- and only the last disagrees about
    arity, sharing no prefix but the empty one, where neither can be the first
    match. So GNU's own table never exercises the arity test either; it is kept
    because the rule belongs to `getopt_long` rather than to one program's
    options.

    Re-sweeping the same 2,268 spellings, as of the `--fixed-regexp` landing
    and superseded by every entry above it: 24 identical where the reorder left
    18, 24 differing by SET where it left 30, none by order. The six that moved
    are `--f` and `--fi` in three spellings each.

  - `-V` is an option GNU sed still TAKES, with an argument, and td-txt refuses.
    GNU's getopt string carries it (the obsolete `--version` spelling) but its
    switch has no arm for it, so `sed -V x -n -e p` consumes the `x`, falls to
    the usage arm and prints `Usage: ...` alone at exit 1; td-txt says
    `invalid option -- 'V'` and then its own usage, also at exit 1. Same status,
    and the difference is one diagnostic line plus the usage wording already
    recorded above. Found by the byte-quoting sweep as the ONE comparison in
    1,530 that neither the byte families nor the usage line explained, and it
    predates the branch -- `V` is ASCII, so nothing about rendering touched it.

  - `-f` naming an ENDLESS stream (`/dev/zero`, `/dev/urandom`): GNU parses the
    script as it reads, so it fails on line 1 -- `unknown command: ` on a NUL
    byte -- and exits 1. td-txt reads the whole script into memory BEFORE
    parsing, so it grows until the allocation fails and exits 4 with `read
    error on /dev/zero: out of memory` -- measured under a `ulimit -v`, and it
    said `couldn't open file` until the open and read failures were told apart.
    Both refuse and neither hangs, so this is fail-closed either way; what
    differs is that td-txt spends memory to get there. Closing it means parsing
    incrementally rather than reading first, which is a different program and
    its own landing, not a corner of this one. Predates the branch --
    `read_input` read to the end the same way `read_script` does. Found by a
    reviewer.

  - `s//\N/` -- the empty regex reusing the last one -- is unchecked here and
    only half-unchecked in GNU: `/\(a\)/s//\2/` and `/a/s//\1/` are refused
    there (at `char 0`, so it is the command's OWN address regex being
    consulted) and expand to empty here, while `s/\(a\)/x/;s//\2/`, where the
    last regex came from an EARLIER command, is accepted by both. Predates the
    branch. Found because a review caught a comment claiming both tools always
    expand it empty -- which was what my own probe showed, the probe having
    reused a group-LESS regex and so never reaching the case that matters.

  - An unresolvable label's DIAGNOSTIC, which diverged in both halves: the
    WORDING (`can't find label for jump to `ar'' in GNU against `can't operate
    on label `ar'` here) and WHICH label a script with several unresolved ones
    names. CLOSED, both. The wording is now GNU's byte for byte, raw name
    included, and the walk that picks the label runs BACKWARDS: GNU prepends
    each jump to a list as it compiles one and resolves from that list's head
    (compile.c:1611), so `ba;bb;bc` names `c' and the reversed script names
    `a'. Two jumps are skipped on the way and both are pinned as cases -- one
    that RESOLVES, and the EMPTY label, which `if (*go->name)` passes over
    because it jumps to the end of the script rather than to a label.
    Predated the branch, and was found by a fuzz over malformed scripts after
    a review of the reader-truncation landing had recorded the wording half.

  - Two divergences a sweep of every command letter x {bare, addressed,
    ranged} x {flag, no flag} turned up, none of them about `--posix` and both
    predating this branch, recorded here because that sweep is how the
    one-address rule above was found and these came with it: the usage line
    after a bad option is `Usage: sed [OPTION]...` there and
    `usage: sed [-nrEsz]...` here, differing in capital and in content; and
    `sed L` is `sed: INTERNAL ERROR:
    Bad cmd L` at exit 4 in GNU, an obsolete command left in its table, where
    td-txt says `unknown command: `L''. The third was a bare `\`, worded
    `unterminated address` here against GNU's `unterminated address regex`,
    and it closed with the locus landing: the boundary case had to say
    something, and two spellings for one failure would have been worse than
    the gap.

  - An unterminated `\{` after a QUANTIFIED atom picks the other complaint:
    `sed 's/a*\{b/Z/'` is `Invalid preceding regular expression` in GNU and
    `Unmatched \{` here, while `sed 's/a\{b/Z/'` -- no quantifier -- is
    `Unmatched \{` in both. So it is the stacking error outranking the
    unterminated-brace one, not the brace wording. Identical with and without
    `--posix`, and every rule on this branch is posix-gated, which is what
    places it before the branch rather than in it.

  - `sed -E 's/a*\B/X/'` over a line `ba` selects `baX` in GNU and `bXa` here:
    a starred atom followed by an assertion picks a different empty match.
    Predates this campaign -- byte-identical on a build of the branch point --
    and found by a reviewer fuzzing ERE patterns rather than by any sweep here,
    which is the argument for the fuzz: 2918 random EREs turned up exactly this
    one, and no hand-written probe had the shape.

  - A PERMUTED multi-digit `-NUM` loses its FIRST digit in GNU, and td-txt
    keeps the whole run. Deliberate. `grep MATCH f -12 -A0` takes two lines of
    context there and twelve here; `-123` is 23, `-1234` is 234, `-210` is 10.
    It is positional and nothing else. Argv reads as alternating OPERAND and
    OPTION blocks, and the FIRST element of every option block that follows an
    operand loses its first digit if it is a digit run. So `-12 MATCH f` keeps
    every digit (its block precedes any operand); `MATCH f -A0 -12` and `MATCH
    f -n -12 -A0` keep every digit (the run does not START its block); and a
    single digit has none to lose. But it recurs per BLOCK rather than once per
    command: `MATCH f -123 /dev/null -456` truncates the SECOND run too, to 56,
    and `MATCH -A0 f -123` truncates, because there the `-A0` is in the block
    BEFORE the operand. Two runs are safe only while ADJACENT, in one block --
    `-12 -34` is 34 because the truncated `-12` loses to last-one-wins. That shape is getopt's
    permutation bookkeeping resetting the digit buffer mid-run, once per run -- no
    reading of the option makes a count mean one thing after a filename and
    another before it. Reproducing it would mean emulating getopt's optind
    state to reproduce a count that silently drops a digit by position, which
    is not a behaviour a caller can want; td-txt answers every position with
    the number that was written. Pinned in divergence.test.txt with td-txt's
    goldens, and the five agreeing positions are pinned in grep-cli.test.txt
    with GNU's.
    Recorded because two earlier readings of this were wrong in the same way:
    first "keeps only its LAST digit", then a review's correction agreeing on
    "last" with `-123`->3 and `-1234`->4. Both were measured on files so small
    that any context past 4 covered the whole file, so 23 and 234 were
    indistinguishable from 3 and 4. Both counts must be UNCLAMPED for the
    answers to differ at all, which is why the pinned cases put 130 lines
    before the match -- a third instance of the same too-small fixture reached
    review inside this very landing.

  - `-x` over a file holding a NUL, where GNU reported `binary file matches`
    at exit 0 and td-txt selected nothing and exited 1. CLOSED, and it was
    never about `-x`: it is the record-terminator entry below on a one-line
    file, which that entry had already said. See it for the mechanism.

  - A CLOSED stdin, which GNU reports as `(standard input): Bad file descriptor`
    at exit 2 where td-txt prints `0` and exits 1. Not reachable as a bug in the
    read path: Rust's runtime opens `/dev/null` onto any of the three standard
    descriptors that is closed before `main` runs, so the applet genuinely reads
    an empty file. Recorded because the processed-file rule above is otherwise
    absolute, and this is the one case where GNU's answer comes from a stat it
    makes and this cannot.

  - The ORDER of a `-r` listing, which td-txt sorts and GNU takes from the
    directory: `grep -rl a tree` answers `tree/sub/g` before `tree/f` in GNU and
    the other way here. Deliberate -- a sorted walk is reproducible where a
    directory's own order is not -- but recorded because the walk sweeps cannot
    SEE it: they compare listings order-insensitively, so a zero from one is a
    statement about content alone. It was a review that found this, not a sweep.
    It is not confined to the order of the output either: `-q` and `-m` stop at
    the first match, so which files are opened AT ALL follows from it, and
    `grep -Rq a tree` beside a dangling symlink reports it here and not in GNU
    purely because sorted order reaches it before the match. Underneath both is a
    second difference the order makes visible: td-txt WALKS THE WHOLE TREE and
    then searches, where GNU interleaves the two. So a `-q` match cannot preempt
    a diagnostic the walk has already printed -- `grep -Rq a t` over a matching
    file and a cycle warns here and is silent in GNU even when the match sorts
    first, which is not an ordering effect at all.

  - An ENDLESS input, and the BINARY verdict, are one entry now that grep
    STREAMS. Both were consequences of reading a whole operand before searching
    it; `util.rs`'s `Records` reads a buffer at a time, so this records what is
    left rather than what was.

    CLOSED: `grep -q a /dev/urandom` returns at the first match, as GNU does,
    where it never returned before. CLOSED: an operand costs a buffer rather
    than its own size -- on a 400 MB file `grep -c` succeeds under a 300 MB
    address-space limit where the parent build reported `out of memory` at
    every limit up to 700 MB. CLOSED: a NUL no longer retracts a match already
    printed. GNU's rule is that a NUL makes matches from the buffer it was
    found in onward one notice, and that rule is STICKY -- a later NUL-free
    buffer does not make a file text again, measured against GNU 3.11 both
    ways.

    NOT closed, and not closable by streaming: `/dev/zero`, which holds no
    separator at all and so is ONE UNBOUNDED RECORD -- and a record has to be
    held whole to be matched against. The two programs differ by whether the
    TEXT is asked for, and the entry has to say which:

      `grep -q x /dev/zero`      GNU runs at constant memory and never
                                 returns; td-txt reports `/dev/zero: out of
                                 memory` and exits 2.  STILL DIVERGENT.
      `grep -aq x /dev/zero`     both die -- GNU `memory exhausted` at 134,
                                 td-txt `out of memory` at 2.

    The default case is the live one: GNU classifies the stream binary, so it
    never has to EMIT a line from it and never has to hold one, which is how it
    stays at a flat ~6.8 MB. td-txt assembles the record before deciding
    anything about it. Closing that needs the binary verdict to bound the
    record, not just the search -- a separate change, and this entry is the
    record of it being open rather than of it being fixed.

    What is deliberately NOT promised is WHICH SIDE of the flip a given match
    falls on, and that is a real limit rather than a hedge. GNU GROWS its read
    buffer to hold the longest line, so its own boundary moves with the data:
    with 80-byte lines a match at 200000 is suppressed by a NUL at 250000, and
    with 100000-byte lines the same pair is not. td-txt reads a fixed 96 KiB --
    GNU's own initial size, so on a regular file, where a `read` fills what it
    is given, the boundaries agree for as long as GNU has not had to grow --
    and reproduces the RULE while declining to reproduce the arithmetic. The
    old binary-search numbers (98303 binary against 98304 text for a file,
    65535/65536 through a pipe) measured GNU's first read; they are kept here
    as what the boundary IS, not as a contract either program owes.

    An ALLOCATION that fails mid-search aborts rather than diagnosing, and the
    streaming reader makes that reachable where it was not before. `-B N`
    retains N whole records, so it is the one place a search's memory is driven
    by an OPTION; `Before::push` uses `try_reserve` and reports `out of memory`
    like a read failure, but Rust aborts on any allocation failure that is NOT
    behind one, and they cannot all be. Measured over a 400 MB operand with
    `-B 200000`: td-txt now succeeds from a 400 MB address-space limit upward,
    where the whole-file reader failed all the way to 700 MB -- but at 300 MB,
    with only ~40 MB of headroom above the ~260 MB this binary needs to start
    at all, it aborts (SIGABRT, Rust's allocator message) where the old reader
    reported `out of memory` and exited 2. Strictly more capable and, in that
    last band, worse-behaved. The band is narrow and the old code was not
    immune either -- it simply hit its ONE big allocation first, and that one
    was behind a `try_reserve`.

    ONE `read` per fill, which is `fillbuf`'s shape: looping until the buffer
    was full would make a pipe wait for 96 KiB before the first match could be
    reported, and on a pipe GNU's own boundaries are read-sized and timing-
    dependent for the same reason.

    `-c`/`-l`/`-L`/`-q` print no line content and so are never SUPPRESSED by
    the verdict in either program. That is not the same as being unaffected by
    it: the verdict also zaps, which moves what matches, and the counting
    modes are exactly where that shows -- see the record-terminator entry
    below. Distinct from the `-x` NUL entry above, which is a one-line file
    and about the anchored test rather than about how much was looked at.

  - A NUL is a RECORD TERMINATOR to GNU in binary mode, which td-txt did not
    reproduce. Found by a randomized sweep, and NOT a streaming matter. CLOSED.

    The general form, with no anchor in the pattern at all: over
    `"ppp\0qqq\nrrr\n"`, `grep -c -v zzz` counted 3 in GNU and 2 here, because
    GNU splits it into `ppp`, `qqq` and `rrr` where td-txt saw two lines.
    `-a` makes GNU agree (2), which is what identifies BINARY MODE rather than
    the pattern as the switch.

    The anchored cases are one symptom of that and not a rule of their own: `^`
    matches after the NUL because the NUL ended a record. So over
    `"\n\n\n\n\0abaa"`, `grep -c -e '^a'` counted 1 in GNU and 0 here, and over
    a one-line `"\0ab"`, `grep -n -o -C 1 -m 1 -e '^$'` said `binary file
    matches` in GNU and nothing here. An earlier draft of this entry recorded
    the anchor as the CAUSE; it is the consequence.

    The MECHANISM, which this entry had right in the general and left open:
    GNU's `zap_nuls` replaces every NUL in a buffer it has judged binary with
    the line separator BEFORE searching it. Its own reason is memory -- a long
    run of zeros holds no separator, so it would otherwise be part of one
    unbounded line, and grep.c's comment says exactly that. Termination is the
    side effect, and it is the observable one. Every NUL of a run becomes its
    own separator rather than the run becoming one, so `x\0\0y` holds an EMPTY
    line; and the NUL is GONE, so a pattern carrying one cannot match and `.`
    no longer spans it. Both were predicted from the rule and then measured.

    Where it shows is narrower than it sounds, which is why a sweep over the
    COUNTING modes is what found it: a printing run over a binary file emits
    no lines either way, GNU replacing them with the `binary file matches`
    notice. So the difference reaches `-c`/`-l`/`-L`/`-q`, the exit status,
    and whether that notice appears at all. `-a` turns the verdict off and the
    zapping with it; `-z` reaches no verdict in the first place, GNU guarding
    the whole test on a non-zero end-of-line byte, so nothing is zapped there.

    Zapping begins with the buffer the verdict TRIPS ON rather than the one
    after it, and a record that began in an earlier buffer keeps the bytes it
    already had -- GNU starts at `beg`, past the carried-over residue. That
    inherits the buffer-boundary caveat of the streaming entry above: which
    side of the flip a given byte falls on depends on where the boundaries
    land, and td-txt reproduces the RULE rather than GNU's arithmetic.

    The OTHER half of the same GNU mechanism is `skip_nuls`, and it is here
    too. Zapping turns a run of zeros into one empty record PER BYTE, which is
    why GNU pairs them (grep.c:1022): a read that is entirely zeros is
    DISCARDED rather than split, and `totalnl` is credited so `-n` still
    counts what was dropped. GNU enables it only where an empty line cannot be
    SELECTED (`skip_empty_lines`, grep.c:2983 -- the pattern is run against an
    empty line and the answer compared with `-v`), which is what makes it
    change no output; td-txt asks the same question once, as `!selects(b"")`.
    GNU turns it on at TWO points and so does this: under `-z` from the start,
    where NULs are separators and no verdict is involved, and again when the
    binary verdict trips. Measured on 64 MiB of NULs followed by `hello\n`,
    every row agreeing on the ANSWER:

      grep -c hello     GNU 0.03s  before zapping 0.12s  zapping 3.07s  now 0.03s
      grep -q hello     GNU 0.03s  before zapping 0.12s  zapping 2.96s  now 0.03s
      grep -z -c hello  GNU 0.04s  before zapping 2.61s  zapping 2.93s  now 0.03s
      grep -c '^$'      GNU 0.89s  before zapping exit 2 zapping 4.59s  now 4.91s

    The last two rows are the shape of it. `-z` was ALREADY paying that cost
    before zapping existed -- NULs are separators there whatever the verdict --
    so skipping closes a pre-existing gap as well as the one zapping opened.
    And `-c '^$'` is the case nobody may skip, because a pattern that selects
    an empty line is exactly the one the drop would change; GNU is slow there
    too. What remains in that row is a per-record cost of this reader rather
    than anything about NULs: GNU is several times faster per empty record
    wherever neither may skip (0.30s against 2.89s for `-v -c hello`).

    One decision in it is td-txt's rather than GNU's: GNU's further `SEEK_DATA`
    jump over a hole is NOT taken -- it needs a raw `lseek`, the same `unsafe`
    boundary as the sparse entry below -- so a hole is read and dropped rather
    than seeked past, which is the bulk of the win without the syscall.

    A SECOND decision was made and then RETRACTED, and it is written down
    because the retraction is the interesting part. GNU discards only the fresh
    read and keeps the carry, so an unterminated record JOINS across the gap to
    whatever follows -- becoming a record the unskipped reading never had. That
    looked like a GNU accident worth declining, and a sweep of 80 alignments of
    the run's start offset produced the joined record in none of them, which
    read as confirmation. The sweep was wrong: every cell in it used a
    newline separator and a three-byte carry, so the run never began on a fill
    boundary and no fill was ever entirely zeros. Under `-z`, with a carry that
    fills a whole buffer, GNU joins and it is plainly observable --
    `^foo.*bar$` selects the joined record, and `^bar$` does not select a
    record at all. So td-txt joins too. The lesson is about the sweep rather
    than the rule: a sweep that cannot REACH the mechanism reports absence with
    the same face as a sweep that ruled it out.

    WHEN skipping starts is the other half of that, and it is an ordering GNU
    never states. `skip_nuls` is set in the loop BODY, after `fillbuf` has
    already delivered the buffer the verdict trips on, so under a newline
    separator that buffer is always kept and zapped -- which is what ends a
    record open at the head of a run instead of joining it. Only later reads
    are dropped. Under `-z` there is no verdict to wait for and the first read
    can go. Enabling it one read early is not a subtle difference: the widened
    sweep put 133 cells of 4900 wrong, all of them the non-`-z` half, where
    td-txt joined records GNU splits.

    What is LEFT after all that is arithmetic rather than rule, and it is four
    cells of the same 4900. GNU GROWS its read buffer to hold the longest line,
    so once a buffer-sized residue is carried its reads are bigger and a run of
    fixed length may no longer fill one entirely -- no read is all zeros,
    nothing is dropped, no join. td-txt's buffer is fixed, so it joins. GNU's
    own answer there is not stable under the run's LENGTH: over
    `p*BUF + NUL*n + q*BUF + NUL*n + end`, `grep -c .` alternates 3, 2, 3, 2, 3
    as n doubles from 2*BUF to 32*BUF, while td-txt answers 2 throughout. An
    answer that flips with the size of a hole is a poor thing to reproduce, and
    reproducing it would mean reproducing GNU's growth heuristic rather than
    its rule -- the same trade the streaming entry already declines.

    What the join costs elsewhere is `-B` context, and the two halves differ.
    Where the dropped reads stood as empty records of their own, they sit
    between the match and whatever the window still holds, so the window is
    replayed with the last `before_cap` of them -- otherwise a record from
    BEFORE the run is offered as the match's neighbour, carrying the line
    number it had then. Where the gap was JOINED into an open record there are
    no such records, and replaying any would be context GNU does not print.
    Both are pinned, and so is the numbering: the dropped records occupy
    `lineno + 1 ..= lineno + dropped`, so the replayed tail starts one past the
    subtraction, which is an off-by-one a test caught rather than a reading.

  - `-b`/`--byte-offset` prints where a record STARTS, and under `-o` where each
    MATCH starts. It comes after the line number rather than before it, so
    `grep -bn` prints `2:6:beta`; the two are independent, and a context line
    carries its own offset with the `-` separator the rest of its prefix uses.
    The counting modes print no line to prefix and so ignore it. Offsets are raw
    byte positions in the INPUT: a CR is one of them, an unterminated last record
    still has one, and under `-z` they are the NUL-separated record starts. That
    holds for every record the reader did not have to JOIN across a dropped run;
    the exception is below, and it is GNU's too.

    Across a run of NULs the reader SKIPPED, td-txt deliberately prints
    something else. GNU credits the LINE count for a discarded read
    (`totalnl = add_count (totalnl, fillsize)`, grep.c:1024) and never credits
    the byte count for one, because `totalcc` only ever advances by what a
    buffer actually held (grep.c:1627). `-b` then prints
    `totalcc + (beg - bufbeg)` (grep.c:1196), so what comes out is the bytes GNU
    SCANNED rather than the position in the file: `run mod 98304`, one
    `INITIAL_BUFSIZE` (grep.c:886). Measured across ten run lengths, it tracks
    that modulus exactly -- 100000 gives 1696, 131072 gives 32768, 196608 gives
    0, and 1, 4 and 16 MiB all give 65536 because that whole family is congruent
    mod 96 KiB.

    So GNU's number is not meaningless, and it is not the option's: `-b` is
    documented as a byte offset into the input, and bytes-scanned is a different
    quantity that coincides with it only when nothing was skipped. What settles
    the divergence is that GNU CONTRADICTS ITSELF -- `-n` counts the discarded
    lines while `-b` declines to count the discarded bytes, both on the same
    record, so `grep -z -b -n` on a 1 MiB run prints `1048577:65536:hello`: one
    line, two fields, two accounts of where it is. td-txt prints
    `1048577:1048576:hello`, and what the test asserts is that the two fields
    AGREE, not that the offset takes any particular value. This is the trade the
    streaming entry above already makes: reproduce GNU's rule, decline its
    arithmetic where the arithmetic contradicts the rule's own neighbour.

    A record JOINED across a dropped run keeps the offset it BEGAN at rather than
    one past the gap, which is `take_skipped`'s two halves seen from the byte
    count instead of from `-B` context: a run with nothing open starts the next
    record past itself, and a run a carry spans belongs to the record already
    going. Reporting the tail would name a position the record does not occupy.

    That divergence reaches exactly as far as where a record STARTS and no
    further. A joined record is NON-CONTIGUOUS in the input, so a span inside
    one has no single position there: `-o` therefore indexes the ASSEMBLED
    record, and `bar` after a 192 KiB dropped run reports 98304 rather than the
    294912 it physically sits at. GNU prints 98304 too, so this is agreement
    and not a second divergence -- the offset arithmetic is shared, and only
    GNU's count of the bytes it discarded is declined.

    Implementing it also retires a substitution in td's own ladder: glibc's build
    wants `grep -ob U`, which the recipes replaced with an awk program because
    this option was refused. `grep -ob U` now matches GNU byte for byte, so that
    replacement can go -- in its own landing, since it touches recipes rather
    than this crate.

  - A SPARSE file is binary to GNU before any NUL has been read, and td-txt
    does not follow. This is the SECOND door to the verdict above: GNU asks
    the kernel for a hole (`lseek(fd, cur, SEEK_HOLE)`) on the first buffer of
    a regular file, and a hole before end-of-file means the file must contain
    NULs whether or not any have been seen. Measured on 270 KiB of ordinary
    text followed by a 64 MiB hole -- the hole past the first 96 KiB buffer,
    so only the seek can see it -- GNU prints nothing but the notice where
    td-txt prints 196605 bytes of matching lines before its own verdict trips
    in the buffer that reaches the hole. `-c` and `-l` agree anyway, both
    files matching either way.

    NOT closed, and the reason is a boundary rather than a difficulty: the
    question is `SEEK_HOLE`, which `std::io::Seek` cannot spell -- it offers
    Start/Current/End and nothing else -- so asking it needs a raw `lseek`,
    an `unsafe` surface td-txt does not have and UNSAFE.md would have to gain.
    The one safe proxy, `st_blocks * 512 < st_size`, is available and is NOT
    the same question: it reports holes on a filesystem that merely COMPRESSES,
    which would suppress the output of an ordinary text file. A wrong answer
    there is silent and costs the reader every match in the file, so the
    divergence is recorded rather than guessed at.

  - The `-v` short cut above under `-r`, which GNU takes before it expands
    operands and td-txt takes after: `grep -rcv -e '' /nosuchdir` is exit 1 and
    silent in GNU, exit 2 with `No such file or directory` here, and the same
    holds for the `-m 0` and empty-list clauses (`grep -rcm0 a /nosuchdir`,
    `grep -rc -f /dev/null /nosuchdir`). `Grep::settled` is consulted after
    `walk` has already reported. Pre-existing, and the part of this axis the
    landing that closed it did NOT close. `-R`'s cycle WARNING joins the same
    entry: `grep -Rlm0 a t` says nothing in GNU, which never traverses, and warns
    here, which traverses first and consults the short cut after.

  - A `^$` whose `$` is followed by MORE pattern, which grep's dfa answers as
    though that `$` were not there: `grep -c '^$|$'` selects a line `|` and
    `'^$|a$'` selects `|a`, where reading both anchors selects neither. It needs
    the `^` IMMEDIATELY before the `$` and the pattern to END in `$` -- `^$|a`,
    `^x$|$` and `.*^$|a$` all agree -- and `grep -o` prints nothing for them, so
    it is the dfa's alone. NOT the bare-`|` rule above, though that is how it was
    found: `grep -c '\(^$\)a$'` selects a line `a` with no stray anchor anywhere
    in it, and the binary built from this branch's parent answers every one of
    them identically. Ten patterns of a 170-pattern `^` sweep. Recorded, not
    modelled: it is a different axis (what an EMPTY line assertion does to the
    rest of a branch) and nothing here reads a second anchor that way.

  - WHETHER sed's dead mid-branch `$` is OBSERVABLE, which is not a property of
    the pattern at all. glibc's reading is the dead one and td-txt reads it that
    way throughout, but for some patterns the LITERAL reading shows through
    instead and the `$` IS replaced: `s@$|*@<&>@` marks the `$` in `a$`. Three
    readings of that boundary were tried and a further probe refuted each --
    "dead everywhere", which `$|*` refutes; "literal only where the `$` starts a
    branch", where `.$|*` is literal past one and `$|a` dead at one; and "an
    unconstrained prefix AND a tail that can match empty", where `\|x$|a` is
    literal though neither holds (an empty sibling branch is enough) and
    `[^a]$|*` is literal while `[^a]$|.` is dead on the same prefix. What the
    boundary tracks is whether GNU's SEARCH visits the position at all rather
    than what the pattern means, so modelling it would mean growing a fastmap in
    order to reproduce where GNU's goes wrong. A fourth reading fitted to the
    probes above is the mistake this file already records twice. 32 of the 667
    patterns swept here, and 8 of 2345 in a wider BRE/ERE fuzz whose grep half
    is clean; three xfail cases carry the goldens, one of them a case that
    passed before this landing.

  - A `[` that sed's reader CONSUMES as the byte it tests after a sub-expression's
    kind character. The consume-don't-peek rule above holds everywhere else; where
    the byte eaten is a `[`, no reading fits the answers. Measured exhaustively
    over the 55,980 bracket bodies of length 2-6 spellable in `[ ] : . = a`, the
    rule makes 16,967 shapes agree with GNU that did not, and 52 disagree that
    did; 8 are wrong either way. Every one of the 52 puts a `[` at the position
    the closer test eats, in any of the three kinds -- `[[..[]]`, `[[::[]]` and
    `[[==[]]` are `Unmatched [` in GNU and `unterminated` here -- so it is a
    family, not the collating-element corner an earlier draft of this entry
    claimed. Four xfail cases: those three, plus `[[...][.[.]]` for the reverse
    direction, where GNU is `unterminated` and td-txt compiles. Three readings
    were tried and each is contradicted by one of four probes: abandon the
    sub-expression on a consumed `[` (`[[..[a]]` refutes it), open a nested one
    (`[[...][.a.]]` refutes it), and treat `[` as ordinary content (`[[..[]]`
    refutes it). Recorded rather than guessed at, because a fourth reading fitted
    to two more probes would be the mistake this file already records once.

  - Nothing here now: the literal-brace family this entry described is closed,
    and the shape of getting there is the entry's remaining value.

    The brace against a `)` was closed by the eaten-paren rule; the OPERATOR
    after it looked unclosable, and a draft of that landing wrote a proof into
    this file saying so. The proof ran: refusing `({*)` requires the `*` to be
    DROPPED so that it eats the paren, and grep's own selections say it was not
    dropped, since `{*a` selects a bare `a` and `{{*a` repeats only the second
    brace -- so no one parse does both, and grep manages only by answering the
    refusal and the selection from different engines. Every measurement in it was
    right and the conclusion was wrong, because the first step is a false
    dichotomy: an eaten paren never needed a dropped operator. `({)` is refused
    with no operator in the pattern at all, by the rule three paragraphs up. The
    parse that does everything at once is the brace's own eat reaching PAST the
    operators it carries -- `({*)` going the way `({)` does -- while those
    operators still repeat it. A reviewer built it, put 55,986 exhaustive
    patterns over `( ) { } * a` through it at zero disagreements, and six xfails
    went with it.

    Two lessons, and the first is not "measure more". Every probe behind that
    proof was sound; what failed was reaching a NEGATIVE conclusion from them
    without asking what else the file already did. The rule that refuted it was
    forty lines above the one being reasoned about. The second is about how such
    a claim should be written: "cannot be closed" is a claim about all parses,
    and a gap entry has no way to check one. Prefer recording what was measured
    and what was tried.

    The SIZE of this family was a method note and stays one. The campaign fuzzer
    put 1708 patterns through it and found thirteen shapes; a reviewer's
    generator, crossing the same tokens to depth four, reached `({+)`, `({?)`,
    `(\b{*)` and more that the first grammar could not spell. Twice this file had
    recorded that a generator only covers the shapes its grammar produces, and
    that was the first time the correction came from someone else's grammar
    rather than from reading. Do not read any of these counts as the count of
    anything but what one grammar emitted.

    That lesson then repeated inside one landing, which is worth the extra line.
    The charge-once rule above was got wrong first, and FOUR generators run
    against a binary built from main -- 16,104 token patterns, 1,708 grammar
    patterns, 1,105 alternation patterns, 840 bracket patterns -- all reported
    zero regressions, because the witness needs an assertion, an operator, a
    brace and TWO closing parens at once and no grammar reached that far. A
    reviewer found `(\b*{))` by reading; a fifth generator, written afterwards to
    cross exactly those axes, found twenty more in the `(*{))` family that
    reading had not. Neither method subsumed the other. A sweep reporting zero is
    evidence about its own grammar and nothing else.

    The family also GREW when the eaten-paren rule landed, and that is worth a
    line because nothing about the shapes themselves changed. Pairing one of them
    with a group the rule now accepts -- `({*)(*))`, `({2}*)({*)` and about 28
    more -- used to agree with grep by accident, main refusing them because its
    rule refused `(*)` where grep refuses them for the brace. Removing the wrong
    refusal left the right one uncovered. Two cases carry it. A gap entry that
    lists shapes reads as though the shapes are the extent of it; what is
    uncovered is a RULE, and it reaches every pattern that contains one.

  - The group a grep ERE drop left unclosed, once a BACKREFERENCE names it. Grep
    answers backreference patterns from glibc rather than its own dfa -- the split
    recorded below -- and glibc keeps the eaten `)` INSIDE the group where td-txt
    closes the group before it. So `(*))\1` counts 2 lines of `)`/`))`/`)))`/`a`
    in grep and 5 here, and `({2}*)\1` selects nothing in grep and 7 here. The
    message differs on the same axis: a group that never closed is `Invalid back
    reference` to grep, because the group was still open when `\1` was read,
    against `Unmatched ( or \(` here -- `(a\b*)\1` and `({)\1`, where the STATUS
    agrees at 2 and main wrongly compiled both. Four xfail cases. Not reachable
    without a backreference: `-c` cannot tell the two boundaries apart, since
    `(a\b*))` is `a` then `)` either way.

  - WHAT FOLLOWS a sed BRE assertion-plus-operator, where the parse rule above is
    uniform and glibc's MATCHER is not. `s|x\b\?|<&>|` selects the `x?` in `x?y`
    and `s|x\b\?y|<&>|` -- the same pattern with a `y` appended -- selects nothing
    at all, from any input; so do `x\b\?[y]`, `x\b\?[qy]`, `x\b\?\w`, `x\b\?yz`
    and `x\b\?y$`, while `x\b\?.`, `x\b\?y*` and `x\b\?[^q]` match. `x\b*$` and
    `x\b*\'` fail too, so it is not a matter of the follower being mandatory or
    single-byte -- an earlier draft of this entry said both and a reviewer showed
    counterexamples in each direction. The follower decides and no property that
    survives probing has been found: the nearest is "the follower can also match
    the operator character" (`x\b*[*qy]` matches, `x\b*[qy]` does not), and
    `x\b*.y` on `x*zy` breaks that too. Two xfail cases; 78 of the 2400 patterns
    in the sed-BRE grid, the other three dialects being 0 of 2400 each.

  - A BACKREFERENCE anywhere in a grep pattern, which switches the whole of it to
    glibc's reading -- that is, to sed's. It reaches the mid-branch `$` above, so
    `grep -c '\(x\)\1$|*'` answers 0 where `grep -c 'x$|*'` selects the line, and
    so does `-o`, which runs glibc for the same pattern `-c` gives the dfa. td-txt
    keeps the dfa reading for grep throughout, that being the one `-c` pins, and
    a pattern LIST is where that costs most. Swept over duplicate-bearing lists
    and sixteen flag sets, every divergence left is a list GNU hands to glibc:
    under `-o`, or because some pattern in it carries a BACKREFERENCE, which
    switches the whole list and so is not confined to `-o` at all --
    `grep -c -e 'x$|' -e '\(x\)\1$|'` selects five lines of seven where td-txt
    selects one, and dropping the `\1` makes both agree. The counts depend on
    which alphabet is swept, so they are not quoted here; the shape does not,
    each being a list the naive literal reading this rule replaced happened to
    agree with glibc about. It reaches the brace rule above too:
    `(a)\1|^{}` selects one line of `aa`/`{}`/`{}a` in grep and three here,
    though `(a)\1|{}` -- the same alternation with the anchor dropped -- agrees
    at three, so it is the anchored brace glibc reads differently. One xfail with
    that control beside it. `grep '\(.\)\1*\b*'` selects exactly
    what `sed 's|\(.\)\1*\b*|[&]|'` marks, while the same pattern WITHOUT the
    `\1` selects every line, the repeat reading modelled above. The minimal
    witness is `grep '\(a\)\1\b*'` on `aa`, which GNU does not select and
    td-txt does; it holds in the ERE too. This is grep choosing between its own
    dfa.c and glibc per PATTERN, and the same split shows up per INVOCATION:
    `grep -o` uses glibc where `-c` and `-q` use the dfa, so GNU itself answers
    `-c '\b*a'` and `-o '\b*a'` differently on `xab` -- the corpus pins `-c`,
    and td-txt, having one engine, cannot satisfy both. The brace rule above
    lands on the dfa side too, so this split now covers that whole family --
    grep counts the lines of `^{}` and its own `-o` selects nothing in them.
    Seven cases carry the goldens, two of them a DROPPED INTERVAL, where the disagreement is with
    itself and plain: on `2a`/`{2}a`/`a`, GNU counts all three lines for `{2}a`
    and its `-o` finds one occurrence in the file. A reviewer read this as
    something the group rule had newly exposed, since `({2}*)a` compiles only
    since that rule landed; the no-paren control is why it is filed here
    instead -- `{2}a` answers identically on main, so what the rule changed is
    which patterns reach the split, not the split. The landing's own METHOD warning applies to its own model here: a
    corner probed through one frame confirms whatever that frame implies.

    This gap is where an earlier draft of this landing went wrong, and the shape
    of the mistake is worth keeping. Every probe of the corner used a follower of
    exactly that form (`x\B\?b`, `x\B\+*y`), so the quirk looked like the RULE:
    the model became "the assertion and its operator collapse into an atom that
    never matches, and a later zero-minimum operator brings the assertion back",
    which fitted forty probes and was still wrong. Dropping the follower is what
    showed it -- `x\b*` matches where `x\b*y` does not. A corner probed only
    through one syntactic frame will confirm whatever that frame implies.

  Goldens are C-locale. Under LC_ALL=C — the only locale td's image sets, and what
  every case here was captured under — GNU `grep -w '\.*'` selects `.a`; under a
  UTF-8 locale the same GNU binary selects nothing. Re-derive a `-w` golden without
  LC_ALL=C and it will look as though the corpus pins non-GNU behavior. It does
  not; it pins GNU in the locale td runs.

  Format: see the module header of ../src/lib.rs. `#### desc` opens a case,
  `## argv:` gives the command (argv[0] is the applet), `## stdin:`…`## END`
  and friends carry verbatim blocks, and `-json` spellings carry exact bytes
  where a block cannot (no trailing newline, a NUL).

  Provenance of the goldens: every case in grep-cli.test.txt and
  sed-cli.test.txt was RUN against the GNU program under LC_ALL=C, and its real
  stdout/status recorded here — GNU grep 3.11 and GNU sed 4.9, both from the
  authoring host's store. So these are upstream goldens too, just captured by
  running rather than copied from a file. To re-derive one by hand:

      printf '<the stdin block>' | grep <the argv, minus argv[0]>; echo $?

  divergence.test.txt is the exception, and says so in its own header: those
  goldens are td-txt's, pinning behavior it chooses differently on purpose (no
  shell-executing `sed e`, no PCRE `grep -P`, no `sed --debug`). Its cases are
  the record of those decisions.

  A case here carries an assertion the gate CANNOT check: that GNU answers
  differently. The goldens are td-txt's, so a case whose divergence has
  quietly evaporated -- or never existed, the fixture being too weak to show
  it -- passes forever while asserting nothing. The oracles are not available
  offline, so this cannot be a test; it is a rule for adding one. RUN the
  oracle against a new case and confirm it does NOT match, comparing status,
  stdout AND stderr: for every refusal here the whole divergence is the
  diagnostic, so a check that reads only status and stdout reports agreement.
  70 cases, of which 68 differ; the other TWO are counts GNU never finishes, so
  they are not comparable by construction and their divergence is the
  non-finishing itself. This number had gone STALE -- it read 36 while the file
  held 33, five commits having touched the file without re-running it, which is
  the failure the sentence at the end of this paragraph is written against. It
  was 32 until the `-f DIR` decision arrived from the gap list above with FOUR,
  each confirmed against GNU sed 4.9 as this paragraph requires. 32 was also
  the count when the `-NUM` decision landed, and that was
  a DIFFERENT 32: `sed -l N` was retired when the option was
  implemented, which is the one way a case leaves this file -- td-txt stopped
  diverging -- and the usage line after a missing option argument replaced it.
  Re-run it when adding one: the count in this sentence is part of what it
  asserts, and it was wrong once already between writing it and checking it.
  What the `-l` landing re-ran was the EIGHT sed cases, against GNU sed 4.9; the
  grep ones were not re-run, that host having no GNU grep any more (its `grep`
  is ugrep), which is the same reason a grep golden cannot be captured there
  today.


Overlay
-------
  expectations.txt   td-txt's known-gap manifest (xfail / skip). Not a case file.
                     GENERATED, not hand-edited, by ../examples/gen_expectations.rs.
                     See its header for the xfail/skip semantics. Regenerate on a
                     pin bump or when passes change:

                       cargo build --release --manifest-path td-txt/Cargo.toml
                       cargo run --release --manifest-path td-txt/Cargo.toml \
                         --example gen_expectations -- \
                         td-txt/target/release/td-txt td-txt/spec > td-txt/spec/expectations.txt

                     It is EMPTY today: td-txt passes every case in this corpus.
                     That is a statement about the corpus as much as about
                     td-txt — growing the corpus is how the next gap gets found.
                     The usage-derived block above is one such growth, and it
                     found four (see the landing commit): `sed -i` with no file
                     operand, `-i` following symlinks and sharing hard links,
                     `--version`/`--help`, and Rust's `(os error N)` suffix on
                     every io diagnostic.

                     The `rootcheck td-volume` block found NONE: td-txt already
                     answered all 17 exactly as GNU grep 3.11 did. Those cases
                     earn their place as the score for a predicate that MOVED
                     onto this binary, not as a bug report — an ERE standing in
                     for an awk field test is worth pinning either way.

                     The fuzz-derived blocks found THREE, all wrong answers
                     rather than refusals, all now fixed: `-w` tested only the
                     greedy span at each start; BRE `^^` treated the second `^`
                     as a second anchor; and the matcher explored every end only
                     for patterns containing an alternation, so a bounded repeat
                     that needed an earlier greedy one to give ground came back
                     short. Hand-written cases had not reached any of the three
                     in 783 tries; a random search reached all three in a few
                     thousand. That is the argument for repeating the campaign
                     rather than only adding cases by reading the option list.

                     A FOURTH came from review rather than from either: `-w`
                     over an empty pattern, on the empty-pattern MATCHING short
                     cut the fuzzer's grammar could not reach. Same rule, second implementation
                     of it — which is the more useful lesson than the bug.

                     A FIFTH and SIXTH came from reviewing the FIX for the third,
                     which is the more instructive pair. Exploring every end made
                     ordinary long-line patterns report `too complex`; and the
                     empty-pattern short circuit, while being rewritten, was seen
                     to discard the rest of the pattern list, so `grep -w -e ''
                     -e ab` missed what GNU selects. The long-line block and the
                     mixed-pattern block are their scores. Both were invisible to
                     a corpus of short lines and single patterns, and the first
                     was a REGRESSION the oracle alone could not name — GNU and
                     the previous td-txt agreed, and only the new one differed.

                     A fourth divergence was pinned instead of fixed, in
                     divergence.test.txt: an anchor inside a `\+`-repeated group,
                     where GNU reports the empty span and td-txt the longer one.
                     POSIX does not define an anchor there, so matching GNU would
                     mean special-casing its artifact.
